HT_DATA.load("detail", {"JTfhYyTuT44":{"wy":"HN treats it as the definitive, compassionate documentary on why people fall into conspiracy theories.","q":{"x":"probably the best documentary I have seen on conspiracy theories.","i":27096954,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":47631872,"author":"simonw","month":"2026-04","story":"Artemis II crew take 'spectacular' image of Earth","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Flat earthers mostly switched to QAnon","quote":"This was a fantastic YouTube video on flat earther beliefs from a few years ago","text":"This was a fantastic YouTube video on flat earther beliefs from a few years ago: [VIDEO] Spoiler - they mostly switched to QAnon instead."},{"id":37068007,"author":"tenacious_tuna","month":"2023-08","story":"Ask HN: Who's Seeking New Friends?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Deep dive into why people join communities like flat earth","quote":"There's a marvelous video [1] that Dan Olsen of Folding Ideas did as a deep dive","text":"> It's not the FE that's attractive, it's that they're people who put very little value on social conformity and thus can be honest in a way that \"fit ins\" can't. There's a marvelous video [1] that Dan Olsen of Folding Ideas did as a deep dive into the community of Flat Earth and how it manages to survive. The TL;DW (though it really is worth the watch) is that people both want and need community, and all communities have ways of signaling that you belong to them. Sometimes it's language, sometimes it's clothes, sometimes it's behaviors--the \"qualifications\" vary wildly by group, but in many cases from the outside the exhibited behaviors of a group (e.g., believing in flat earth) don't jive with the rest of the perceived individual (e.g., scientifically literate) until you factor in that the behaviors don't have to matter beyond ensuring membership to a group. A whole lot of things make a lot more sense to me when seen through that lens, including a lot of organized religion. People just want to belong, and often the \"price of entry\" is inconsequential vs. the payoff of belonging somewhere. Thanks for sharing your story. It helps me to understand the \"Why\" of some of these things, and helps me feel more connected to my fellow beings. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36398739,"author":"fallingfrog","month":"2023-06","story":"Dunning–Kruger effect and flat-earthers: An exploratory analysis","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explores flat earther mindset and shows curvature visually","quote":"","text":"There’s a great YouTube essay called “in search of a flat earth” that explores this phenomenon. It’s definitely worth a watch- it sort of lays bare the mindset of a group of people who want the world to be a simple, familiar place where there is certainty about everything. And, the host actually goes to a really long, calm lake and using a camera, shows the curvature of the earth in a visceral, visual way that is way more moving than I thought it would be. [VIDEO]"},{"id":34588593,"author":"jiggawatts","month":"2023-01","story":"Interesting","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Absurd beliefs as in-group shibboleth","quote":"One of the best analyses of this whole thing I've seen","text":"One of the best analyses of this whole thing I've seen is \"In Search Of A Flat Earth\" by Folding Ideas: [VIDEO] Essentially, professing absurd beliefs is a sort of shibboleth to demonstrate in-group membership. The absurdity is the point , because it differentiates them from outsiders. Normal people make true or factual statements all the time, that's not a good differentiator! The statements need to be false and/or absurd to function for their intended purpose. The Flat Earth mythos isn't even that outlandish compared to mainstream religions, and well within the same wheelhouse as the type of beliefs used to identify the in-group in cults. Scientology comes to mind, but there are many others. I highly recommend watching the entire video, because there is a lot more to the phenomena than I initially suspected."},{"id":30888698,"author":"MrAwesome","month":"2022-04","story":"Study conspiracy theories with compassion","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Compassionate documentary with lake experiment on earth's roundness","quote":"complete with a nifty and thorough visual experiment on the roundness of the Earth","text":"Folding Ideas on YouTube put out a documentary on YouTube which I think is a great example of this approach (complete with a nifty and thorough visual experiment on the roundness of the Earth, and a still-topical plot twist halfway through): [VIDEO]"},{"id":31137076,"author":"cyborgx7","month":"2022-04","story":"Umberto Eco on common features of fascism (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why flat earth belief persists as in-group test","quote":"A great documentary on the topic by Dan Olson aka Folding ideas","text":"Belief in Flat Earth is most definitely not a joke for a very large group of people (though it is absolutely a joke for a much larger group of people). It is part of a wider movement of biblical literalism and conspiracy theories describing an international anti-christian plot. The fact that it is easily disproved isn't really a problem for these kinds of conspiracy theories. It actually kind of acts as a purity test for members. Kind of like how scams still use the \"nigerian prince\" trope, because it ensures only the most promising marks fall for it. A great documentary on the topic by Dan Olson aka Folding ideas: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27096954,"author":"th0ma5","month":"2021-05","story":"There’s no such thing as a tree, phylogenetically","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on conspiracy theories","quote":"probably the best documentary I have seen on conspiracy theories.","text":"And if you want a chaser for any flat Earth material check out [VIDEO] \"In Search of a Flat Earth\" probably the best documentary I have seen on conspiracy theories."},{"id":26242688,"author":"jesseryoung","month":"2021-02","story":"Letters from House members to cable providers [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Difficult to debunk but easy to produce content","quote":"A fantastic video on the topic of difficult to debunk, but easy to produce content","text":"Perhaps a better example: Jewish people are telling you the earth is round so that way they can distract you from the fact they're kidnapping children and drinking their blood. A fantastic video on the topic of difficult to debunk, but easy to produce content [VIDEO] ."},{"id":24488480,"author":"listennexttime","month":"2020-09","story":"Remembering my father","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains how flat earth anti-intellectualism led to QAnon","quote":"this video is invaluable","text":"It's pretty easy to find other sources that cover the same survey that is shown in the graphic of the grandparent's comment. (Business Insider, for example: [LINK] ) Keep in mind this survey was in May and I'd be willing to bet the numbers have grown, as I've watched QAnon grow, multiple mainstream Republicans have embraced them (and won primaries ) and the Vice President was slated to attend a fundraiser put on by a QAnon group until days of pressure forced him to cancel just days ago. I've watched HN downplay gamergate. I've watched it downplay climate change (until recently, finally). I've watched it downplay anti-intellectualism at every turn, whether it be flat-eartherism, anti-vaxxers, or qanon, there's this purveying sense of \"it will all work out\", and that's just completely out-of-touch with reality. Here's a fun one for some background on QAnon, and how the rest of the world perceives it/us right now: [LINK] And for a complete understanding of how Flat Eartherism proud anti-illectualism has DIRECTLY LED US TO QANON, this video is invaluable: [VIDEO] It spells it out step-by-step and I'd love to hear someone try to respond to it. I haven't even expressed any of my own political feelings but I'm sick of watching people use ovenmits to talk about the fact that the anti-intellecutual, pro-conspiratorial actors are by and large self-identified as \"conservative\", or \"Republican\" or \"pro-Trump\". We can't keep acting like this isn't the case. All of these issues, politics, "},{"id":42184065,"author":"vundercind","month":"2024-11","story":"Scientific American's departing editor and the politicization of science","stance":"rec","why":"Lake experiments, community reaction, and YouTube algorithm promoting flat earth","quote":"The real story is how they react to contrary evidence delivered entirely on their terms","text":"I figured your link would be this one: [VIDEO] (Folding Ideas, \"In Search of a Flat Earth\") He takes a couple of their claims seriously about what one will see when attempting particular experiments involving a very large lake, attempts them, sees the results one would expect if the Earth were curved, and reports this to some flat earth community forum, refining the experiment as they suggest ways he may have screwed it up, and continuing to find curvature (obviously). The real story is how they react to contrary evidence delivered entirely on their terms, and where that community was heading four years ago (beware—I guess—that part also becomes necessarily \"political\"). [EDIT] I guess I buried the lede for this site's interest, which is that the video devotes a fair bit of time to how the Youtube \"algorithm\" took a little success for Flat Earth videos as a cue to aggressively promote them to people it identified as maybe liking them (those inclined to fall down that particular rabbit hole—which involves a lot more than just the specific belief that the earth is flat), but now flat earth is in decline, because that and other \"algorithms\" started sending the same folks to... Q-anon content instead. Incidentally, there was a somewhat-big documentary on Flat Earth some years ago that included some folks from a flat earth convention trying some experiments very similar to the ones depicted in this video (involving visibility of objects across a large lake), with predictable outco"},{"id":40058807,"author":"well_actulily","month":"2024-04","story":"Amazon is filled with garbage e-books, this is how they get made","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among highlights of Folding Ideas","quote":"- \"In Search of a Flat Earth\" (2020)","text":"Highlights include: - \"In Search of a Flat Earth\" (2020): [VIDEO] - \"Line Goes Up\" (2022): [LINK] - \"The Future is a Dead Mall\" (2023): [LINK]"},{"id":31977568,"author":"kadoban","month":"2022-07","story":"I watched hundreds of flat-Earth videos to learn how conspiracy theories spread","stance":"rec","why":"Deeper look at how conspiracy theories spread","quote":"If you're looking for more depth on this subject, I found this video very good","text":"Seems like a good, well thought out article. If you're looking for more depth on this subject, I found this video very good [VIDEO] (though I was already a fan of Folding Ideas)"},{"id":31276699,"author":"Nzen","month":"2022-05","story":"How Crossrail was affected by the curvature of the Earth (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Lake experiment showing curvature and ideology behind denial","quote":"outlining his experiment on Minnewanka Curve and the ideology driving people who deny his, and similar results","text":"Dan Olsen published a video [0], last year, outlining his experiment on Minnewanka Curve and the ideology driving people who deny his, and similar results, demonstrating the curvature of the earth. While there is no artificial structure, he observes that trees on the opposite side of the lake are obscured by the water surface. He also published raw footage of the multiple recordings he took as a separate 28 minute video. [0] [VIDEO] 90 minutes"},{"id":28855320,"author":"WillDaSilva","month":"2021-10","story":"Why “Trusting the Science” Is Complicated","stance":"rec","why":"Why flat earth thinking is harmful","quote":"For flat earthers in particular this video sheds some light on why their way of thinking is harmful","text":"The harm people cause by holding believing false beliefs is rather complicated at times, yet extremely important and impactful nonetheless. For flat earthers in particular this video sheds some light on why their way of thinking is harmful: [VIDEO] To be extremely reductive, allowing false beliefs into one's web of beliefs corrupts it. This typically gives rise to a multitude of other false beliefs. Flat earthers almost never only believe one obviously false conspiracy theory. They become epistemically susceptible, and worse still, they tend to spread these awful ways of thinking with great zeal. In the more general case, I recommend you learn about the ethics of belief. The Standford Encyclopedia of Philosophy page on the topic is a good starting point: [LINK] Maybe someone can come along and answer your question in a more succinct yet equally (or ideally more) convincing way. This request that you commit several hours to educating yourself is the best I can do at this time."},{"id":28698115,"author":"YossarianFrPrez","month":"2021-09","story":"YouTube is banning anti-vaccine activists and blocking all anti-vaccine content","stance":"rec","why":"How YouTube recommendations created modern flat earth movement","quote":"flat earth videos basically created the modern flat earth movement [1].","text":"So the second-order effects of YouTube banning content, as you point out, are interesting and hard to predict. The question is should fear of second order effects prevent YouTube from taking action towards a desired first order effect? For some context, prior to Youtube changing the algorithm, flat earth videos basically created the modern flat earth movement [1]. It's likely that this effect can go the other way, too. I like to think that people are less drawn to conspiracy theories etc. when they are socially well-connected. The trouble about the pandemic is that there is a negative feedback loop: we have these socially isolating restrictions because there is a deadly virus, and one reason the virus remains unchecked is, in part because a subset of socially isolated people are drawn to misinformation. [1] See the documentary In Search of A Flat Earth for more details: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25735349,"author":"pas","month":"2021-01","story":"Amazon, Apple and Google Cut Off Parler","stance":"rec","why":"Long insightful video on conspiracy beliefs and social order","quote":"Recommended long, but very insightful video","text":"It's not about real financial problems. There's that too, but people in that situation don't spend days protesting, they usually work a lot or are looking for, or do something that helps them escape their problems. QAnon, fascism, conservative ideology all have \"social order\" in common. QAnon of course drives it up to 11+, it's a full blown doomsday cult, with Trump being some kind of figurehead. And there's always something that threatens the status quo, their place in society. Immigrants, globalization, China, muslims, mexicans, antifa, gays, transgender frogs, liberal elites, uppity n....s. Recommended long, but very insightful video: [VIDEO] Naturally facts don't matter. You mentioned globalism, which indeed displaced many people from jobs, but this has always been ongoing. A hundred years ago this change was even more drastic. But back then this wasn't \"weaponized\". (Then the unions came, but then they too faded as technological changes dispersed big groups of homogeneous workers.) And now modern political parties try to play on these identities. (Mostly through candidates, as they locally present themselves, and naturally try to mobilize people.)"},{"id":24644447,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2020-09","story":"My Father Firmly Believes the Earth Is Flat, How to Help","stance":"rec","why":"Demonstrates earth curvature across a long lake","quote":"Dan Olson (\"Folding Ideas\") demonstrates this experiment in the video[1] I recommended in an earlier post[2].","text":"You don't need to send anything to 35k feet; you only need a camera with a decent telephoto lens and a long lake on clear day with calm winds. From one shore of the long lake, view objects at the far shore from normal standing height using the camera+lens. If you then carefully lower the camera to the ground, you can watch objects (boats! people! small buildings!) dissapear behind the curvature of the lake's surface. Dan Olson (\"Folding Ideas\") demonstrates this experiment in the video[1] I recommended in an earlier post[2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":37725034,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-10","story":"This is Financial Advice [video by Folding Ideas]","stance":"reference","why":"Flat earth waning as people move to QAnon","quote":"See also the 2020 video that starts out on Flat Earth, a belief that is waining","text":"> […] last year's video on NFTs. * [LINK] * [LINK] See also the 2020 video that starts out on Flat Earth, a belief that is waining… because people are going to Qanon: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":25146344,"author":"themacguffinman","month":"2020-11","story":"‘Extremely aggressive’ internet censorship spreads in the world’s democracies","stance":"reference","why":"Flat earthers moved on to QAnon","quote":"It's dead because flat earthers have moved on to QAnon [1].","text":"Nothing about QAnon is inherently viral, there's no real rhyme or reason to QAnon theories and they contradict each other half the time. QAnon is just the latest adaptation of solipsistic, uncritical, and delusional thinking that started long before the internet and will continue long after it dies. Ever wonder what happened to flat earth theory? That used to be the \"viral\" bad idea of the time, but if you look around now it's practically dead. It's dead because flat earthers have moved on to QAnon [1]. \"Wealthy democrats drink the blood of children\" is literally a random stupid idea that only spreads because these idiots all talk to each other. It could be anything; In a different decade they would instead be talking about the Freemasons, or a flat earth, or the Jewish agenda. The common theme is not the idea or the politics, it's the broken mentality that creates a worldview in which nothing is your fault, everything bad is caused by inhuman others, and you are one of the few smart enough to see what others cannot. It's foolish to think that censoring these people actually solves anything. Because you never addressed the broken psychology that attracts them to this garbage in the first place, they'll continue finding other outlets, your attempts to censor them only feeding their delusions further. Censorship might help you avoid them on the internet, but let's see if you can avoid them at the polls. [1] In Search Of A Flat Earth [VIDEO]"},{"id":24475148,"author":"jcranmer","month":"2020-09","story":"Archaeologists with drones discover pre-Columbian earthworks in Kansas","stance":"reference","why":"Video on flat earther argumentation style","quote":"I saw a video over the weekend [1] discussing Flat Earthers and some of the argumentation style they used","text":"When I first poked at this hypothesis, I came away feeling that the best characterization was that it's an area of academic dispute where both sides have credibility and neither side can be accurately considered fringe or mainstream, in contrast to your initial comment. Nothing you have said has shaken that belief, and I have no relevant scientific expertise to offer in support of one side or the other. I have my personal opinion on which side I feel has the better evidence and prefer on the balance of probabilities, but what that opinion is is ultimately unimportant. That said, there is one thing that mildly concerned me when I read your first comment and that your later comments have only reinforced my impressions. You seem to be approaching this topic from an evangelical perspective, and not a scientific one: that there is a core belief, this needs to be disseminated to as many people as possible, and that anything that challenges that belief needs to be swiftly eliminated. Your rhetoric--\"Neanderthal\", \"black eye for geology\"--does not help your argument. I only bring this up because I saw a video over the weekend [1] discussing Flat Earthers and some of the argumentation style they used, and on reflection, it was striking how similar your comments were, just on an acceptable opinion rather than a loony idea. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["Dp-mQ3HxgDE",2],["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["GC-0tCy4P1U",1],["O133ppiVnWY",1],["kPR8h4-qZdk",1],["SFv8Wm2HdNM",1],["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["SDtAh9IwG-I",1],["LIb3L4vKZ7U",1],["_Cp-BGQfpHQ",1],["ig_qpNfXHIU",1],["YQ_xWvX1n9g",1],["koTf7u0v41o",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["pgoetgxecw8",1],["GuigptwlVHo",1],["0rR9IaXH1M0",1],["_yMMTVVJI4c",1],["MiUHjLxm3V0",1],["99dKzubvpKE",1]]},"dEaecUuEqfc":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as the talk that finally made quantum mechanics click, especially for programmers.","q":{"x":"This is the video that finally made it click for me[1].","i":30577780,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30577780,"author":"raphlinus","month":"2022-03","story":"A wave function does not collapse upon detection?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains what is and isn't a measurement in detail","quote":"This is the video that finally made it click for me[1].","text":"This is the video that finally made it click for me[1]. Despite the somewhat click-baity title (\"What Popularizers of QM Don't Want You to Know\") it actually goes into quite a bit of detail of what is and isn't a measurement, in the sense of interactions in a quantum mechanical system that strongly resemble classical behavior (it can be said that the quantum mechanical world is a simulator that can run classical mechanics). [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21539468,"author":"oceanghost","month":"2019-11","story":"How Quantum Teleportation Works","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clarified quantum concepts for the commenter","quote":"It really clarified some things for me.","text":"If I may recommend a lecture given at Google Tech Talks-- \"The Quantum Conspiracy: What Popularizers of QM Don't Want You to Know\", It really clarified some things for me. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7602116,"author":"cromwellian","month":"2014-04","story":"Quantum Entanglement Drives the Arrow of Time","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear, approachable explanation of spooky quantum phenomena via entanglement","quote":"Even if you don't know any QM, just basic algebra and calculus, it's really approachable.","text":"This Google Talk [VIDEO] uses entanglement and quantum information theory in a clear and understandable way to explain 'spooky' quantum phenomena, like the quantum eraser, de-coherence, the aspect experiment, and the measurement problem. Even if you don't know any QM, just basic algebra and calculus, it's really approachable. I used to be a fan of the Many Worlds interpretation, but after seeing this, I'm now a big fan of the Quantum Information Theory explanation. Starting about 43 minutes in, he goes into the QM Information Theory explanation, but I'd recommend watching the entire prezo. Link to my original post on the subject: [LINK]"},{"id":5095996,"author":"lisper","month":"2013-01","story":"Quantum physics made relatively simple: Three lectures by Hans Bethe","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Video to really understand QM","quote":"if you want to really understand QM, there's this","text":"Before you invest three hours to watch these lectures you should know that the title is terribly misleading. These lectures are not about QM at all, they are about the history of QM, albeit told by someone who was actually part of it. But the coolness of hearing the history of QM firsthand is undermined by the fact that Bethe is clearly well past his prime, and he's not particularly good at telling stories. If you want a video that is true to this title I'd recommend this: [LINK] EDIT: And if you want to really understand QM, there's this: [VIDEO] :-)"},{"id":43706167,"author":"david927","month":"2025-04","story":"Are You Living in a Computer Simulation? (2003) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Ron Garret's talk making a case for living in a simulation","quote":"He makes a tremendous case that, yes, we're in a simulation","text":"Ages ago I talked about this with Ron Garret here on HN and he pointed me to his Google Talk: [VIDEO] He makes a tremendous case that, yes, we're in a simulation, and the more I learn the more that conviction grows."},{"id":41391782,"author":"newpavlov","month":"2024-08","story":"Computer scientists prove that heat destroys quantum entanglement","stance":"rec","why":"Good introduction to superdeterminism","quote":"This video can be a good introduction to this topic","text":"There is a very important and often forgotten caveat to the Bell's inequalities: it covers local hidden states. If we are to assume that measurement and particle creation apparatuses are entangled with each other (after all, they had plenty of opportunities since the big bang), then we have a global hidden state and the quantum measurment randomness becomes a simple artifact of removing global state from the picture. This interpretation of QM is usually called \"superdeterminism\" and, personally, I like it much more than the black vodoo measurement magic with collapsing wave functions or creation of whole new worlds on each tiny measurement. This video can be a good introduction to this topic: [VIDEO] (don't mind the clickbaity title, the video itself is good)"},{"id":31549470,"author":"newpavlov","month":"2022-05","story":"Chaos: The real problem with quantum mechanics","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk introducing superdeterminism","quote":"the talk itself is quite good even if you only vaguely remember QM.","text":"Personally, I am firmly in the \"superdeterminism\" camp. To me it's the most elegant interpretation of QM without any mystical measurements magically dependent on \"consciousness\" and whole universes created on each interaction. If you want to learn basics about it, I recommend watching this video: [VIDEO] Do not mind the (joking) clickbait title, the talk itself is quite good even if you only vaguely remember QM."},{"id":19405424,"author":"cromwellian","month":"2019-03","story":"Experimental rejection of observer-independence in the quantum world","stance":"rec","why":"Demystifies spooky quantum effects like the quantum eraser","quote":"Ron Garrett gave a presentation at Google years ago on Quantum Information Theory that kind of demystified a lot of spooky stuff","text":"Ron Garrett gave a presentation at Google years ago on Quantum Information Theory that kind of demystified a lot of spooky stuff (like quantum eraser), and I'm wondering how much of this is merely an artifact of the way we're interpreting the math. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16166375,"author":"lisper","month":"2018-01","story":"Blocking via an Unsolvable CAPTCHA","stance":"rec","why":"Video alternative to the paper","quote":"Or if you prefer, this video","text":"No, it's a little more subtle than that. If you really want to know, the answer is in this paper: [LINK] Or if you prefer, this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16254834,"author":"neel8986","month":"2018-01","story":"The Quantum Theory and Reality (1979) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Better explanation of observation as entanglement","quote":"You can look at a better explanation here","text":"One explanation[1] i heard is observation is a actually equivalent to entanglement. Now we know that two elements entangled will have to maintain the state corresponding to its entangled peer. Now if you observing (or taking measurement) the whole system gets entangled to the particle. you are observing and hence the wave function collapses. You can look at a better explanation here [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":13344353,"author":"phkahler","month":"2017-01","story":"What If Time Really Exists? (2008)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why measuring one entangled particle does not affect the other","quote":"But you don't need to listen to me, check this out","text":"Nothing changes at the time of \"wave function collapse\". When you measure one particle of an entangled pair, nothing actually happens to the other one. But you don't need to listen to me, check this out: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12656831,"author":"lisper","month":"2016-10","story":"Physicists Create World’s First Time Crystal","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how classical behavior emerges from the wave function","quote":"Or this video","text":"> Isn't the entire universe one quantum system? Yes. > If so, how can we talk about discrete systems. That is an excellent question! And the answer is that when you do the math, the result of taking a subset of the wave function (the mathematical operation is called a \"trace\") is something that looks like a classical system. If you want the details, see this paper: [LINK] Or this video [VIDEO] which covers the same material."},{"id":12378785,"author":"lisper","month":"2016-08","story":"There are no particles, there are only fields (2012)","stance":"rec","why":"Measurement as mutual entanglement of degrees of freedom","quote":"Or the movie version","text":"> (whatever that means) That (implied) question has an answer: measurement is the mutual entanglement of a number of degrees of freedom. See: [LINK] Or the movie version: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9450870,"author":"dools","month":"2015-04","story":"Is the Universe a Hologram?","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent lecture on the topic","quote":"Anyone interested in this can find an excellent lecture here","text":"Anyone interested in this can find an excellent lecture here [VIDEO] and of course by looking up leonard susskind's stuff on the black hole wars and universe as a hologram"},{"id":4719495,"author":"mtrimpe","month":"2012-10","story":"Quantum entanglement shows that reality can't be local","stance":"rec","why":"Understandable argument for programmers","quote":"This Google Talk from last year provides an argument and interpretation of this that's fairly understandable with a programmer's mindset","text":"This Google Talk from last year provides an argument and interpretation of this that's fairly understandable with a programmer's mindset: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3592558,"author":"replax","month":"2012-02","story":"A Schrödinger cat with eight lives: quantum entanglement of eight photons","stance":"rec","why":"Ron Garret's talk arguing against the Copenhagen interpretation","quote":"watch his google talk about it","text":"In fact, the Copenhagen interpretation has been proven wrong by fellow lisper Ron Garret. You can read his paper here: www.flownet.com/ron/QM.pdf and watch his google talk about it [VIDEO] . The problem is, that most people (not scientists, mind you) do not really care anyway, and the Copenhagen interpretation gives you a nice story to tell, which is fairly \"strange\", yet somewhat understandable. I hate the fact that even science magazines still 100% subscribe to the Copenhagen interpretation, just to attract a greater readership and to be able to use catchier headlines."},{"id":25307098,"author":"lisper","month":"2020-12","story":"Thirty Years of ‘Against Measurement’","stance":"reference","why":"Commenter's own take on QM interpretation","quote":"Or the movie version","text":"Just about any modern book on QM interpretation will tell you this, though not in so many words. (They won't necessarily endorse this point of view, but they will tell you about it.) Here is my take on it: [LINK] (Or the movie version: [VIDEO] ) (These are now a wee bit dated, but still defensible.) For something written by an actual physicist I recommend anything by Tim Maudlin (he's a Bohmian, but his pedagogy is without peer) or David Z. Alberts, \"Quantum Mechanics and Experience.\" UPDATE: There's also [LINK]"},{"id":18025466,"author":"lisper","month":"2018-09","story":"Quantum observers with knowledge of quantum mechanics break reality","stance":"reference","why":"Video with same content as a related argument","quote":"Same content in a video","text":"I'm working on it (definitely room for improvement there), but the original paper is long and it will probably take me the better part of the day to work through it (and I also have to get some work done). In the meantime, the same conclusion can be reached by a completely different argument: [LINK] Same content in a video: [VIDEO] UPDATE: Just found this: [LINK] By way of: [LINK]"},{"id":13127863,"author":"lisper","month":"2016-12","story":"It's Not Cold Fusion but It's Something","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of a quantum eraser from polarizing film","quote":"You can make a quantum eraser out of a few sheets of polarizing film.","text":"Quantum erasers are a lot less impressive than they sound. They don't really \"erase\" anything. You can make a quantum eraser out of a few sheets of polarizing film. See: [VIDEO] around the 15 minute mark for a demo."},{"id":10232762,"author":"themgt","month":"2015-09","story":"Interpretations of quantum theory: A map of madness","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Garret's zero-worlds interpretation","quote":"as Ron Garret coined, the \"zero-worlds interpretation\"[1]","text":"Mermin put it perhaps most succinctly, that reality is \"correlations without correlata\", or as Ron Garret coined, the \"zero-worlds interpretation\"[1] It also makes sense to me on a sociological level, that the way in which this interpretation seems to undermine the very foundation of western, scientific thought is the reason it has been so desperately avoided for so long, with many preferring to believe in an infinite forking (but somehow still \"real) multiverse, than the idea that reality itself is intersubjective. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NfnWJUyUJYU",1],["KbB0FjPg0mw",1],["OQQ-W_63UgQ",1],["Q1lL-hXO27Q",1],["5_KcQt0z-eE",1],["QEJpZjg8GuA",1],["1MGjFKs9bnU",1],["LJ4W1g-6JiY",1],["B5NULPSiOGw",1],["4YTfxresvS8",1],["kLiwvnr4L80",1],["Ll6-eGDpimU",1],["Tml94je2edk",1],["BDqvzFY72mg",1],["dBnniua6-oM",1],["CjVQJdIrDJ0",1],["KkpqA8yG9T4",1],["OsDy-4L6-tQ",1],["aircAruvnKk",1],["f-b4QOzMyfU",1]]},"a-BOSpxYJ9M":{"wy":"Offered in nearly every Agile or Scrum complaint thread as the authoritative take on what agile was meant to be versus what it became.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this video of a Dave Thomas talk about what agile was supposed to be vs what it has become.","i":17159169,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":17159169,"author":"jrs95","month":"2018-05","story":"Reality Driven Development: Fixing Project Management in Software","stance":"strong_rec","why":"What agile was supposed to be versus what it became.","quote":"I highly recommend this video of a Dave Thomas talk about what agile was supposed to be vs what it has become.","text":"I highly recommend this video of a Dave Thomas talk about what agile was supposed to be vs what it has become. Most of us are probably stuck working with some sort of Agile methodology regardless of whether or not we'd like to, but it can still be nudged in the direction that Dave talks about. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12639363,"author":"monksy","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Anti-hype, controversial, and correct take on agile.","quote":"I love his talks for a few reasons","text":"Agile Is Dead: By Dave Thomas [VIDEO] I love his talks for a few reasons: Often times... 1. He's anti-hype 2. He's contriversal 3. He's right."},{"id":20329567,"author":"rb808","month":"2019-07","story":"Dear Agile, I’m Tired of Pretending (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Agile is an adjective, not a noun.","quote":"Heres's a good one by Dave Thomas.","text":"I think most of the people who wrote the agile manifesto said \"Agile\" been warped into something they don't agree with. Its supposed to empower teams, not enforced from on high to increase pressure on teams. Heres's a good one by Dave Thomas. A quote: \"Agile is an adjective not a noun\". [VIDEO]"},{"id":20020039,"author":"lugg","month":"2019-05","story":"Scrum is fragile, not Agile","stance":"rec","why":"What agile truly is and why scrum mismatches it.","quote":"Speaks a lot bout what agile truly is and how scrum is a very large mismatch.","text":"Reminds me of this goto conference talk from one of the original Agile developers \"pragmatic\" Dave Thomas. Speaks a lot bout what agile truly is and how scrum is a very large mismatch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18643036,"author":"dvtrn","month":"2018-12","story":"JIRA is an antipattern","stance":"rec","why":"Thomas declares agile dead and offers a better definition.","quote":"offers up what I think is a better definition","text":"Dave Thomas himself declared Agile dead a few years ago, but offers up what I think is a better definition [VIDEO]"},{"id":17567419,"author":"jppope","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: What are the things keep in mind while giving/preparing for a tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Included in a list of fantastic technical speeches.","quote":"","text":"1) Do not attempt to live code. change your code via version control, build small videos, copy and paste... whatever you do... do not try and live code 2) Read \"The presentation secrets of Steve Jobs\" ... its not really about Steve Jobs so much as its about how to be a great presenter. 3) building or writing a talk is significantly less important than the delivery... which means that more of your time should be spent on how to deliver the speech (recording I find is the best way to practice) 4) Pick a single thing and do it well, do not attempt to put too much into a talk or tackle something massively complex. 5) Make sure that everyone is walking away with something they can actually use. This doesn't require it be a hard skill... \"Soft\" or philosophical utilities are just as good 6) Have Fun and be funny... Speakers have a tendency to want to show off the size of their intellect, and the level their competency- having a speech be entertaining is way way more important. 7) DO NOT READ FROM YOUR SLIDES. know your shit. slides are supplementary Extra Credit. Here's a collection of some fantastic technical speeches: * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"},{"id":17191357,"author":"FrankyHollywood","month":"2018-05","story":"Scrum disempowers developers","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk on problems with scrum and agile.","quote":"Dave has an interesting talk about it.","text":"Dave has an interesting talk about it. [VIDEO] My personal experience is any project where scrum actually works is a boring project, no interesting problem is predictable. Fortunately I see mostly frustrated scrum masters, not understanding why planning fails :)"},{"id":15004028,"author":"okreallywtf","month":"2017-08","story":"Split user stories ruthlessly and get value earlier","stance":"rec","why":"Thomas on what agile has become and why he dislikes it.","quote":"I think Dave Thomas[0] made some great points recently [1] about what Agile (as a noun) has become","text":"I think Dave Thomas[0] made some great points recently [1] about what Agile (as a noun) has become and I think it addresses some of your complaints. Note this isn't just a repeat of the agile manifesto, it is him discussing why he hates what Agile has come to mean to a lot of people and it sounds like his grump-level closely matches yours. The jist I get from it is that agile has become an Enantiodromia[2] and has in some ways simply replaced the problems it was there to fix. That doesn't mean it has to be that way, but thats what happens when people take something and create a religion around it. In my company we call our process \"agile\", but what that really means to us is to do the minimum amount of analysis we can to develop the features that the customer actually wants, and when they realize what they thought that they wanted was not really what they wanted, we can change it without wasting massive amounts of time. If we find that our process is causing problems for the devs, we tweak it. When we find we're not doing enough analysis in some situations, we try to improve those situations. No buzz words really, just what is best for us and what is best for the customers. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13015185,"author":"jeremiep","month":"2016-11","story":"It’s not the methods that are Agile, it’s the people","stance":"rec","why":"Dave Thomas talk on agile as an adjective, not a methodology.","quote":"He also made a talk about it","text":"Usually, the more a team advertise itself as Agile, the less agility it actually has. I myself have never seen Agile work well, not even close. I saw plenty of make-believe and head-in-the-sand and as mentioned in earlier comments, insane amounts of cargo culting. I really like the way Dave Thomas put it: [LINK] He also made a talk about it: [VIDEO] These days this is the only methodology I follow and it works wonders, anything more I feel is just noise. At the end of the day nothing replaces actual engineering and understanding what you're doing."},{"id":12637561,"author":"lumannnn","month":"2016-10","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed as a favorite tech talk by Dave Thomas.","quote":"","text":"by Dave Thomas (PragDave) \"LoneStarRuby 2015 - My Dog Taught Me to Code by Dave Thomas\" - [LINK] and \"GOTO 2015 • Agile is Dead • Pragmatic Dave Thomas\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":12061600,"author":"rimantas","month":"2016-07","story":"In a nutshell, why do a lot of developers dislike Agile?","stance":"rec","why":"Agile manifesto author explains what agile really means.","quote":"Listen to one of the authors of the manifesto itself","text":"Listen to one of the authors of the manifesto itself: [VIDEO] (BTW, he is very opposed to the use of Agile as a noun ;)."},{"id":47060346,"author":"rgoulter","month":"2026-02","story":"Semantic Diffusion (2006)","stance":"reference","why":"Thomas's point that agile is an adjective.","quote":"","text":"> Agile should still mean exactly the same thing as it did in the manifesto I recall Dave Thomas (one of the signers of the manifesto) made the point: He points out \"agile\" is an adjective, \"agile\" is not a noun. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41638680,"author":"andrei_says_","month":"2024-09","story":"Ask HN: What's the Alternative to Agile?","stance":"reference","why":"Thomas on the agile industrial complex twisting the manifesto.","quote":"has a talk where he expresses his disappointment with consultants twisting the manifesto into a cult-like industry.","text":"Dave Thomas, one of the creators of the agile manifesto, has a talk where he expresses his disappointment with consultants twisting the manifesto into a cult-like industry. He calls this the agile industrial complex and says that anyone using agile with capital A is not referring to the manifesto but the industry and are selling you that. Agile is Dead - [VIDEO]"},{"id":37127676,"author":"rr808","month":"2023-08","story":"Why Developers Hate Agile","stance":"reference","why":"Agile declared dead by a manifesto author after managers hijacked it.","quote":"Agile is dead\" (now that it was hijacked by managers) from Dave Thomas, one of the guys who wrote it.","text":"\"Agile is dead\" (now that it was hijacked by managers) from Dave Thomas, one of the guys who wrote it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37221834,"author":"lloydatkinson","month":"2023-08","story":"Why KPIs are destroying businesses","stance":"reference","why":"Cited on the capital-A Agile industry.","quote":"","text":"In those cases (the majority) the word Agile is used to describe it. Not actually agile, just whatever the hell Agile is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36435206,"author":"drawkbox","month":"2023-06","story":"Generative AI Could Add $4.4T a Year to the Global Economy, McKinsey Finds","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for consultancies ruining agility.","quote":"","text":"McKinsey has many controversies. [1] McKinsey and Jeff Skilling used Enron as a \"sandbox\". [2] McKinsey is somewhat a consult cult that ruined agility with \"Agile\" which goes against everything agile was supposed to be. [3] Real agile and agility is now crushed due to them turning it into a micromanagement always on critical path. [4] McKinsey margin cutting has killed off lots of research and development and innovation. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27464639,"author":"agentultra","month":"2021-06","story":"Developers can't fix bad management (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"History and context for agile.","quote":"","text":"[LINK] provides some good content on his Youtube channel [LINK] helps to understand managing from an engineering perspective To understand some of the history and context: [VIDEO] A bit dated but still useful book: [LINK] Best of all, for surviving at a difficult organization, invest in yourself and get a good therapist. I can't recommend this enough. Burnout is the worst but you can manage if you have solid, professional support to aid you."},{"id":26351020,"author":"u678u","month":"2021-03","story":"Ask HN: Do you think Agile/Scrum is beneficial for software delivery?","stance":"reference","why":"Agile hijacked by management; source for adjective-not-noun quote.","quote":"Agile is a Adjective not a Noun\".","text":"\"Agile is a Adjective not a Noun\". It was supposed to empower devs but has been hijacked by management to enforce standards. from Pragmatic Dave Thomas: Agile is Dead [VIDEO]"},{"id":24515620,"author":"afarrell","month":"2020-09","story":"How I learned to charge my customers","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for process that gives repeated visibility into risk.","quote":"","text":"> if you're not making an active effort to bill as much as humanly possible for the minimum amount of actual work, you are bad at business. Deep Neural Networks who try too hard to maximize or minimize things will take weird misaligned actions. [LINK] I won't go into details, but my experience from the past year of trying to minimize the chances of losing my home and work visa has provided ample evidence that this also applies to human mental health. > There is no way to know ahead of time what kind of contractor you are. Which is why any strategy that resembles \"fire and forget\" is a poor one for anything that hasn't been repeatedly tested before with mostly the same components. It's better to have a process where you repeatedly get visibility into a risk than to imagine that you can wall yourself off from it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16772608,"author":"pi-squared","month":"2018-04","story":"The Agile Fluency Model","stance":"reference","why":"Original idea of the manifesto; agile is an adjective.","quote":"Agile is an adjective;","text":"Agile is an adjective; The values of the manifesto for agile software development are lost, this is the original idea of The Manifesto: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["csyL9EC0S0c",1],["hyYCn_kAngI",1],["kb-m2fasdDY",1],["aXflBZXAucQ",1],["Zn7oWIhFffs",1],["FACK2knC08E",1],["AbgsfeGvg3E",1],["vtIzMaLkCaM",1],["7kVeCqQCxlk",1],["b-7dMVcVWgc",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["7tsXtk7psUc",1],["ecIWPzGEbFc",1]]},"KrksBdWcZgQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a clever, entertaining and easy-to-follow talk on finding hidden x86 instructions with the Sandsifter tool.","q":{"x":"I found it easy to follow and pretty entertaining.","i":18219350,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":27780469,"author":"brokenmachine","month":"2021-07","story":"Undocumented x86 instructions to control the CPU at the microarchitecture level [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Fuzzing opcodes to discover hidden CPU instructions.","quote":"I watched a super interesting Black Hat video on youtube","text":"I watched a super interesting Black Hat video on youtube that talked about discovering secret instructions on CPUs by iterating over each bit of opcodes until you get an illegal instruction, and thereby discovering if an opcode is valid or not. He set up a room full of PCs running his code and had hardware to auto-reset them when they crashed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26890879,"author":"gbrown_","month":"2021-04","story":"How many x86 instructions are there? (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Good watch on finding undocumented x86 instructions.","quote":"the video from BlackHat[2] is a good watch","text":"How about undocumented instructions? Sandsifter[1] is an interesting project and the video from BlackHat[2] is a good watch. There's also a previous discussion of it on HN[3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":26522958,"author":"BlackLotus89","month":"2021-03","story":"Undocumented x86 instructions in Intel CPUs that can modify microcode","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting talk on automatically finding undocumented instructions.","quote":"There was an interesting black hat talk about automatically finding undocumented instructions.","text":"There was an interesting black hat talk about automatically finding undocumented instructions. [0] Interesting comment on twitter to the instruction of the original post[1] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":25035126,"author":"tyingq","month":"2020-11","story":"XuanTie C906 based Allwinner RISC-V processor to power $12 Linux SBC's","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on reverse engineering an ISA implementation.","quote":"A really good talk on reverse engineering an ISA implementation","text":"\"I thought you were saying that the use of microcode would complicate verification of the hardware.\" I do think that microcode opens up the possibility of subtle injections to follow code/hardware paths you might not easily predict. A really good talk on reverse engineering an ISA implementation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19733933,"author":"jchw","month":"2019-04","story":"A Complete Formal Semantics of x86-64 User-Level Instruction Set Architecture","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on finding undocumented opcodes via Sandsifter.","quote":"There was a really good Blackhat talk a year or two ago","text":"There was a really good Blackhat talk a year or two ago in a sort of similar vein: they developed an approach to iterate and find undocumented opcodes. [VIDEO] The software they developed is called Sandsifter."},{"id":18219350,"author":"nnnate","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"rec","why":"Easy to follow and entertaining talk by Christopher Domas.","quote":"I found it easy to follow and pretty entertaining.","text":"Breaking the x86 Instruction Set by Christopher Domas. [VIDEO] I found it easy to follow and pretty entertaining."},{"id":16046342,"author":"anfractuosity","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"rec","why":"Extremely clever talk on undocumented x86 instructions.","quote":"I thought the 'Breaking the x86 Instruction Set' talk was extremely clever","text":"One of my favourite talks I watched was \"Low Cost Non-Invasive Biomedical Imaging - An Open Electrical Impedance Tomography Project\" [LINK] As it presented an interesting technique I'd never heard of before, along with an implementation. Also I thought the 'Breaking the x86 Instruction Set' talk was extremely clever - [VIDEO]"},{"id":16047624,"author":"DyslexicAtheist","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among favorite talks of 2017.","quote":"BlackHat 2017: Breaking the x86 Instruction Set","text":"my favorite were: - How the reputation economy is creating data-driven conformity [LINK] - DEF CON 25 (2017) - Weaponizing Machine Learning [LINK] - BlackHat 2017: Breaking the x86 Instruction Set [VIDEO]"},{"id":15253753,"author":"AceJohnny2","month":"2017-09","story":"IDA: What's new in 7.00","stance":"rec","why":"Shows limitations of all disassemblers including IDA's.","quote":"he demonstrates the limitations of all disassemblers out there, including IDA's","text":"> I trust its disassembler (especially for mainstream languages) more than almost any other disassembler Might I suggest Christopher Domas' Black Hat talk \"Breaking the x86 ISA\", along the way of which he demonstrates the limitations of all disassemblers out there, including IDA's :) Talk: [VIDEO] Slides: [LINK] Paper: [LINK]"},{"id":32398680,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2022-08","story":"Ask HN: Do you worry about CPU backdoors like IME?","stance":"reference","why":"Overview of tools for decoding undocumented instructions.","quote":"a talk with a brief overview of the tools","text":"What are the implications of such hardware on user security? Threat persistence, i.e. persistent rootkits, persistent remote monitoring if one has privs to tickle the CPU instructions that give access. It is just a matter of time before there are public tools for script kiddies to manage the OS within the CPU. Here [1] is an example of someone slowly making progress decoding all the undocumented instructions and here is a talk with a brief overview of the tools. [2] This is not specific to Intel's ME but it is the way people will eventually tame/exploit that beast in my opinion. There are more recent talks that get deeper into security rings in the CPU. This video [3] more related to your question but not specific to ME however can be used to access ME and much more. If this appears to difficult and time consuming just know that some folks out there have the documentation for the undocumented instructions. You may find some tools on github and other public repos for disabling ME. Use with care. Test on an identical model of system that is at the same firmware/BIOS and same model of CPU as those tools can brick your CPU. As @rocket_surgeron stated, one can buy CPU's with ME disabled but that will not disable the \"God Mode\" referenced in [3]. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [VIDEO] [3] - [LINK]"},{"id":26930715,"author":"skunkworker","month":"2021-04","story":"A Silly Anti-Disassembly Trick","stance":"reference","why":"Source for opcodes that disassemblers misinterpret.","quote":"","text":"I would be really interested to see how often malicious software utilized undocumented opcodes that disassemblers incorrectly interpret and thus lead a security researcher down a rabbit hole while the actual opcode does something different. Like the 66e9xxxxxxxx and 66e8xxxxxxxx opcodes in x86_64 [1] If my understanding is correct, Stuxnet incorporated bytecode for the PLCs in S7comm, a protocol that was not open at the time. Though this is different then including undocumented opcodes for the system being targeted directly. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25561822,"author":"dnautics","month":"2020-12","story":"Cosmopolitan Libc: build-once run-anywhere C library","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how bad the x86 encoding is.","quote":"really hit home to me how bad the encoding is","text":"xoreaxeaxeax's videos about how to systematically parse the asm space of x86 really hit home to me how bad the encoding is. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22526971,"author":"caleb-allen","month":"2020-03","story":"Undocumented CPU behavior: analyzing undocumented opcodes on Intel x86-64 (2018) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Researcher identified many undocumented instructions.","quote":"a security researcher was able to identify a significant number of undocumented instructions","text":"This was also linked elsewhere but a security researcher was able to identify a significant number of undocumented instructions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21409688,"author":"mmcloughlin","month":"2019-10","story":"Destroying x86_64 instruction decoders with differential fuzzing","stance":"reference","why":"Related project: Sandsifter x86 fuzzer.","quote":"Sandsifter x86 fuzzer.","text":"Great project and write-up. I'm reminded of a couple other projects. MC Hammer project for LLVM tests round-trip properties of the ARM assembler. [LINK] Sandsifter x86 fuzzer. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16066683,"author":"FLUX-YOU","month":"2018-01","story":"Reading privileged memory with a side-channel","stance":"reference","why":"Related to controlling CPU behavior via undocumented instructions.","quote":"","text":"I do also wonder if some speculative prediction / branching stuff can be controlled through undocumented CPU instructions: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16070040,"author":"ohaal","month":"2018-01","story":"A CPU bug with undocumented opcodes, possibly Intel (scroll to p. 155)","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk with relevant section and quote.","quote":"I think it's mostly interesting from an academic perspective that we have a tool that is able to find these kinds of things now.","text":"Related talk (relevant part starts at 38:43) [VIDEO] Quote from the talk: I don't want to make it sound like the sky is falling. This was found on one very esoteric processor that is not used in widespread production. I think it's mostly interesting from an academic perspective that we have a tool that is able to find these kinds of things now."},{"id":15753019,"author":"zilitor","month":"2017-11","story":"I'm Testifying to Congress about Data Breaches – What Should I Say?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited regarding Intel hidden instructions.","quote":"","text":"Data Breach Transparency - At a minimum, their should be a penalty that grows from the time the breach was learned to when they disclose it publicly. - There should also be penalities for not being transparent about what exact data was leaked for what users. Social Security Number - SSN is similar to a password- you want to keep it hidden, and if it leaked, you should change it. However, we can't change it. Perhaps it should be considered more as a password? User Data Rights - People should know what personal data companies have on them. A good example of this is Equifax storing peoples home addresses- this could be disclosed. On the other hand, a it is probably fine to exclude other types of data, such as an advertiser storing your zip code- people probably don't care as much. - Should people have a right to have certain kinds of data (e.g. SSN) removed from websites? Adoption from USA Nutrition Label - Is it a good idea to mandate companies disclose the security they use? For example, at one time reddit had their passwords stored as plaintext and they got hacked. Disclosing basic security hygiene (e.g. password storage) somewhere standardized in the website would make it much less outrageous. Technology Improvement - Certain technologies enable hackers more than others. SQL seems to enable a lot of hacking. Should we discourage it? - Get rid of Intel ME technologies - [LINK] - Get rid of Intel hidden instructions - [VIDEO] - Get rid of Simon and Speck - [LINK] - What is \"be"}],"r":[["-zRN7XLCRhc",1],["IQZ_bQbxSXk",1],["6chLw2aodYQ",1],["79fvDDPaIoY",1],["aP0Y1uAA-2Y",1],["nIKhUizkXxA",1],["JXpoALotxf0",1],["AcQifPHcMLE",1],["F8JCh0owT4w",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["Gs790JOeN3Y",1],["fLA_d9q6ySs",1],["jvPPXbo87ds",1],["fhOHn9TClXY",1],["dP1xVpMPn8M",1],["ZRkm6-bBk4U",1],["8X7Ng75e5gQ",1],["15RTC22Z2xI",1],["RIctzAQOe44",1],["DnPmg0R1M04",1]]},"oeYBdghaIjc":{"wy":"Frequently recommended as the best free university course on database internals, praised for depth and Pavlo's presentation.","q":{"x":"Highly recommended both for the depth and how Andy Pavlo presents the topic.","i":21962332,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":35747696,"author":"runlaszlorun","month":"2023-04","story":"The Part of PostgreSQL We Hate the Most (Multi-Version Concurrency Control)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Intro class diving into database internals, with advanced courses and guest lectures","quote":"Highly recommend.","text":"[VIDEO] There are multiple years available for his first DB class but that’s the one I watched. I almost called it his ‘basic’ class but there’s literally only like one or two classes on SQL before he dives into all the various layers of the internals. There’s also a few of his advanced courses. And then you’ll see guest lectures from industry on about every one of the new DB platforms you can think of. They’re all under “CMU Database Group” on Youtube. Highly recommend."},{"id":30601667,"author":"maxmcd","month":"2022-03","story":"How does database indexing work? (2008)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"CMU database lecture series by Andy Pavlo","quote":"Obligatory recommendation for the CMU Databases Systems lecture series by Andy Pavlo.","text":"Obligatory recommendation for the CMU Databases Systems lecture series by Andy Pavlo. [VIDEO] Here's the lecture on tree indexes: [LINK]"},{"id":26813274,"author":"alistairw","month":"2021-04","story":"Foundations of Databases (1995)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Helped database choices and inspired building own database","quote":"it's significantly helped me in using and making choices about databases.","text":"Agreed. I finished watching them at the start of this year and it's significantly helped me in using and making choices about databases. I've also since started implementing my own database for learning off the back of them. Couldn't be more grateful for those courses being public. link if anyone is interested: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21962332,"author":"ptr","month":"2020-01","story":"Ask HN: What Technologies to Learn in 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"CMU intro to database systems lectures with depth and great presentation","quote":"Highly recommended both for the depth and how Andy Pavlo presents the topic.","text":"I'd start with CMU's \"Intro to Database Systems\", their lectures are on youtube. Highly recommended both for the depth and how Andy Pavlo presents the topic. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37248619,"author":"avinassh","month":"2023-08","story":"Which books, courses, projects have improved your programming skills immensely?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed among resources that improved programming skills","quote":"Intro to Database Systems by Andy Pavlo -","text":"Intro to Database Systems by Andy Pavlo - [VIDEO] MIT 6.824 Distributed Systems by Robert Morris - [LINK] Designing Data-Intensive Applications (DDIA) - [LINK] Crafting Interpreters - [LINK]"},{"id":36083018,"author":"joelrwilliams1","month":"2023-05","story":"Ask HN: What are some good resources for learning about low level disk/file IO?","stance":"rec","why":"CMU course going deep into database technologies","quote":"this guy gets really deep into the technologies behind a database.","text":"I stumbled across this Carnegie Mellon University database course a year or two ago and this guy gets really deep into the technologies behind a database. Here's the list of YouTube videos of lectures that the professor has posted online: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35500682,"author":"avinassh","month":"2023-04","story":"Ask HN: What's the best textbook or video lectures on a topic you've come seen?","stance":"rec","why":"Breaks down complex topics approachably for newcomers","quote":"these break down really complex topics and make the concepts approachable.","text":"Intro to Database Systems by Andy Pavlo - [VIDEO] MIT 6.824 Distributed Systems by Robert Morris - [LINK] Designing Data-Intensive Applications (DDIA) - [LINK] Crafting Interpreters - [LINK] > What made it so special these break down really complex topics and make the concepts approachable. As a noob, I learned a lot from these."}],"r":[["UEAMfLPZZhE",1],["cQP8WApzIQQ",1],["fU9hR3kiOK0",1],["MFRWDuduuSw",1]]},"3Ea3pkTCYx4":{"wy":"Cited constantly in threads on microservices, composability and the Unix philosophy as a compact explanation of why small interoperable pieces win.","q":{"x":"A guy on YT [1] simulates in 7 minutes how the two biggest operating systems were built in the sixties","i":27491650,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27491650,"author":"mettamage","month":"2021-06","story":"Glue: The Dark Matter of Software","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Seven-minute simulation of Multics versus Unix development, mind-blowing for commenter.","quote":"A guy on YT [1] simulates in 7 minutes how the two biggest operating systems were built in the sixties","text":"I wrote a non-programmer summary to some of my friends, because my mind is blown. Here it is (warning: it's very informal). This is some next level 80/20 stuff, I'd argue it's even 99/1 (consultant speech for \"being efficient\" by seeing if you can exploit Pareto's principle [0]). A guy on YT [1] simulates in 7 minutes how the two biggest operating systems were built in the sixties (Multics by IBM and Unix by 2 guys in a garage). Nowadays we only have descendants of Unix, this simulation shows IMO two things: 1. How a chain of command screws up productivity (I doubt that anyone at IBM had the power/autonomy to change complete structure of the product. This was needed, but such change management is insane). 2. How the pipe command in Unix changed the mathematical nature of developing an operating system (in fact, it's even better than 99/1, when you think about it). In normal human language, the difference is: IBM programmed modular blocks. Unix programmed modular blocks. IBM then programmed how those modular blocks should interact. Unix created a pipe command and allowed computer users themselves to implement how these modular blocks should interact. At the time, computer users were all savvy enough like that. Result: when IBM created a feature, it needed to integrate it with all the other features that came before. When those 2 guys in a garage (Unix) created a feature, all they needed to do was create that feature. So mathematically you could say (where each term is the amou"},{"id":27494671,"author":"deckard1","month":"2021-06","story":"Disasters I've seen in a microservices world","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows Multics versus Unix and why microservices struggle.","quote":"a youtube video on Multics vs Unix[2] which really outlines why microservices were always doomed","text":"I was going to comment on this very thing in the other thread about software glue[1]. In that article there is a youtube video on Multics vs Unix[2] which really outlines why microservices were always doomed. Someone should coin a new law for how programmers have to rediscover Brooks's law every 5-10 years. The issue with microservices, as it has always been, is that you need an enormous company to brute force the communication pathways and maintenance overhead for it to all work. And by work, I don't mean function efficiently (as the Multics vs Unix video shows). I mean just function . Just work at all. The Multics team had all the devs and the Unix team was two guys doing laps around the Multics team. Because they had the mathematics on their side. Remember the bad old days of memory thrashing? That's what happens to teams that do not have enough bandwidth to properly maintain the dozens of services they are responsible for. Your organization gets frozen. This is what we all get for taking advice from the Googles and Facebooks of the world. Google has like a billion lines of code in a monorepo. They do not do things remotely like 99% of the businesses out there. They are sitting on huge piles of money that lets them be incredibly inefficient for decades . [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37466297,"author":"kgr","month":"2023-09","story":"How could the early Unix OS comprise so few lines of code?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains and simulates how pipes enabled reuse and less code.","quote":"This is explained and simulated in this video","text":"Unlike previous OSes, UNIX provided the pipe facility to allow you to combine the functionality of many small programs. Ex. ls | grep ... | sort ... | more Before that every command-line program had to support the full suite of additional functionality like search, filtering, paging, etc. This UNIX/Pipe approach allowed for greater reuse and much less code. This is explained and simulated in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37218342,"author":"kgr","month":"2023-08","story":"Unix is both a technology and an idea","stance":"rec","why":"Short video analyzing UNIX architecture complexity versus earlier OSes.","quote":"It's demonstrated in this short video","text":"If we analyze the computational complexity of software architectures the way we do regular algorithms, we see that UNIX was much more efficient than previous OSes/approaches. It's demonstrated in this short video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33552972,"author":"foooobaba","month":"2022-11","story":"Following the Unix philosophy without getting left-pad (2021)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains writing N+M composable programs rather than N*M.","quote":"This video explains it well","text":"Programs/functions like left-pad do not follow the unix philosophy (which the author did say). The idea of the unix philosophy is to write less programs. Not write a ton of tiny programs, but a small number of composable programs. So instead of left-pad, right-pad, middle-pad, and anything else to get specific need you can use sprintf which can format a string in general, one program that can do implement the features of all of those. It is about “coding the permitter, not the area”, so if you have NxM features to build, don’t write N*M individual programs/functions, instead write N+M programs/functions that can be composed together to get all NxM features. This video explains it well: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23562670,"author":"kgr","month":"2020-06","story":"Apollo – Funding for Moonshots","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why UNIX was a moonshot developed by few people.","quote":"This video explains why UNIX was a moonshot, and why it could be developed by a small number of people because of it","text":"This video explains why UNIX was a moonshot, and why it could be developed by a small number of people because of it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18969258,"author":"kgr","month":"2019-01","story":"For the Love of Pipes","stance":"rec","why":"Author's simulation showing why the pipe is so powerful.","quote":"I made a simulation and video which shows why the pipe is so powerful","text":"I made a simulation and video which shows why the pipe is so powerful: [VIDEO] I showed it to Doug McIlroy, and while he thought there was more to the story, he didn't disagree with it."},{"id":11868981,"author":"kgr","month":"2016-06","story":"The Strange Birth and Long Life of Unix (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Shows mathematically why Unix beat Multics in efficiency.","quote":"This video shows how Unix was mathematically more efficient than Multics","text":"This video shows how Unix was mathematically more efficient than Multics, which goes a very long way at explaining its success and longevity: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34347309,"author":"foooobaba","month":"2023-01","story":"Architecture diagrams should be code","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sort of related but an interesting idea is take it one step further with the Feature Oriented Active Modeler (FOAM) [1,2] paradigm and use code to model your whole system, which generates diagrams model, and runnable code in whatever language needed. The project is still young and it may not be practical today with the available tooling but it seems like a cool idea and project. It is influenced by the unix principle of “coding the perimeter not the area” which is essentially factoring your dev tasks into building NxM capabilities, but instead of building NxM things individually build N+M tools that can be composed into NxM capabilities [2]. So with FOAM the idea is if we want to maintain a model of our software, and build it as well, what if we can use one composable tool to generate both, rather than model everything and code it separately. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34456542,"author":"kgr","month":"2023-01","story":"The Myth of the Myth of the 10x Programmer (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Simulation showing best programmers change the exponent, not multiplier.","quote":"I explain this a bit by means of a simulation in this video","text":"I would say that the best programmers don't change the multiplier, but the exponent, so that the factor by which they're better than the average increases with the size of the problem. This is why you might not see as much of a big difference with a test lasting a few hours, as you would with one lasting weeks, months or years. I explain this a bit by means of a simulation in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31289256,"author":"deckard1","month":"2022-05","story":"GraphQL Is a Trap?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Google understanding microservices coordination problems.","quote":"And you can see they understand the whole problem with microservices.","text":"> The whole thing was invented by a \"software consulting\" firm I don't know the whole origin story. It definitely does feel like something Martin Fowler would come up with. But I blame Google for really making it a trend: [VIDEO] And you can see they understand the whole problem with microservices. It's the same thing The Mythical Man-Month was trying to tell everyone decades ago[1] > When n people have to communicate among themselves, as n increases, their output decreases Microservices exasperates this. It is the Multics model. Each microservice implements its own, often wildly different, API. Which every bit of code that needs to use that microservice has to go and implement. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":27570917,"author":"ItsMonkk","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: Do software engineers have leverage?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"No. pg has a good article on doing things that don't scale[0] and showed how the AirBnB guys went and setup the first listings manually, but it's actually far more general than just kicking off the network effect. Moats are things that don't scale. Open Source software scales perfectly. Billion dollar companies are billion dollar companies because they can use Open Source software wrapped around some Moat-able structure. The more Open Source Software that exists, the bigger the walled garden. When Google spends a billion dollars sending cars all around the world taking pictures, they are doing so in a way that the Open Source world can't centrally compete against, as they don't have the billions to get that done. This means that all Open Source mapping software will be missing a crucial feature that users rely on, and will therefore go unused. But meanwhile the libraries that those Open Source Software use, Google can freely use within their walled garden. On the other hand, Microsoft paid a great deal of money to researchers with Encarta and had this same exact structure and Wikipedia came in and disrupted them. It takes more time for the decentralized approach to come online, but as long as it's possible and the data is kept high quality Google Maps will slowly lose market share to OSM. Open Source software is generally created as libraries, whereas most companies build frameworks. Frameworks are easier to setup and give control to the makers, but they do not scale. Why is "},{"id":27579657,"author":"ItsMonkk","month":"2021-06","story":"The “API Mandate” memo at Amazon","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I posted a comment on libraries vs frameworks yesterday[0], here's the relevant section. > Frameworks are easier to setup initially, but they do not scale. Why is it that Microsoft Windows has 13 different dialog generations? Because each is a framework on top of a framework on top of a framework. It's amazing that they can even get that done. > On the other side, OSS is generally built on libraries. When the 2 UNIX devs were in a basement building UNIX and were able to out-compete Multics[1], they did it because they were building libraries that could talk with each-other using pipes around the boundary. Applications that communicate based on input-output with no internal state behave just like pure functions do. Pure functions compose. When Linus built git in 10 days, he was able to do this because the core idea of git isn't actually that much work. The library is built out of composable blocks that neatly come together. Microsoft's TFS Source Control is a framework that acts on your behalf and therefore the bigger the project gets, you need n^2 people to work on it. Amazon's API mandate is the same exact unification as UNIX's pipe effect was to Operating Systems. Amazon's internal teams are building libraries whereas all other companies started at the same time were building frameworks. Jeff was brilliant to see this at the time, and I expect that this memo will be seen as just as pivotal as the Toyota Production System, and it might already have that prestige to some. Unf"},{"id":27618695,"author":"ItsMonkk","month":"2021-06","story":"Opinion-Driven Design","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"This is my take as well. If you are building a framework and its sub-components are frameworks that act on their own behalves, you better build exactly what you need in an opinionated style. But if you are building a framework and its sub-components are libraries, those libraries can be as general as possible, and your framework can then call those libraries in just the way that you need to. The library strategy will always out-compete in the end if you can handle it properly. If you try to do it half-way like these developers did, you will be ruined just like they found. Reposting from a similar comment yesterday - > Frameworks are easier to setup initially, but they do not scale. Why is it that Microsoft Windows has 13 different dialog generations? Because each is a framework on top of a framework on top of a framework. It's amazing that they can even get that done. > When the 2 UNIX devs were in a basement building UNIX and were able to out-compete Multics[0], they did it because they were building libraries that could talk with each-other using pipes around the boundary. Applications that communicate based on input-output with no internal state behave just like pure functions do. Pure functions compose. When Linus built git in 10 days, he was able to do this because the core idea of git isn't actually that much work. The framework is built out of composable blocks that neatly come together as libraries. Microsoft's TFS Source Control is a framework that acts on your behal"}],"r":[["8aGhZQkoFbQ",1],["ydqReeTV_vk",1],["kBdfcR-8hEY",1],["D6EPuUdIC1E",1],["P4VBqTViEx4",1],["XGbPHp6QfkQ",1],["h7apO7q16V0",1]]},"Gv2I7qTux7g":{"wy":"HN treats it as the standard introduction to Zig by its creator, with some calling it among the best talks they have seen.","q":{"x":"Andrew Kelley's intro[0] is probably the best talk I've ever seen","i":36577748,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":42498895,"author":"pixelpoet","month":"2024-12","story":"C++ is an absolute blast","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Introduction to Zig","quote":"his talk introducing Zig[0] is in my top 10 of all time technical presentations","text":"Sorry, one more thing to add to this: Andrew Kelley is obviously a genius, and his talk introducing Zig[0] is in my top 10 of all time technical presentations, for many reasons. But I really do wish someone close to him with a passion for how coding is in many ways applied mathematics, would ask him to please have broader algebraic support for basic operations like +, -, * and maybe divide, with their basic dataflow characteristics. Optimal speed for complex numbers vs std::complex out of the box would be attractive. I understand his point about not wanting to allow every random C++ feature, but in these cases, it isn't a C++ feature, it's language-level basic algebra. In C++ land, ISPC[1] is often what you use when you want top speed rendering perf on SIMD CPUs, e.g. Moonray project[2] Please, just go ahead and define a nice clean API for vectors and scalars like OpenCL provides on its beautiful reference cards: [LINK] [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] Final edit sorry: in the end I love C++ and have been learning Rust mainly out of curiosity. Avoiding C++ quirks one can have few problems and a great time."},{"id":38273960,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2023-11","story":"A decade of developing a programming language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Inspiring talk on Zig's ideas","quote":"The creator of Zig certainly made an impression on me with this talk","text":"I can only hope my own language will become \"incredibly small\" like that one day. :) Even if a language doesn't take off, its ideas can make a difference and influence future designs. The creator of Zig certainly made an impression on me with this talk: [VIDEO] Even if Zig hadn't been successful, the ideas it represents would've enriched the world. Gives me hope I'll be able to make something out of my own ideas too one day."},{"id":36577748,"author":"pixelpoet","month":"2023-07","story":"Problems of C, and how Zig addresses them","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Intro to Zig by its creator","quote":"Andrew Kelley's intro[0] is probably the best talk I've ever seen","text":"Andrew Kelley's intro[0] is probably the best talk I've ever seen, and his continuing leadership seems really great to me and worth supporting. Besides my reservations for its use in graphics programming (see my other comment) it looks very attractive in many practical ways (build system!! fuck CMake!) I could go from how I currently write my hobby code, sometimes a prototype for future commercial code. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35571758,"author":"fileeditview","month":"2023-04","story":"Zig Build System","stance":"rec","why":"C versus Zig examples and improvements","quote":"I would recommend this talk by the creator of Zig","text":"I would recommend this talk by the creator of Zig: [VIDEO] He gives some examples of C vs Zig and what they tried to improve."},{"id":29631677,"author":"von_lohengramm","month":"2021-12","story":"Zig 0.9.0","stance":"rec","why":"Convincing case for Zig","quote":"The Road to Zig 1.0[1] are pretty convincing","text":"In addition to the other recommendations, the Zig Zen[0] and The Road to Zig 1.0[1] are pretty convincing. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21740413,"author":"AndyKelley","month":"2019-12","story":"Game Boy Advance “Hello World” Using Zig","stance":"rec","why":"Newer introduction to Zig by its creator","quote":"Here is a newer talk (April 2019) which is also by me and also an introduction to Zig","text":"Here is a newer talk (April 2019) which is also by me and also an introduction to Zig: [VIDEO] A lot has changed since then, and a lot has changed since that Localhost talk. To catch up: * (the older talk happened here) * 0.3.0 release notes - [LINK] * 0.4.0 release notes - [LINK] * (the newer talk happened here) * 0.5.0 release notes - [LINK]"},{"id":46130770,"author":"troad","month":"2025-12","story":"Anthropic acquires Bun","stance":"reference","why":"Zig creator framing Zig as a C replacement","quote":"","text":"You have pretty explicitly framed Zig as a C replacement yourself, e.g.: [VIDEO] More broadly, I think the observation tends to get repeated because C and Zig share a certain elegance and simplicity (even if C's elegance has dated). C++ is many things, but it's hardly elegant or simple. I don't think anyone denies that Zig can be a C++ replacement, but that's hardly unusual, so can many other languages (Rust, Swift, etc). What's noteworthy here is that Zig is almost unique in having the potential to be a genuine C replacement. To its (and your) great credit, I might add. >> At its core Zig is marketed as a competitor to C, not C++/Rust/etc, which makes me think it's harder to write working code that won't leak or crash than in other languages. Zig embraces manual memory management as well. @GP: This is not a great take. All four languages are oriented around manual memory management. C++ inherits all of the footguns of C, whereas Zig and Rust try to sand off the rough edges. Manual memory management is and will always remain necessary. The only reason someone writing JS scripts don't need to worry about managing their memory is because someone has already done that work for them."},{"id":44209133,"author":"Cloudef","month":"2025-06","story":"Low-Level Optimization with Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Zig as evolution of C","quote":"This old talk from andrew really hammers in how zig is evolution of C","text":"> I do not understand your criticism of [N]. This gives compiler more information and catches errors. This is a good thing! It only does sane thing in GCC, in other compilers it does nothing and since it's very underspec'd it's rarely used in any C projects. It's shame Dennis's fat pointers / slices proposal was not accepted. > warrant the additional complexity IMHO In zig case the comptime reduces complexity, because it is simply zig. It's used to implement generics, you can call zig code compile time, create and return types. This old talk from andrew really hammers in how zig is evolution of C: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42670223,"author":"throwawaymaths","month":"2025-01","story":"Obvious things C should do","stance":"reference","why":"Shows comptime existed from early on","quote":"","text":"the constexpr stuff has been around since the beginning[0]. concretely, what other thing from D do you claim zig has added since? [0] minimally 2019, 3 years in: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37452694,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2023-09","story":"Learning Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Zig positioned as C with problems fixed","quote":"Zig is not supposed to be a Rust equivalent. It's supposed to be C but with the problems fixed.","text":"Zig is not supposed to be a Rust equivalent. It's supposed to be C but with the problems fixed. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36745576,"author":"smarx007","month":"2023-07","story":"EU to vote regulation that has a considerable potential to hurt OSS","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"All right, now that someone downvoted you (not me), I feel a mild obligation to respond. Regarding the ad hominem part, I invite you to watch [VIDEO] to understand why I think our industry needs to elevate the level of our craft. And also please take a look at the actual requirements CRA puts on devs in [LINK] (pages 2 and 3 – yes, just those two pages* plus requirements on documentation on page 8, which could be a bit more annoying than the requirements on pp. 2 and 3). I hope you will find them reasonable for the most part. Regarding GDPR, I am indeed sad that so many people interpret it incorrectly. This happens in part due to the influence of various groups, as you say. I invite you to read the blog of [LINK] to understand the spirit of GDPR (indeed, there is a thing called \"data minimization\" that could be the reason you find it difficult to collect more data without a solid justification; in a fun twist, §1(3)(e) of the CRA annex also mentions data minimization) and see that the progress is made slowly yet steadily. If you noticed big websites recently show the option to deny tracking cookies directly instead of \"manage cookies\", you got these folks to thank ( [LINK] ). BTW, I donate to them and think they are doing awesome work. * unless you are doing \"serious stuff\" (TM) as described on pages 5 and 6."},{"id":36745773,"author":"smarx007","month":"2023-07","story":"EU to vote regulation that has a considerable potential to hurt OSS","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I just think our industry is long overdue for some regulation ( [VIDEO] as an inspiration ) and I also found the CRA requirements on pp. 2 and 3 ( [LINK] ) quite reasonable. What shocked me is how nobody on the thread considered/appreciated those two things. Having said that, there is a chance CRA implementation will get bungled, but I hope for the better. Regarding the commercial activity: I found the screenshot of a 3-part test from a recent Eclipse call and I hope it's OK to post it ( [LINK] ). I think it's important to understand that in a multi-part test, you typically need to seriously hit a few points to \"pass\" the test. Some examples of what I would consider passing each point (but not necessarily the whole test): 1. Rust with its 6-week release cadence will quite likely pass the 1 part. 2. Nginx, k8s, Ubuntu LTS will quite likely pass the part 2 of the test. That's the part of the test that worries Github and others, because receiving money and significant contributions from corporate will contribute to scoring high on this point. 3. A project like Eclipse 4diac ( [LINK] ) would quite likely pass the 3rd part of the test. But then, Eclipse 4diac could claim that it's mainly an R&D project and thus not passing the part 2 of the test. And even part 1 of the test would be hard to establish with barely 1 release a year."},{"id":28461119,"author":"woodrowbarlow","month":"2021-09","story":"Maintain It with Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Zig creator on build system dependencies","quote":"andrew kelley has talked about this","text":"not really, unless you're only building for one platform. gnu make isn't portable and doesn't have any real dependency management, so meta build systems quickly become a necessity. cmake is the most portable and the only real option on windows, but it's common to see projects that maintain support for both cmake and autotools. and that's ignoring the nightmare of cross-compiling with gcc. andrew kelley has talked about this[1]. his main point was about the number of dependencies you end up with compared to zig, where the standard library includes a high-level build system api. so your zig build scripts are written in ziglang and run by the zig compiler. no extra dependencies. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28095562,"author":"xhealthgothx","month":"2021-08","story":"Looking into Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Zig's origin as a C dialect","quote":"","text":"Zig libraries can be exported with the C ABI[0], so pretty much anything can interface with it. You can write very fast code with it. It is also small and fits in your head. The only thing missing is a variety of implementations, however there is a clear path to bootstrapping the compiler from source with a semi-recent C++ compiler[1] and from there, there's already a growing number of supported targets[2]. There is also a major focus on making sure cross-compilation is well supported out of the box, including C code that Zig can also compile[3]. It's perfectly fair to compare Zig to C in this case, as the project originated as a dialect of C before the author decided to do a complete clean break[4]. The primary frame of reference when designing the language was C and addressing its shortcomings, both language and tooling. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27143147,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2021-05","story":"Building an efficient and portable programming language with Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Explains Zig's design choices around binary interfaces","quote":"In this video he explains the reason why he made those choices","text":"Indeed. In this video he explains the reason why he made those choices: [VIDEO] All these programming language features require a lot of machinery, complicating the binary interfaces of the resulting software and preventing code reuse."},{"id":27146044,"author":"AndyKelley","month":"2021-05","story":"Building an efficient and portable programming language with Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Zig creator's quote on C++ features","quote":"If everyone had their favorite feature from C++ in Zig, Zig would just be C++.","text":"quoting myself [1]: \"It's really easy to make fun of C++, but everyone has that one feature of C++ that they like. And they want to put it in Zig. If everyone had their favorite feature from C++ in Zig, Zig would just be C++. But I'm not going to let that happen.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":23807236,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2020-07","story":"Announcing The Zig Software Foundation","stance":"reference","why":"Why Zig avoids features that complicate binary interfaces","quote":"","text":"Operator overloading leads to a complex binary interface. Avoiding complexity in this area is important given that simple binary interface is a huge reason why reusable software is still written in C. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21971498,"author":"AndyKelley","month":"2020-01","story":"Hobby x86 kernel written with Zig","stance":"reference","why":"Author's talk addressing the question","quote":"Here is a talk I gave that directly addresses this question","text":"Here is a talk I gave that directly addresses this question: [VIDEO] (the title & abstract in the youtube description is the one I gave for the RFP; I ended up going in a slightly different direction than it once I actually made the talk)"},{"id":21385979,"author":"matheusmoreira","month":"2019-10","story":"JavaScript Is C","stance":"reference","why":"Zig creator on C ABI interoperability","quote":"if it doesn't speak the C ABI, someone's gonna rewrite it in C.","text":"Using anything other than C makes one's code unusable as a general purpose library due to the complexity of the binary interfaces. Writing C++ ensures the code won't be touched by anything but more C++ code. A Python script can load a C library shared object, resolve a symbol to a function pointer and call it. I don't think I'll ever see Python load a C++ library shared object, obtain a pointer to a C++ object and call methods on it using the C++ ABI and handle the C++ exceptions it may throw. The only way to interoperate is to sacrifice all the benefits of C++ and adopt a C interface. Rust has this feature as well. I like how the creator of Zig put it: if it doesn't speak the C ABI, someone's gonna rewrite it in C. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25418691,"author":"dnautics","month":"2020-12","story":"A Defer Mechanism for C","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> With a finger you can follow the code as it runs and know exactly what is going on and when exactly. Oh yeah? [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["-cEn_83zRFw",1],["UV1V9-nnaAs",1],["ON0A1dsQOV0",1],["ZDxLa6P6exc",1]]},"wrwgIjBUYVc":{"wy":"Called the best visualization of general relativity by many commenters, who say it cleared up long-held misunderstandings of gravity.","q":{"x":"hands down the best visualization of general relativity I've seen.","i":32228862,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32115958,"author":"nextaccountic","month":"2022-07","story":"Mass and angular momentum, left ambiguous by Einstein, get defined","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Made the commenter finally understand gravity as inertial motion","quote":"Here's the video that made me finally understand it","text":"But under this point of view there's no force at all. Gravity doesn't exert a force on you, the movement under gravity is purely inertial. I know this is bonkers. Here's the video that made me finally understand it [VIDEO] ( [LINK] is another good video from the same author)"},{"id":32228862,"author":"lahwran","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: YouTube Channels for the Intellectually Curious","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best visualization of GR","quote":"hands down the best visualization of general relativity I've seen.","text":"scienceclic also comes in an english edition. hands down the best visualization of general relativity I've seen. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31450268,"author":"nemothekid","month":"2022-05","story":"PBS SpaceTime","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing relativity presentations from ScienceClic","quote":"An absolute gem I've stumbled across is ScienceClic","text":"Science Asylum is great, but I dislike the 90s educational show presentation format. An absolute gem I've stumbled across is ScienceClic: [LINK] They have amazing presentations on Relativity, my favorite being: \"A new way to visualize General Relativity\" - [VIDEO] \"Hawking Radiation\" - [LINK] Another, while heavier and less accessible channel is Physics Explained: [LINK] These are closer like lectures, but I enjoy them because they go into the histories of some of these experiments; and what the prevailing wisdom was around the time the experiments were performed: \"What is the Ultraviolet Catastrophe?\" - [LINK]"},{"id":24822017,"author":"zestyping","month":"2020-10","story":"Gravity is not a force – free-fall parabolas are straight lines in spacetime","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clears up misunderstandings of gravity via spacetime visualization","quote":"This video really cleared up a lot of misunderstandings for me","text":"This video really cleared up a lot of misunderstandings for me: [VIDEO] I had never visualized it this way in my head before, and it all makes a lot more sense now. Highly recommend!"},{"id":48461809,"author":"rfgplk","month":"2026-06","story":"Show HN: Gravity – interactive solar-system simulator, from Newton to Einstein","stance":"rec","why":"Probably the best and most innovative spacetime visualization","quote":"probably the best and most innovative one I've seen.","text":"Very nice, fairly efficient too. I don't like the explicit split of Newtonian and relativistic gravity, this is often how it's presented in educational content, but it creates too much confusion; for instance it gives the illusion that they are somehow separate theories even though Newtonian gravity is a limiting case of Einsteinian gravity when v << c and gravitational fields are weak (see Poissons eq for Newtons gravitational potential. Lastly, you should consider rendering spacetime similar to Alessandro Roussels spacetime visualization [VIDEO] ; probably the best and most innovative one I've seen."},{"id":35092763,"author":"Amezarak","month":"2023-03","story":"Why AI Won't Cause Unemployment","stance":"rec","why":"Example of video making an idea clear","quote":"A great example (to me) is this [1] for example.","text":"Sure, no doubt, a video can sometimes make things much clearer. A great example (to me) is this [1] for example. I had understood from the old illustrations that I was supposed to imagine something like that, but video makes it incredibly clear. The actual net impact of screens in classrooms, however, appears to me to be extremely negative, and I think learning outcomes bear that out. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29488235,"author":"ben_w","month":"2021-12","story":"Gravitational waves should permanently distort space-time","stance":"rec","why":"Visualization starting from what is wrong with rubber sheet","quote":"Here’s a different visualisation that starts with what’s wrong with the rubber sheet","text":"The rubber sheet analogy is criticised for this exact reason. Here’s a different visualisation that starts with what’s wrong with the rubber sheet: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24822444,"author":"manigandham","month":"2020-10","story":"Gravity is not a force – free-fall parabolas are straight lines in spacetime","stance":"rec","why":"Good visualization of GR","quote":"Another good video to visualize GR is this by ScienceClic","text":"That's linked at the bottom of the article. Another good video to visualize GR is this by ScienceClic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41423376,"author":"passion__desire","month":"2024-09","story":"Einstein's Other Theory of Everything","stance":"reference","why":"Video visualizing GR","quote":"A new way to visualize General Relativity","text":"A new way to visualize General Relativity [VIDEO] This uses Adobe tools to visualize and take advantage of video format."}],"r":[["MBnnXbOM5S4",2],["LA_DrBwkiJA",1],["KVl5kMXz1vA",1],["Erp8IAUouus",1],["NGFhc8R_uO4",1],["996OiexHze0",1],["pwaWilO_Pig",1],["clnozSXyF4k",1],["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["U7VwtOrwceo",1],["rCfPQLVzus4",1],["ObvxPSQNMGc",1]]},"gcUWTa16Jc0":{"wy":"HN readers say it changed their view of APL and array languages, with a clear walkthrough of how a compiler can be written in this style.","q":{"x":"Nothing has ever convinced me about the potential for array languages in practice quite like watching Aaron Hsu","i":38982944,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40806408,"author":"Nadya","month":"2024-06","story":"APL Demonstration (1975) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed commenter's view of APL","quote":"I thought the same about APL until arcfide made a video about codfns a few years back. Was eye opening.","text":"I thought the same about APL until arcfide made a video about codfns a few years back. Was eye opening. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38982944,"author":"refset","month":"2024-01","story":"Thinking in an array language","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hsu develops parallel APL compiler using two Notepad windows","quote":"Nothing has ever convinced me about the potential for array languages in practice quite like watching Aaron Hsu","text":"Nothing has ever convinced me about the potential for array languages in practice quite like watching Aaron Hsu describe how he develops his parallel APL compiler [0] using two Notepad.exe windows side-by-side: [VIDEO] He has also written many comments (arcfide on HN) about this stuff before, e.g. discussing \"semantic density\" [1] > The compiler is designed so that I can see as much as possible with as little indirection as possible, so that when I see a piece of code I not only know how it works in complete detail, but how it connects to the world around it, and every single dependency related to it in basically one single half screen full of code (usually much less than that) without any jumps, paging, scrolling or any movement. [...] The idea of semantic density is critical to this point. The semantic density of the APL code I'm using to solve the problem is at a certain rate. I maintain a consistent density rate by choosing my variable names in such a way that they visually align with the expressivity per character of the built in primitive symbols. And > [...] idiomatic programming methods that are so concise, they can begin to be read as we read and chunk English phrases. By doing so, it becomes actually easier to just write out most algorithms, because the normal name for such an algorithm is basically as long as the algorithm itself written out. This means that I start to learn to chunk idioms as phrases and can read code directly, without the cost of name lookup indi"},{"id":27829579,"author":"Nadya","month":"2021-07","story":"Jelly: a recreational programming language inspired by J","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear walkthrough of Co-dfns compiler structure and APL code","quote":"He explains it very well - it's quite easy to follow along.","text":"One of the most memorable and interesting things I've ever seen on HN was an AMA from the creator of the Co-dfns parallel APL compiler. Here is co-dfns [0] and the AMA by the creator [1]. The video may give you some insight and at the very least may be extremely interesting to hear the APL code explained (jump to around 13:30 for the beginning of the explanation behind the structure + what the compiler is doing). He explains it very well - it's quite easy to follow along. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16421555,"author":"Nadya","month":"2018-02","story":"Common combinators in JavaScript","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Author explains the Co-dfns parallel APL compiler code","quote":"I'd highly recommend, if you have the time, to listen to the author explaining the code for the compiler","text":"What use is Chinese for someone who doesn't read Chinese? The S combinator written like that is perfectly legible and fine for someone who is already familiar with the S Combinator. I don't see the problem there and I don't already grok the S Combinator. I do appreciate writing code under the assumption people working on the code would have some level of prerequisite knowledge. I'm sure you'd get a kick out of Co-dfns [0]. But once you learn to \"speak\" APL programming it makes a lot more sense. I'd highly recommend, if you have the time, to listen to the author explaining the code for the compiler [1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO] / HN discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":17177051,"author":"a-saleh","month":"2018-05","story":"APL deserves its renaissance too","stance":"rec","why":"Aaron Hsu stream on readable APL code","quote":"I remember being floored by APL few months ago when I learned about co-dnfs and watched the stream by Aaron Hsu","text":"I remember being floored by APL few months ago when I learned about co-dnfs and watched the stream by Aaron Hsu [1] with quotes like \"This code is perfectly readable, it just isn't in English\". Thing is, when I was thinking about trying it, I never figured out a good toy project. Like, when I wanted to try anything else, most of the time I would know I would be able to cobble together a web-service. Haskell, Erlang, even Prolog. I understand co-dnfs to be self-hosting, so maybe a toy programming language? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45804321,"author":"tom_","month":"2025-11","story":"Learning to read Arthur Whitney's C to become smart (2024)","stance":"reference","why":"Dense-style compiler video related to the thread's terse C","quote":"Possibly related(ish): video about co-dfns","text":"Possibly related(ish): video about co-dfns, prompted by a previous HN thread (links in video summary), not written in C but put together in a similarly dense style: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37276803,"author":"sctb","month":"2023-08","story":"Modernizing Compiler Design for Carbon Toolchain [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Older, more detailed talk on compiler design","quote":"I just found the one I was thinking of, which is older but perhaps goes into more detail","text":"Thanks for the link! I just found the one I was thinking of, which is older but perhaps goes into more detail: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":21543385,"author":"9214","month":"2019-11","story":"The 80/24 Rule","stance":"reference","why":"Hsu discusses discouraging bloated definitions in APL","quote":"an idea that Aaron Hsu briefly mentions in his APL talks","text":"Old Forths with block-based I/O used 64/16 (so that definition takes ≤ 4096 bytes and fits into disk block). Makes no sense in the modern world of course, but, in the context of block editors, it creates a development environment where making complex and bloaty word definitions is actively discouraged -- an idea that Aaron Hsu briefly mentions in his APL talks [1]. [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ShEez0JkOFw",1],["ZJUyVVFOXOc",1],["gC295d3V9gE",1],["oYk8CKH7OhE",1]]},"UJ7Qc3bsxjI":{"wy":"HN commenters offer it as a dry but fascinating, hype-free introduction to Cascadia earthquake risk and the evidence for past events.","q":{"x":"It's better than most TV if you've got the time.","i":43503550,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43503550,"author":"OgsyedIE","month":"2025-03","story":"Orphan tsunami of 1700–Japanese clues to a parent earthquake in America (2015)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"70-minute lecture on PNW earthquakes by a great geology communicator","quote":"It's better than most TV if you've got the time.","text":"If you have 70 minutes of free time on the weekend and want to see one of the greatest geology communicators at their best, Nick Zentner did a lecture at the CWU on Pacific Northwest earthquakes that goes into decent depth about this past quake and tsunami: [VIDEO] It's better than most TV if you've got the time."},{"id":39673400,"author":"pests","month":"2024-03","story":"Why star dunes are rarely recognised in the rock record","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Introduced commenter to Nick Zentner's geology lectures","quote":"I actually found him from his Cascadia Earthquake video","text":"I'm a big fan already. I actually found him from his Cascadia Earthquake video: [VIDEO] (on the University channel) I highly suggest others check him out too if you're into this stuff."},{"id":27058279,"author":"breakyerself","month":"2021-05","story":"1700 Cascadia Earthquake","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Enthralling lecture by Nick Zentner on Cascadia","quote":"Here is an excellent lecture on the subject from CWU professor Nick Zenter.","text":"Here is an excellent lecture on the subject from CWU professor Nick Zenter. If you watch this there is a good chance you'll decide to watch all of his lecture on the channel. Very enthralling. [VIDEO]"},{"id":41825059,"author":"RickS","month":"2024-10","story":"Washington's 'forgotten giant' volcano stirs","stance":"rec","why":"Hour-long dry but fascinating presentation, free of popsci FUD","quote":"There's an hour long presentation from CWU [2] that's dry but fascinating, and pretty free of popsci FUD.","text":"Here in seattle, volcanoes feel like an afterthought attached to the thing that's actually scary, which is the Cascadia Subduction Zone[1]. There's an hour long presentation from CWU [2] that's dry but fascinating, and pretty free of popsci FUD. When the CSZ eventually goes, it's gonna be truly catastrophic. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25062118,"author":"01100011","month":"2020-11","story":"Disaster prepping is mainstream again","stance":"rec","why":"Introduction to evidence for past Cascadia megaquake timeline","quote":"This is a good introduction if you want to know more","text":"You should look into the supporting data before making comments like this. The evidence for the timeline of past Cascadia zone megaquakes is actually quite solid. We can even corroborate the records from North American samples with samples from Japan when the resulting tsunamis caused damage there. This is a good introduction if you want to know more: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37840159,"author":"vidanay","month":"2023-10","story":"Multifault earthquake threat for Seattle region revealed by mass tree mortality","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I just watched this video this evening.","text":"Bizarre...I just watched this video this evening. \"Great Earthquakes of the Pacific Northwest\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":24757446,"author":"01100011","month":"2020-10","story":"New Geologic Map Reveals Portland's Deep History","stance":"reference","why":"Lecture on Pacific Northwest great earthquakes","quote":"Great Earthquakes of the Pacific Northwest","text":"Links for the lazy: [LINK] Supervolcanoes in the Pacific Northwest: [LINK] Great Earthquakes of the Pacific Northwest: [VIDEO] Supercontinents and the Pacific Northwest: [LINK]"}],"r":[["NcOb3Dilzjc",1],["yXbJe-rUNP8",1],["dXdoim96v5A",1],["-ZXrJRpA0Jw",1],["I8Xc2_FtpHI",1],["X2wLP0izeJE",1],["SCAGc-rkIfo",1]]},"nfDoml-Db64":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends the series as a gripping account of scientific fraud, and cites it whenever research misconduct comes up.","q":{"x":"amazing piece of documentary","i":36926856,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38243825,"author":"atrettel","month":"2023-11","story":"Stanford Data Science Handbook","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on Schon's context and incentives to commit fraud.","quote":"I suggest watching this excellent series of videos by BobbyBroccoli on YouTube","text":"For those unfamiliar with the story of Jan Hendrik Schön, I suggest watching this excellent series of videos by BobbyBroccoli on YouTube [1-3]. The series does a good job breaking down the context and the situation that Schön was in, along with the incentives that encouraged Schön to commit fraud. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":36882486,"author":"asmor","month":"2023-07","story":"‘A very disturbing picture’: another retraction for controversial physicist","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Long video essay on the Schon case.","quote":"One of my favorite video essays is a long exploration of that case","text":"Schön got away with it for years. One of my favorite video essays is a long exploration of that case: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36926856,"author":"treesciencebot","month":"2023-07","story":"Room Temperature Superconductor Drama and Hope","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how science runs on trust and reputation, enabling fraudsters.","quote":"amazing piece of documentary","text":"This reminds me an amazing piece of documentary[1] I've watched a couple months prior about a man who tried to \"scam his way onto a Nobel prize\". It really goes into showing how the majority of the system is based on trust and reputation and without well skepticism fraudsters can thrive. [1]: \"The man who almost faked his way to a Nobel Prize\" -- [VIDEO]"},{"id":36952555,"author":"lucubratory","month":"2023-08","story":"Origin of correlated isolated flat bands in LK99","stance":"rec","why":"First part of a three-part documentary on Schon.","quote":"There's a great documentary on him","text":"Oh yeah, he is absolutely fucking wild. There's a great documentary on him, here's part one, the other two parts of the documentary are also on YouTube on the same channel: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35098993,"author":"mustacheemperor","month":"2023-03","story":"Allegations of Scientific Misconduct Mount as Physicist Makes His Biggest Claim","stance":"rec","why":"Three-part documentary on Schon's falsified data and Bell Labs fallout.","quote":"There's a great 3-part documentary on youtube about it","text":"It reminds me of Jan Hendrik Schön, who looked like physics' next emerging all-star wunderkind until his \"discoveries\" of crystalline semiconductors won him so many awards and attentions it was discovered his entire career had been built on falsified data. A huge embarassment for Bell Labs and a black mark on the final years of more than one great physicist's careers there, since so many people thought they were helping a great new mind succeed when they were in reality perpetuating his fraud. There's a great 3-part documentary on youtube about it, which argues Schön was probably a contender for the Nobel Prize in Physics before everything collapsed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30142990,"author":"jhallenworld","month":"2022-01","story":"Royal Society cautions against censorship of scientific misinformation online","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on the Schon organic semiconductor fraud.","quote":"There is an engrossing set of videos about it","text":">Most research is flawed or useless, Also the research could be downright fraudulent. A very interesting case is the Schön scandal, involving organic semiconductors. [LINK] There is an engrossing set of videos about it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36671398,"author":"bjourne","month":"2023-07","story":"Harvard ethics prof fabricated multiple behavioral science studies","stance":"reference","why":"Example of fraud in hard sciences.","quote":"","text":"Fabricated data and fraudulent results are not unknown to the hard sciences either. See f.e [VIDEO] A professor manipulating data for an amusing pop-psychology result is small potatoes in comparison to what has rocked the physics world."},{"id":36795837,"author":"dotnet00","month":"2023-07","story":"Stanford president resigns over manipulated research, will retract 3 papers","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary covering fallout of research fraud on students and postdocs.","quote":"This documentary series comes to mind","text":"This documentary series comes to mind [1], where in the end they go over the fallout and all the collective research years wasted and budding careers stunted (including people outright leaving research) because as students/postdocs trying to replicate the work of a scientist who was considered to be a potential nobel laureate, they couldn't possibly question the work they were trying to replicate. I feel this aspect of research fraud is even worse than the other established scientists who had their research impacted. In the mentioned case, the established scientists were essentially unharmed, while the PhDs and postdocs suddenly found themselves unable to list most of their experience on their CV, despite the established scientists being the most qualified to verify the fraudster's work. Especially for someone in a leadership position at a university, I wonder how that doesn't eat them up inside. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":31756407,"author":"rasz","month":"2022-06","story":"The first high-yield, sub-penny plastic processor","stance":"reference","why":"Story of the Schon fraud at Bell Labs.","quote":"","text":"My mind immediately went to Bell Labs Jan Schön superconducting plastics almost landing him a Nobel [VIDEO] (spoiler alert - it was all a scam)"}],"r":[["9qljpi5jiMQ",1],["s9aCwCKgkLo",1],["MondapIjAAM",1],["qv5b1Xowxdk",1],["YJrOuBkYCMQ",1],["WZskjLq040I",1],["huEtJw7pfLk",1]]},"plVk4NVIUh8":{"wy":"HN links it as a visually striking demonstration of evolution and antibiotic resistance happening in days.","q":{"x":"the mega-plate video is an unbelievably good demonstration of bacterial evolution","i":20367074,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20367074,"author":"robocat","month":"2019-07","story":"Bladder cancer 'attacked and killed by common cold virus'","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Unbelievably good demonstration of bacterial evolution","quote":"the mega-plate video is an unbelievably good demonstration of bacterial evolution","text":"> Why do you think the strongest of the strong bacteria are found in hospitals? I presume you mean strong against antibiotics and sterilisation. The evolved bacteria are likely weaker along other dimensions (that are less important for survival in a hospital setting). Some evolution is compromises, improving X has cost Y. Aside: the mega-plate video is an unbelievably good demonstration of bacterial evolution: [VIDEO] especially note the tree at the end showing how many different mutations evolved."},{"id":12555965,"author":"lossolo","month":"2016-09","story":"The Age of the Superbug Is Here","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Bacteria become antibiotic resistant in ten days","quote":"This is an amazing video[1] from Harvard Medical School","text":"This is an amazing video[1] from Harvard Medical School released couple of days ago showing evolution of bacteria that evolve in 10 days to become resistant to antibiotics. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34309571,"author":"idiocrat","month":"2023-01","story":"It's 2023 and I still have to touch a piss soaked door handle to exit a bathroom","stance":"rec","why":"Visualization of bacteria developing antibiotic resistance","quote":"here is an interesting visualization of bacteria developing resistance to antibiotics.","text":"Strengthens your immune system. Without exposure (playing in mud, touching other people), we are only weaker. Bio life is really complicated. Sterilization works well only to a certain degree, before becoming damaging, e.g. causing resistance. Somewhat related, here is an interesting visualization of bacteria developing resistance to antibiotics. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16507186,"author":"ramgorur","month":"2018-03","story":"The man who bottled evolution","stance":"rec","why":"Fast bacterial resistance evolution with lineage shown","quote":"It also clearly shows the lineage of evolution. Very interesting.","text":"Harvard medical school did a similar experiment on fast evolution of bacterial resistance to antibiotics on a petri-dish. It also clearly shows the lineage of evolution. Very interesting. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13454431,"author":"sengork","month":"2017-01","story":"How to Avoid a Post-Antibiotic World","stance":"rec","why":"Visualizes bacteria evolving against antibiotics over 11 days","quote":"For those that would like to visualise how the bacteria vs anitbiotics evolve in an experiment over 11 days","text":"For those that would like to visualise how the bacteria vs anitbiotics evolve in an experiment over 11 days, have a look at this video from Harvard: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12474724,"author":"Retric","month":"2016-09","story":"Videos of Evolution in Action","stance":"rec","why":"Visually striking real-time bacterial mutation demo","quote":"visually it's just really striking.","text":"The video shows mutations in real time as bacteria grow or fail to grow in various places. [VIDEO] It's more a demonstration than experiment, but visually it's just really striking."},{"id":48507925,"author":"MontyCarloHall","month":"2026-06","story":"CRISPR Tech Selectively Shreds Cancer Cells, Including \"Undruggable\" Cancers","stance":"reference","why":"Antibiotic selection mirrors tumor therapy resistance","quote":"It's exactly akin to how dosing isolated populations of bacteria with antibiotics selects for individual cells that are resistant","text":">there's no \"be a better/stronger cancer and spread more effectively to more hosts\" No, but there is \"be a better/stronger cancer cell and don't succumb to whatever therapy is killing its neighboring cells.\" It's exactly akin to how dosing isolated populations of bacteria with antibiotics selects for individual cells that are resistant, which then multiply and dominate [0], just like a tumor. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34768479,"author":"robocat","month":"2023-02","story":"A tiny genetic change inflicts old age on young kids","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Aging is one side effect of a major internal system that counteracts cancers. Most of our cells are preprogrammed to die after a certain number of replications. This is one of many systems that counteract normal genetic mutations - which can cause cancers and other side effects. By definition a cancer has to reproduce and one way of defeating cancers is for cells to die (or be killed) once they reach a number of cell reproductions. Think of a binary tree where the tree depth is the number of cell reproductions, and telomere length controls tree depth. Each time a cell reproduces it risks mutations. Stem cells are the progenitor branches, and stem cells maintain “cleaner” copies of DNA, which then become normal cells. Normal cells then reproduce a number of times before they kill themselves. We have evolved this system because it balances the shorter term harms of cancer (high cost preventing reproduction), and senescence (lower cost after reproduction). There can be no “cure” for cancer, or aging. The cure would for cancer would require some way to either cause perfect cell reproduction, or to remove (or correct) all cells with imperfect reproductions. The “cure” for aging would require a cure for cancer. If I were to invest in something myself, it would be brain transplant, since brain cancers are rare, and dealing with organ rejection seems doable, and the issue of nerve reconnection seems solvable. The main problem is finding spare bodies, which can be unethically solved f"},{"id":34919177,"author":"dennis_jeeves1","month":"2023-02","story":"Antibiotic that does not evoke bacterial resistance","stance":"reference","why":"Famous serial dilution style bacterial evolution video","quote":"Great video, ( if it's not fictitious)","text":">BTW, the \"serial dilution\" method name sounds to me like what is illustrated by this famous video: [VIDEO] Great video, ( if it's not fictitious)"},{"id":32040853,"author":"taf2","month":"2022-07","story":"No antibiotics worked, so this woman turned to phage scientists","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Really I feel like this has been a hot topic for at least 15 years.. for example 5 years ago this [VIDEO] was on hacker news and got a lot of discussion as I recall… Ok maybe you are right my search yielded few posts that got any traction - so maybe it was just me sharing with people I know!"},{"id":29648454,"author":"jasode","month":"2021-12","story":"DNA seen through the eyes of a coder","stance":"reference","why":"Short demo of bacteria evolving resistance","quote":"short 2 minute video showing how bacteria evolves with random mutations to produce resistance to antibiotics","text":">What are the tests? Tests == adaptation to the environment E.g. short 2 minute video showing how bacteria evolves with random mutations to produce resistance to antibiotics: [VIDEO] Likewise at a macro scale, if Earth's atmosphere becomes inhospitable because of global nuclear war or a big asteroid collision, then the new \"test for life\" is adapting to the radioactive dust cloud. Keep mutating across generations until survivors can thrive."},{"id":28205558,"author":"_vertigo","month":"2021-08","story":"Vaccination blunts, but does not defeat Delta","stance":"reference","why":"Resistance emerging under rising selective pressure","quote":"","text":"Honest question here, do vaccines actually reduce the chance for covid to mutate? Spitballing, I would expect a vaccine to decrease the number of total mutations that can occur but also simultaneously increase the selective pressure on the virus, increasing the chance that any mutations that improve the virus’ ability to resist the vaccine become dominant. To make an analogy, does spraying every surface in a hospital with an antibacterial solution result in no bacteria or just make it more likely to see resistant bacteria? I’d guess it’s a function of how potent the solution was and how well every last surface was coated. If you start with a 10% solution, don’t achieve full coverage and keep ramping up slowly perhaps we’ll just see something like this: [VIDEO] Agree with the rest of your post though."},{"id":27441165,"author":"woopwoop","month":"2021-06","story":"History as Weather","stance":"reference","why":"Shows natural selection verified without centuries","quote":"you absolutely do not need 500 years to verify natural selection","text":"Non-obvious is necessary to avoid appending \"and also the sun rises in the east and sets in the west every day\" to every prediction to get it to satisfy the other three. That a fact is observational does not make it non-scientific. I have no problem calling a description of a human cell science. I'd also add that you absolutely do not need 500 years to verify natural selection, see e.g. this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26488850,"author":"HPsquared","month":"2021-03","story":"Moore's Law for Everything","stance":"reference","why":"Visual metaphor for diminishing returns in innovation","quote":"","text":"Technological innovation is a balance between diminishing returns (ever-smaller niches getting filled, ever-increasing complexity) and the occasional breakthrough which spawns further development. The visual metaphor of bacteria developing antibiotic resistance in ever-greater levels of the antibiotic, springs to mind: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24920515,"author":"estebank","month":"2020-10","story":"SARS-CoV-2 spike D614G variant confers enhanced replication and transmissibility","stance":"reference","why":"Pattern of dispersion and mutation","quote":"","text":"The mitigations are effective in slowing the spread (to varying degrees, due to a multitude of factors), which in turn slow down the rate of mutation. In any case, I would expect the dispersion of the virus to follow a pattern similar to [VIDEO]"},{"id":18844138,"author":"lmilcin","month":"2019-01","story":"Pliocene and Eocene provide best analogs for near-future climates","stance":"reference","why":"Shows life's adaptive capacity","quote":"","text":"It is absolutely nowhere near evolutionary adaptive capacity. Life will not end and the world will still be there. Life's evolutionary adaptive capacity is stagerring! Just take a look at the experiment where it barely stretches muscles: [VIDEO] What I understand they mean is \"Life as we know it\" defined as large animals and familiar plants roughly in places where we are used to seeing them. We are looking at life with our antropocentric point of view and we basically want familiar stuff to stay the same and we don't want unfamiliar stuff to show up. We don't want things to change and that's why potential large shift in how life presents itself may be caulled as \"end of the world\" event, but it is not. Life doesn't care whether oceans are clean or polluted, hot or cold. It WILL adapt some way or another even if all crustaceans and fish will die because they can't adapt quickly enough to lowering pH. We should care not because life is at danger but because we are at danger. Life as we know it is a machine that supports us, let's us grow food and provides air and water and soil and climate that we need. Humans will survive for sure in one way or another even if the temperature rises by 20 degrees but what kind of life will it be and how much suffering for those that can't deal with it?"}],"r":[["wSdV1M7n4gQ",1],["ia7IKW0yuG0",1],["otbnC2zE2rw",1],["Nb2tebYAaOA",1],["u-x7PdnvCyI",1],["Jv7sLrON7QY",1],["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["Mjx3S3UjmnA",1],["0jGaio87u3A",1],["Cj4y0EUlU-Y",1],["cUzklzVXJwo",1],["PQmSUHhP3ug",1],["1OEu9C51K2A",1],["Zk11vI-7czE",1]]},"NlIdzF1_b5M":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on the 747 and A380 for explaining why big planes were phased out.","q":{"x":"This is a great video on the economics of the hub and spoke vs. direct model embodied by the A380 vs 787","i":16154412,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16052173,"author":"crikli","month":"2018-01","story":"A personal ode to the Boeing 747","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains why the 747 and A380 are being phased out","quote":"Some great (sometimes indirect) information on why the 747 and to a lesser extent the A380 are being phased out","text":"I was rolling my eyes at the subtitle. The article devolved from there. Some great (sometimes indirect) information on why the 747 and to a lesser extent the A380 are being phased out, from my new favorite YouTube channel, Wendover Productions: The Economics of Long-Haul Flights: [VIDEO] ETOPS: Explained [LINK] Politics of Aviation / 5 Freedoms of Aviation: [LINK]"},{"id":16154412,"author":"mthomasb","month":"2018-01","story":"Airbus A380, Once the Future of Aviation, May Cease Production","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hub and spoke versus direct model, A380 versus 787","quote":"This is a great video on the economics of the hub and spoke vs. direct model embodied by the A380 vs 787","text":"This is a great video on the economics of the hub and spoke vs. direct model embodied by the A380 vs 787: [VIDEO] That channel also has alot of very interesting content on the economics of air travel (e.g., seating classes, how tickets are priced, etc)."},{"id":34617656,"author":"rdhatt","month":"2023-02","story":"Easter egg in flight path of last 747 delivery flight","stance":"rec","why":"Covers economics of big versus small long-haul planes","quote":"Wendover Productions has a great video on this topic","text":"Wendover Productions has a great video on this topic: Big Plane vs Little Plane (The Economics of Long-Haul Flights) [VIDEO]"},{"id":19110134,"author":"michaelt","month":"2019-02","story":"A380 cancellations by Qantas raise new questions about the superjumbo's future","stance":"rec","why":"Explains A380 hub-and-spoke bet versus 787 point-to-point","quote":"You might enjoy watching [1].","text":"You might enjoy watching [1]. TLDR: There's a ~20 year lead time on a radical new plane design. The A380 is designed for an airline industry dominated by 'hub-and-spoke' airlines (where a large plane can make efficient use of expensive landing slots at busy airports), while the 787 Dreamliner is designed for an airline industry dominated by 'point-to-point' airlines (where a small plane can avoid the busy airport and expensive landing slot by serving smaller airports). 20 years after they were both conceived of, it's looking like the hub-and-spoke model is the future. Oh, there's still some demand for such a plane, and some routes are busy enough to benefit from it. But if there isn't enough demand to keep the production lines open, the production lines get closed, leaving existing operators high and dry. That fact makes operators nervous to place orders, which is of course a vicious cycle. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16096356,"author":"jasode","month":"2018-01","story":"As of today, no US passenger airlines operate the Boeing 747","stance":"rec","why":"Compares A380 and 787 economics in ten minutes","quote":"The youtube channel Wendover has lots of educational videos about transportation","text":">, what has taken over in the big jumbo market? The \"big jumbo market\" itself has shrunk in demand and was made less relevant by the newer generation of long-range fuel-efficient (but smaller) planes like Boeing 737Max/777/787 and Airbus A330. Airbus originally projected sales of 1200 A380s in 2005 but only sold ~300 so far. The \"missing\" 900 sales happened because many airlines rejected A380s designed for hub-&-spoke in favor of smaller planes designed for point-to-point. The youtube channel Wendover has lots of educational videos about transportation and he has a 10 minute video about the different economics of Airbus A380 vs Boeing 787: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16153355,"author":"peterjlee","month":"2018-01","story":"Airbus A380, Once the Future of Aviation, May Cease Production","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why the 787 is doing better than the A380","quote":"Here's a relevant video from Wendover productions explaining why Boeing 787 is doing better than A380.","text":"Here's a relevant video from Wendover productions explaining why Boeing 787 is doing better than A380. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15479165,"author":"nighthawk454","month":"2017-10","story":"As the 747 Begins Its Final Approach, a Pilot Takes a Flight Down Memory Lane","stance":"rec","why":"Touches on the need for a middle-tier plane","quote":"Wendover Productions has an interesting video which touches on this, and the need for a new middle-tier plane.","text":"Wendover Productions has an interesting video which touches on this, and the need for a new middle-tier plane. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14174169,"author":"afarrell","month":"2017-04","story":"Ask HN: How do you unwind at the end of a day?","stance":"rec","why":"Channel on transportation, listed among unwinding favorites","quote":"Wendover Productions — mostly transportation","text":"Ooooh, these are nice. Here are mine: Extra History: stories of historical incidents — [LINK] Real Engineering: mostly materials science — [LINK] - Wendover Productions — mostly transportation - [VIDEO] Tom Scott: a bit of everything — [LINK] - Jay foreman: Urban planning and maps — [LINK] The Engineer Guy: Manufacturing — [LINK] Practical Engineering: Mostly Civil Engineering — [LINK] 3Blue1Brown: Math, visualized to me more intuitive — [LINK]"}],"r":[["HSxSgbNQi-g",2],["VYhAGnsnO7w",1],["S4O5voOCqAQ",1],["B2482h_TNwg",1],["Bd8_vO5zrjo",1],["gDfNRWsMRsU",1],["wh4aWZRtTwU",1],["qs2QcycggWU",1],["KRcecxdGxvQ",1],["0MeRN7LE1LQ",1],["069y1MpOkQY",1],["hBpetDxIEMU",1],["thqbjA2DC-E",1],["PYylPRX6z4Q",1],["rStL7niR7gs",1],["Yocja_N5s1I",1],["nm1TxQj9IsQ",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["bBC-nXj3Ng4",1],["kjBOesZCoqc",1]]},"URSWYvyc42M":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an incredible talk whose testing principles apply beyond Ruby.","q":{"x":"watch Sandi Metz' incredible RailsConf talk","i":6450331,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":6450331,"author":"AlwaysBCoding","month":"2013-09","story":"Testing code is simple","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sandi Metz on testing the interface rather than the implementation.","quote":"watch Sandi Metz' incredible RailsConf talk","text":"What I wish people had told me about testing: * You write tests in accordance with a pattern: 1. Arrange 2. Act 3. Assert 4. Annihilate If you annihilate your test data with configuration options, and you abstract your \"arranging\" into factories and custom methods, then every test you write should be exactly three lines. When I learned this it gave me a consistent mental framework to think about how to write good tests. * Test the interface not the implementation: This was an eye opening realization about what to test. If you haven't seen it yet, watch Sandi Metz' incredible RailsConf talk - [VIDEO] - it's so good * Stubs: Stubs are exactly like the viruses' that we learned about in biology class. You load them up with a value then they literally latch onto a method and inject that value into it. When you're just starting out with testing, effective use of stubs can get you really far, and only when you start hitting the limits of practicality is when you need to think about bringing in something more complicated. * Testing isn't easy per se, but it is definitely enjoyable if you focus on making it that way * Cucumber is pretty stupid, don't waste your time with it"},{"id":21546125,"author":"jakespracher","month":"2019-11","story":"Ask HN: Do you write tests before the implementation?","stance":"rec","why":"Good strategies for making testing easier and less painful.","quote":"There are many good strategies to make it easier that in my experience have yet to be well adopted","text":"Not always before, but always eventually and usually once I’m done with “exploration”. Testing done right should be a time saver in the long run! I think many people are turned off by testing and especially unit testing because it ends up being difficult and maintenance is more of a pain than it’s worth. There are many good strategies to make it easier that in my experience have yet to be well adopted: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":18438677,"author":"teeray","month":"2018-11","story":"Measuring and Improving Your CI/CD Pipelines","stance":"rec","why":"Sandi Metz advice on using mocks in tests appropriately.","quote":"I particularly like her advice on using mocks in tests appropriately.","text":"There are some really fantastic testing resources in the Ruby community. The talk that most influenced my approach to building unit tests (even though I write Go these days) was one of Jim Weirich’s: [LINK] I’ve found that his zero-knowledge approach gives me a suite of tests that have high signal when they fail. Sandi Metz has also spoken extensively on testing, and I particularly like her advice on using mocks in tests appropriately. This talk of hers on the subject comes to mind: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16251742,"author":"matchmike1313","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: How much time do you invest in writing tests?","stance":"rec","why":"Methodology for deciding what to test.","quote":"I would recommend watching this video by Sandi Matz on testing","text":"I spend about 30% of my time on a new feature writing tests. BTW that is in RoR which the primary language I code in. I would recommend watching this video by Sandi Matz on testing: [VIDEO] . I have used this methodology a lot to determine what I should test."},{"id":7133157,"author":"richardjordan","month":"2014-01","story":"The Failures of \"Intro to TDD\"","stance":"rec","why":"Testing principles applicable beyond Ruby for OO programmers.","quote":"Shout out for Sandi Metz book POODR, and her Railsconf talk The Magic Tricks of Testing","text":"Shout out for Sandi Metz book POODR, and her Railsconf talk The Magic Tricks of Testing, if you're a rubyist (though the principles hold true for non-ruby OO programmers too). [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4fFDFbi3toc",1],["N3bJnKmeNJY",1],["dP1xVpMPn8M",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1]]},"iukBMY4apvI":{"wy":"Commenters credit it with opening their eyes to JSPM and using it to choose build systems.","q":{"x":"This video really opened my eyes to what it could do","i":11018397,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":15064758,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2017-08","story":"Vue.js and Brunch: Webpack Alternative","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Old but still valid video that got the commenter hooked on JSPM.","quote":"here's the video that got me hooked (it's pretty old but still valid)","text":"If you're looking for alternatives to Webpack, please also check out JSPM (a good short description might be \"preprocessing system + package manager + systemjs\"): [LINK] It takes a little more to get it to work with vue (the plugin that makes .vue files work is older and unsupported nowadays), but I use it just fine, with my template, styling and component files separated (I wrote a little thing: [LINK] ) If you dislike how complex webpack is, you'll like JSPM -- here's the video that got me hooked (it's pretty old but still valid): [VIDEO]"},{"id":11018397,"author":"hardwaresofton","month":"2016-02","story":"What's New in Webpack 2","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Showed what JSPM can do as package manager, bundler and loader.","quote":"This video really opened my eyes to what it could do","text":"I regularly use JSPM (with systemJS) for new projects, and I've liked it a lot. Back when webpack first came out, I struggled a little bit with figuring out what it actually did. While I understand now, I am not why I would want to (or you) switch from JSPM to webpack. I don't see any features that webpack has that JSPM doesn't (and JSPM offered ES6 support well before of course, with babel on by default). I like to use JSPM because of how relatively easy everything fits together -- and how straightforward it is to use and configure. For those not familiar, JSPM is a package manager, module bundler, and module loader all in one for javascript applications. This means you can: jspm install <package> And JSPM will look in NPM/Github (and it's possible to add registries, like bower) for stuff . That's the package management facet. jspm bundle-sfx js/main dist/build.js Bundle your code all together, and drop it in a file <import src=\"system.js\"> <script> // set our baseURL reference path System.config({ baseURL: '/app' }); // loads /app/main.js System.import('main.js'); </script> Load your modules on the fly like you would with something like RequireJS. As you may have guessed, JSPM relies on SystemJS ( [LINK] ) internally for some of the features above. This video really opened my eyes to what it could do: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9415166,"author":"mrspeaker","month":"2015-04","story":"Deploying ECMAScript 6","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing dev video that introduced the commenter to JSPM and live-server.","quote":"I got into that after watching this amazing dev video","text":"My favourite way is via JSPM and live-server. I got into that after watching this amazing dev video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9955344,"author":"bkurtz13","month":"2015-07","story":"Browserify vs. Webpack – Configuration isn't evil","stance":"rec","why":"Nice overview of JSPM for choosing build systems.","quote":"watching this video[2] for a nice overview.","text":"As if the churn wasn't already bad enough, I've recently started using JSPM[1] and it's pretty awesome. I'm writing ES6+ code with hot reloading react components. If you're trying to decide between build systems I would recommend checking it out, and watching this video[2] for a nice overview. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["B5NULPSiOGw",1],["cYsVvV1aVss",1],["o2HBHckrdQc",1],["AdNJ3fydeao",1],["sK5i-N34im8",1],["HPuvDm8IC-4",1],["HyznrdDSSGM",1],["aAlR3cezPJg",1],["pW-SOdj4Kkk",1]]},"LEZv-kQUSi4":{"wy":"HN commenters love it as a witty and insightful tour of design anti-patterns, applicable to DSLs, OOP and API design.","q":{"x":"a great talk by Stuart Halloway called Narcissistic Design.","i":18528303,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18528303,"author":"smnplk","month":"2018-11","story":"The faster you unlearn OOP, the better for you and your software","stance":"strong_rec","why":"","quote":"a great talk by Stuart Halloway called Narcissistic Design.","text":"Reading some of the comments here reminded me of a great talk by Stuart Halloway called Narcissistic Design.[1] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17783089,"author":"ajss","month":"2018-08","story":"How to Write Unmaintainable Code (2017)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on anti-patterns in system design.","quote":"I love Stuart Halloway's talk on Narcissistic Design","text":"Along a similar line, I love Stuart Halloway's talk on Narcissistic Design - [VIDEO] Prefer APIs over data. Start with DSLs. Always connect (and never enqueue). Create abstractions for information. Use static typing across subsystem boundaries. Put language semantics on the wire. Write lots of unit tests. Leverage context. Update information in place."},{"id":27844573,"author":"blacktriangle","month":"2021-07","story":"SQLBolt – Interactive lessons and exercises to learn SQL","stance":"rec","why":"Covers anti-patterns including why SQL is only a DSL.","quote":"This talk goes into many anti-patterns","text":"[VIDEO] This talk goes into many anti-patterns, but he gets to the real problem with SQL, that it is ONLY a DSL and lacks either an API or a fundamental information model to program against. Composability when working with a DSL is a nightmare. I also have been moving away from ORM to directly writing SQL, but lets be honest about the reasons that programmers are wary of working directly with SQL as they are legitimate issues."},{"id":26415395,"author":"echlebek","month":"2021-03","story":"I don't want to learn your query language (2018)","stance":"rec","why":"Insights and quips about DSLs and design.","quote":"Has some great insights and quips.","text":"Nobody seems to have posted Stuart Halloway's Narcissistic Design yet, so I'll just leave this here. [VIDEO] Has some great insights and quips. \"Nothing says 'screw you' like a DSL\""}],"r":[["ciStnd9Y2ak",1],["IRTfhkiAqPw",1],["2V1FtfBDsLU",1]]},"jVoSQP2yUYA":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it whenever cassette quality comes up, as proof that cassettes sound good with decent gear, metal tape and Dolby noise reduction.","q":{"x":"They are more than capable of sounding great with the right tools.","i":46203056,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":46203056,"author":"klez","month":"2025-12","story":"Modern Walkmans","stance":"rec","why":"Discussion of cassette quality, arguing good gear makes them sound great.","quote":"They are more than capable of sounding great with the right tools.","text":"Take a look here[0] for a discussion about cassette quality. TL;DR: Like many of us you probably had shitty equipment and shitty cassettes. They are more than capable of sounding great with the right tools. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17504039,"author":"voltagex_","month":"2018-07","story":"Vaporwave’s Mini-Boom of Floppy Disk Releases","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting look at cassettes","quote":"This is an interesting watch if you've got the time.","text":"[VIDEO] - \"better than you don't remember\". This is an interesting watch if you've got the time."},{"id":15319776,"author":"rwmj","month":"2017-09","story":"Take a trip through music history with Archive.org's Great 78 Project","stance":"rec","why":"How to make cassette tape sound very good","quote":"They're worth watching","text":"Matt from Techmoan did a couple of videos about how to make cassette tape sound very good: [LINK] [LINK] They're worth watching but to be honest it hasn't made me want to convert all my music to tapes. Edit: The link I was actually looking for is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13387233,"author":"anonova","month":"2017-01","story":"Music's weird cassette-tape revival is paying off","stance":"rec","why":"Cassettes sounded better than remembered with good tapes and noise reduction","quote":"Here's a great video on the subject","text":"> They sounded bad. Cassettes weren't _that_ bad. The problem is that there were essentially different grades of tapes and players that supported different noise reduction techniques, and people tend to remember the ones played back with low end gear. Here's a great video on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13390526,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2017-01","story":"Music's weird cassette-tape revival is paying off","stance":"rec","why":"Type IV cassettes with Dolby S give decent sound","quote":"you can get pretty decent sound output","text":">\"As others have attested there is nothing good about tapes.\" Tapes weren't as bad as you may think/remember, at least not from an audio point of view, if you Type-IV cassettes and Dolby S noise reduction you can get pretty decent sound output: [VIDEO] I personally preferred MiniDisc, but I don't see them making a resurgence, probably lots of proprietary parts of the tech owned by Sony."},{"id":12135122,"author":"voltagex_","month":"2016-07","story":"Japan Will Make Its Last VCR This Month","stance":"rec","why":"Techmoan's video on cassette tapes","quote":"TechMoan on YouTube has a great video on cassette tapes","text":"TechMoan on YouTube has a great video on cassette tapes - granted he's got thousands and thousands of dollars worth of audio equipment. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46203406,"author":"tmountain","month":"2025-12","story":"Modern Walkmans","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for cassettes sounding good with proper equipment.","quote":"When recorded and played back with dolby noise reduction, it sounds pretty damn good!","text":"Tapes are exceptionally durable when cared for properly. Here's a video of a guy that tests for loss of quality after 1,000 plays. [LINK] Quality is indistinguishable from the first playback. Tapes have a bad reputation because most people used them in the cars, which is the equivalent of storing them in an oven on a daily basis. A lot of car stereos were very cheap, and that lead to a lot of cassettes being damaged when they would have been fine otherwise. Regarding the quality argument. Again, it's going to depend on the media and the equipment. I have a very nice Marantz tape deck, and I use chrome tapes with it. When recorded and played back with dolby noise reduction, it sounds pretty damn good! [VIDEO] I say this as someone that also owns a very nice turntable and has a digital FLAC media collection, so I'm not married to tapes in any way. They're just something fun to goof around with (and mostly to give my kid a more tangible experience with playing music at home). Regarding convenience, I can't argue that they're the least convenient media. That said, I'm an album guy, so I like to listen to recordings in their entirety most of the time."},{"id":43225867,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2025-03","story":"Vinyl carver sparking a craze for cutting records at home","stance":"reference","why":"Shows cassettes sounding fantastic with decent equipment.","quote":"With decent equipment, cassettes sound fantastic","text":"With decent equipment, cassettes sound fantastic [VIDEO] Unfortunately all the cassette mechanisms (and cassettes themselves) manufactured and sold new today are complete garbage. And the old ones need a lot of (physical) maintenance to get back into spec."},{"id":20603967,"author":"kalleboo","month":"2019-08","story":"Cassettes Are Back, and It’s Not About the Music","stance":"reference","why":"Techmoan examines how good cassette audio quality can really be.","quote":"A video on the subject of cassette tape audio quality from Techmoan","text":"A video on the subject of cassette tape audio quality from Techmoan: \"Cassettes - better than you don't remember\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["j-vJxez9UF8",1],["jAhiMCSvtCc",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["7jPKEyM44GU",1],["AvEqfg2sIH0",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["FnEWBtCnFs8",1],["fD2briZ6fB0",1],["8S3B8a8N83k",1],["Qaq9vlfoGnA",1],["KgzQuE1pR1w",1],["fg0_Vhldz-8",1],["R5Gppi-O3a8",1],["GLG9g7BcjKs",1],["ERbvKrH-GC4",1],["iRsSjh5TTqI",1],["iFDe5kUUyT0",1],["Hy4QjmKzF1c",1],["Mh5LY4Mz15o",1]]},"k7X7sZzSXYs":{"wy":"HN commenters point lonely people to it as a moving, comforting piece of art about getting comfortable with solitude.","q":{"x":"I think it nicely captures the sentiment of doing things alone.","i":9562984,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":47302566,"author":"idealboy","month":"2026-03","story":"Ask HN: How to Be Alone?","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful for learning to be alone.","quote":"an old YouTube video I found helpful called how to be alone","text":"Most responses are about how not to be alone. Your question reminded me of an old YouTube video I found helpful called how to be alone: [VIDEO]"},{"id":47305124,"author":"forrestpitz","month":"2026-03","story":"Ask HN: How to Be Alone?","stance":"rec","why":"Moving poem about comfort in being alone.","quote":"I've always found this poem moving and valuable when it comes to building comfort in being alone.","text":"Maybe not specific advice for your situation but I've always found this poem moving and valuable when it comes to building comfort in being alone. I hope it helps you too [VIDEO]"},{"id":30668785,"author":"ptrklly","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: I'm So Lonely","stance":"rec","why":"Art about loneliness, worth watching.","quote":"One piece of art that I loved that is about this topic is this","text":"I'm sorry you feel this way! I've felt very lonely in the past and it was among my hardest times--though I'm sure my version of it was different and doesn't have specific applicability, just figured I'd share. You asked on \"tips for giving yourself attention\". Can you elaborate on what you mean by that? Are there examples you could give us of what that is like, to help us think of other ideas? One piece of art that I loved that is about this topic is this: [VIDEO] . Like most art it doesn't really answer your question directly with concrete suggestions, but maybe it will be worth watching anyway."},{"id":9562984,"author":"barbs","month":"2015-05","story":"Start doing more things alone","stance":"rec","why":"Captures the sentiment of doing things alone.","quote":"I think it nicely captures the sentiment of doing things alone.","text":"This video did the rounds a few years ago, and I think it nicely captures the sentiment of doing things alone. [VIDEO]"},{"id":5935463,"author":"paganel","month":"2013-06","story":"Only the Lonely","stance":"rec","why":"Helped the commenter through loneliness.","quote":"it just genuinely helped me from time to time and I hope it will also help others.","text":"Maybe lots of people already know about it, but I'll just copy-paste this in here: [VIDEO] It's not an anti-dote (to loneliness and everything), it just genuinely helped me from time to time and I hope it will also help others."},{"id":1722934,"author":"fleitz","month":"2010-09","story":"How Loneliness is Contagious","stance":"rec","why":"Sums up the commenter's view on being alone.","quote":"I guess this video kind of sums up my thoughts.","text":"I've often been alone but never felt lonely, I guess this video kind of sums up my thoughts. \"How to be alone\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ge7i60GuNRg",1],["xLEe496IS1o",1],["bwDrHqNZ9lo",1],["sJj9y4t9UnU",1],["uXdtafGdIVM",1],["w-I6XTVZXww",1]]},"AD4b-52jtos":{"wy":"HN regularly recommends Hamming's lecture series as a fascinating, wisdom-rich course on how to approach hard technical problems and a career, often preferred to the book.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend Richard Hamming's wonderful course The Art of Doing Science and Engineering: Learning to Learn [1]","i":17895603,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":19250494,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2019-02","story":"Ask HN: Best Online Courses?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating lectures showing how Hamming thinks, from computers to codes","quote":"every one of them is fascinating.","text":"Sapolsky - Biology of Human Behavior 25 lectures [LINK] It's accessible to laymen, no prior knowledge needed. It covers so many different fields and levels of knowledge (\"buckets\" Sapolsky calls them), different ways of explaining human behaviour. By the signals in their brain, or the hormones in their blood, or what happened that day, or their childhood, or their genes, or evolution of humans etc. Also looks at other animals. A lot of touching/funny/inspiring/poignant stories about scientists in the field(s). Sapolsky is an amazing lecturer/raconteur. Hamming - Learning to Learn 32 lectures [VIDEO] The famous \"You and your research\" is one lecture in this, but every one of them is fascinating. History of computers, AI, codes, n-space, digital filters etc etc. Mainly it's great seeing how his mind works, his thinking style. (I've since read a few of his books, and I love how they're soaked with practical experience, in the same way these talks are. It's all stuff he's lived .)"},{"id":17895603,"author":"charlysl","month":"2018-09","story":"How to teach yourself hard things","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hamming's course on learning hard subjects","quote":"I highly recommend Richard Hamming's wonderful course The Art of Doing Science and Engineering: Learning to Learn [1]","text":"If your are interested in learning hard subjects, I highly recommend Richard Hamming's wonderful course The Art of Doing Science and Engineering: Learning to Learn [1], and the related book [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":11130885,"author":"hydandata","month":"2016-02","story":"Why There Is No Hitchhiker’s Guide to Mathematics for Programmers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hamming's lectures helped commenter try learning mathematics again","quote":"Recently, after watching R.W. Hamming lectures and reading his book","text":"I love programming, but never got the hang of Mathematics due to various reasons. Failed a big project as a solo programmer long time ago, when I was 17, because I could not create the necessary mathematical model and no help was available, with everything else completed, infrastructure, web site/interface database etc. Soon afterwards set off on a different career path, but always kept an intimate relationship with programming, doing some both at work and for fun at home. Recently, after watching R.W. Hamming lectures and reading his book \"Art of Doing Science and Engineering\" [1] [2] decided that I would try learning mathematics again. I found it really hard to do. There are so many implicit assumptions, so much ground that is not covered. I was about to give up again, when I found a book [3] by retired computer science professor, who seemed to have written it specifically for me! it really felt that way. Now I recommend it to almost everyone, especially tech-savvy people. I am no longer afraid of mathematics, with the help of this book, and great advice by Hamming, I am finally \"getting it\". If you find learning new programming languages easy, but struggle with mathematics, check it out! The book is not aimed at teaching you mathematics, rather, it teaches you those missing pieces, that almost every other book either assumes you already know, or describes vaguely and badly, gives you context and valuable advice. To me it almost feels like it is teaching you a functional pr"},{"id":18023238,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2018-09","story":"You and Your Research (1986)","stance":"rec","why":"Complete Learning to Learn lectures, even better than the books","quote":"But the complete Learning to Learn lectures are on youtube, which is even better","text":"I love all his books - you get the know-how, not just know-that. But the complete Learning to Learn lectures are on youtube, which is even better: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24056436,"author":"pinkrobotics","month":"2020-08","story":"Ask HN: Ambitious robotics entrepreneur with failing Kickstarter. Seeking advice","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as something the commenter's reply reminded them of","quote":"Your image and words remind me of this","text":"Your image and words remind me of this: [VIDEO] Marketing and sales are the domains I am the worst at, maybe we should chat. Though, while I do foresee a market for carbon, I do not see much in the way for climate solving robots, only return is saving the world ;)"},{"id":19952935,"author":"dmos62","month":"2019-05","story":"Highly Compressed Richard Hamming's Lectures","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"These are encoded with Speex, the precursor to opus.","text":"These are encoded with Speex, the precursor to opus. If you actually want to download the audio, you can use youtube-dl to get opus encoded audio-only recordings directly: > youtube-dl -F [VIDEO] [youtube] [VIDEO] Downloading webpage [youtube] [VIDEO] Downloading video info webpage [info] Available formats for [VIDEO] format code extension resolution note 249 webm audio only DASH audio 51k , opus @ 50k, 16.97MiB 250 webm audio only DASH audio 57k , opus @ 70k, 17.50MiB 171 webm audio only DASH audio 99k , vorbis@128k, 31.57MiB 251 webm audio only DASH audio 106k , opus @160k, 32.55MiB 140 m4a audio only DASH audio 132k , m4a_dash container, mp 4a.40.2@128k, 43.56MiB 160 mp4 194x144 144p 69k , avc1.4d400c, 30fps, video on ly, 10.50MiB 278 webm 194x144 144p 86k , webm container, vp9, 30fps, video only, 22.85MiB 133 mp4 322x240 240p 121k , avc1.4d400d, 30fps, video on ly, 17.77MiB 242 webm 322x240 240p 153k , vp9, 30fps, video only, 26.6 1MiB 18 mp4 352x262 small , avc1.42001E, mp4a.40.2@ 96k, 114. 86MiB 43 webm 640x360 medium , vp8.0, vorbis@128k, 136.89MiB (b est) The lowest quality version (opus @ 50k) is around 17mb compared with this author's 1.5mb speex version."}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["MdmQUlD7P40",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["cKPlPJyQrt4",1]]},"MdmQUlD7P40":{"wy":"Recommended as a jaw-dropping, must-see talk that gives an amazing intro to static analysis alongside Might's personal story.","q":{"x":"If you haven't seen it, Might's talk \"Winning the War on Error: Solving the Halting Problem and Curing Cancer\" is amazing, a must-see.","i":19277708,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19277708,"author":"yesenadam","month":"2019-03","story":"How To: Get Tenure – Matt Might","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Amazing intro to static analysis and Might's personal story.","quote":"If you haven't seen it, Might's talk \"Winning the War on Error: Solving the Halting Problem and Curing Cancer\" is amazing, a must-see.","text":"If you haven't seen it, Might's talk \"Winning the War on Error: Solving the Halting Problem and Curing Cancer\" is amazing, a must-see. It tells some of the same story, also is an introduction to static analysis..and cancer. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17958792,"author":"the_grue","month":"2018-09","story":"A Battle Plan for a War on Rare Diseases: Conversation with Matt Might","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Ambitious talk by Matt Might on static analysis and curing disease.","quote":"gave a jaw-dropping talk a year ago","text":"Matt Might (the subject of this article) gave a jaw-dropping talk a year ago, named \"Winning the War on Error - Solving the Halting Problem and Curing Cancer\" [1]. At least you can't blame him for the lack of ambition! But the best part is he's really getting it done. His security research led to developing very successful static analysis tools that exposed security bugs in real code. And he did manage to find a treatment for his own son's rare genetic disorder. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15927497,"author":"dpeck","month":"2017-12","story":"Ask HN: What's your favorite tech talk from 2017?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Matt Might talk on solving the halting problem and curing cancer.","quote":"is one that is well worth watching.","text":"Matt Mights \"Winning the War on Error: Solving the Halting Problem and Curing Cancer\" is one that is well worth watching. [VIDEO] previous discussion on it: [LINK]"}],"r":[["ras_VYgA77Q",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["G1r55efei5c",1],["AD4b-52jtos",1],["NP9AIUT9nos",1],["ynbgMKclWWc",1],["8pTEmbeENF4",1],["e3msMuwqp-o",1],["h2cs8QLnxlU",1],["EY6q5dv_B-o",1],["oUaOucZRlmE",1],["agOdP2Bmieg",1],["443UNeGrFoM",1],["klTjiXjqHrQ",1],["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["q_vivC7c_1k",1],["ef2jpjTEB5U",1],["XvDZLjaCJuw",1],["KJe9H6qS82I",1],["lKXe3HUG2l4",1]]},"QRaACa1Mrd4":{"wy":"HN links it in threads on absolute pitch as a musician's useful summary of its downsides and age-related decline.","q":{"x":"This video by Adam Neely was helpful","i":43043745,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43043745,"author":"physicles","month":"2025-02","story":"Learning fast and accurate absolute pitch judgment in adulthood","stance":"rec","why":"Helpful for understanding pitch drifting with age.","quote":"This video by Adam Neely was helpful","text":"This is currently happening to me: I’m starting to be off by a half step when I guess what key something is in. I’m 40 now, and I started noticing it a few years ago. Maybe it started early because I don’t play much music so I wasn’t using it much. This video by Adam Neely was helpful: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43046424,"author":"soyyo","month":"2025-02","story":"Learning fast and accurate absolute pitch judgment in adulthood","stance":"rec","why":"Musician's summary of the problems of perfect pitch fading with age.","quote":"I think this video gives a nice summary of all this from the point of view of a musician","text":"Maybe someone can correct me, but I don't think this is absolute pitch. It is pseudo-perfect pitch, based on pitch memory, and it was already known that it can be trained. As an amateur musician myself, I understand the desire to have perfect pitch, but it seems that the problem of perfect pitch is seldom mentioned. Usually, people talk about the common annoyances, such as transposed music, non-standard tuning, choruses that drift in pitch, etc... but the actual hard one is that it fades away with age. First, it starts \"shifting,\" and people will start to believe that a note is actually a semitone higher or lower than it actually is, and then eventually, it is completely lost. There is research that indicates that this is very common, and people with perfect pitch are more likely to lose it than to keep it. This is a huge blow—imagine a whole life relying on this one skill to support all your music-related activities, and suddenly, it's completely gone. I think this video gives a nice summary of all this from the point of view of a musician: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36430064,"author":"eskaytwo","month":"2023-06","story":"How My Children (N=2) Acquired Absolute Pitch","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses perfect pitch as cognitive rather than ear physiology.","quote":"Adam Neely had a good YouTube video on it","text":"It seems more cognitive than due to physical effects in the ear. Adam Neely had a good YouTube video on it [VIDEO]"},{"id":35757435,"author":"2pEXgD0fZ5cF","month":"2023-04","story":"Children aged 2-6 successfully trained to acquire absolute pitch","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting look at the disadvantages of perfect pitch.","quote":"interesting video on that topic by Adam Neely","text":"> For example singers, musicians playing bowed stringed instruments or a trombone benefit from perfect pitch. The benefit is rather small if you look at ear training for professional musicians and what properly trained relative pitch looks like. It basically doesn't matter anymore as soon as a professional musician holds his/her instrument. In short: There is basically no difference anymore as soon as the trained musician without perfect pitch gets provided 1 reference tone (which is why perfect pitch gets commonly attributed to people that do not have it). It is more like a \"shortcut\" when it comes to ear training, but ultimately a professional musician with perfect pitch and a professional musician without pefect pitch arrive at basically the same destination in practice, making the advantage minimal when it comes to the things a musician actually does. Rick Beato has a certain kind of obsession with the topic that makes it seem so much more important than it actually is in the real world. Also noteworthy that there are disadvantages to perfect pitch (transposing instruments, losing it with age, etc.), and interesting video on that topic by Adam Neely [1] was already linked. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28371935,"author":"leoc","month":"2021-08","story":"Perfect pitch study: Why can’t we identify music notes as well as colors?","stance":"rec","why":"Covers age-related decline of perfect pitch and perfect pitch generally.","quote":"Adam Neely also has a video about age-related degradation of perfect pitch and about perfect pitch in general","text":"Adam Neely also has a video about age-related degradation of perfect pitch and about perfect pitch in general: [VIDEO] . Relative pitch is certainly learnable."},{"id":28372235,"author":"mkr-hn","month":"2021-08","story":"Perfect pitch study: Why can’t we identify music notes as well as colors?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains drawbacks of perfect pitch versus relative pitch.","quote":"It has other drawbacks. Video on the subject","text":"The good news for people without perfect pitch who feel bad about it is that people who learn relative pitch retain it for life while perfect pitch declines precipitously with age. It has other drawbacks. Video on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36424185,"author":"KnobbleMcKnees","month":"2023-06","story":"How My Children (N=2) Acquired Absolute Pitch","stance":"reference","why":"Shows perfect pitch can drift over time.","quote":"It can drift over time so that even if you're in the right key","text":"It gets worse than that. It can drift over time so that even if you're in the right key, you end up as much as a half step out of tune. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35756554,"author":"tylerhou","month":"2023-04","story":"Children aged 2-6 successfully trained to acquire absolute pitch","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates the downsides of perfect pitch.","quote":"","text":"There are some downsides of perfect pitch. For one, choral singers with perfect pitch sometimes have a hard time re-tuning if the rest of the choir shifts pitch. (This can happen naturally over a long piece that has no accompaniment.) Another downside is that almost all people with perfect pitch lose it when they hit a certain age. Imagine being an accomplished painter. One day, you wake up and see leaves as blue-ish green instead of green. It might be difficult to adjust to no longer being able to see the world in color. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ieb1lmm9xHk",1],["fWqBmmPQP40",1]]},"rJjKP8vYjpQ":{"wy":"HN points to it as the source and best distillation of 'fuck you money', in threads about FIRE, windfalls, layoffs and financial literacy.","q":{"x":"this scene really distills a lot of good stuff into a few minutes","i":34532478,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35689599,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2023-04","story":"Red Hat cutting hundreds of jobs, CEO says in letter to employees","stance":"rec","why":"Rant on mitigating effects of job loss through financial cushion","quote":"A great take on this can be mined from this little rant by John Goodman's character in The Gambler","text":"Anyone have tips to avoid this? Avoid? No. It probably can't be avoided in the long-run. But you can mitigate the effects. A great take on this can be mined from this little rant by John Goodman's character in The Gambler : [VIDEO]"},{"id":34532478,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2023-01","story":"Ask HN: Good resources to become financially literate","stance":"rec","why":"Scene distilling financial wisdom into a few minutes","quote":"this scene really distills a lot of good stuff into a few minutes","text":"You might accuse me of being glib in saying this, but this scene from the movie The Gambler[1] contains a lot of wisdom. The movie as a whole is moderately OK, but this scene really distills a lot of good stuff into a few minutes. [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":29606714,"author":"dharmab","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: 27, accidentally became wealthy, lost drive. What should I do?","stance":"rec","why":"Clip on building a stable base before riskier ventures","quote":"setting up a Position of Fuck You (a stable base from which you can launch riskier endeavours)","text":"Lots of great answers in this thread about finding meaning after your base needs are taken care of. I'd like also include this clip about setting up a Position of Fuck You (a stable base from which you can launch riskier endeavours) [VIDEO]"},{"id":18610519,"author":"mindcrime","month":"2018-12","story":"Ask HN: What to do after $8M (all cash, post tax) exit?","stance":"rec","why":"Movie scene on building a position of financial independence","quote":"The very first thing I'd do is watch this video","text":"First of all, congrats. You are in a great situation, now you just have to manage it to allow you to live your life the way you want from here on out. The very first thing I'd do is watch this video (it's a famous scene from the movie The Gambler ). [VIDEO] The basic idea is to establish your \"position of 'Fuck You'\". You basically have that already... house paid for, cars paid for, etc. Put enough of your money aside in conservative, safe, long-term investments that you'll never be hurting for money. Include things like your kids college tuition or whatever things are important to you. Once you have that, even if you blow the rest of the money on toys, sex, drugs, rock 'n roll, whatever, you always have the ability to say \"fuck you\" to anybody who hassles you. Beyond that, you can decide if you want to try and grow your wealth by investing and actively managing your money. Maybe you would want to do some angel investing, who knows? Or maybe you want to engage in some philanthropy. You probably don't have enough money to get a hospital named after you, but you could buy uniforms for the local high-school band group, or donate to a local volunteer fire department, or any number of things. Should you continue to work? It's up to you... my guess is that if you flat-out retire at your age you'll find yourself bored pretty quickly, but it depends on what kinds of things you choose to engage in. Maybe there are some hobbies that have been latent in the back of your mind, that you w"},{"id":33978616,"author":"travisjungroth","month":"2022-12","story":"After 20 years the Dwarf Fortress devs have to get used to being millionaires","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates what fuck you money really means","quote":"It's about having enough that you're not beholden to anyone for your basic needs.","text":"> 2m each is not \"Fuck you\" money perhaps but it is enough to comfortably retire That is exactly what \"fuck you\" money means. It's not about having so much money you couldn't possibly want for anything. People will always want. It's about having enough that you're not beholden to anyone for your basic needs. [VIDEO]"},{"id":26546296,"author":"tick_tock_tick","month":"2021-03","story":"Early-Retirement Update","stance":"reference","why":"Source of the fuck you money phrase","quote":"","text":"\"independently wealthy\" is the normal word choice. Or \"fuck you money\" from the gambler [VIDEO]"},{"id":20522046,"author":"bfieidhbrjr","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: What did you do when you suddenly got rich?","stance":"reference","why":"Movie monologue on financial independence as freedom from obligation","quote":"That puts you, for the rest of your life, at a level of f you.","text":"[VIDEO] Frank: You get up two and a half million dollars, any asshole in the world knows what to do: you get a house with a 25 year roof, an indestructible Jap-economy shitbox, you put the rest into the system at three to five percent to pay your taxes and that's your base, get me? That's your fortress of f ing solitude. That puts you, for the rest of your life, at a level of f you. Somebody wants you to do something, f * you. Boss pisses you off, f * you! Own your house. Have a couple bucks in the bank. Don't drink. That's all I have to say to anybody on any social level. Did your grandfather take risks? Jim Bennett: Yes. Frank: I guarantee he did it from a position of f * you. A wise man's life is based around f * you. The United States of America is based on f * you. You have a navy? Greatest army in the history of mankind? F * you! Blow me. We'll f * it up ourselves."},{"id":19804307,"author":"gubsz","month":"2019-05","story":"Ask HN: What do people think of FIRE (Financial Independence, Retire Early)?","stance":"reference","why":"Scene capturing motivation behind financial independence","quote":"My interest in FIRE can really be boiled down to this scene","text":"My interest in FIRE can really be boiled down to this scene: [VIDEO] I'm on board and actively pursing FIRE. I'm not sure if I'll actually choose to retire when I hit the magic number (~20x your yearly expenses), but it does provide me with a sense of satisfaction and stability. I think a lot of the FIRE community over evangelizes the \"RE\" portion of FIRE, but that may just be a reflection of the high stress/high pay types of jobs that many FIRE pursuers find themselves in."},{"id":28573089,"author":"mathgladiator","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: What is your strongest contrarian opinion?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Holy shit, that might be right. While I believe it was a disaster, it was definitely American spirit. This made me think about: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["c2J91UG6Fn8",1],["meHYBhcpdvQ",1],["k1kndKWJKB8",1],["DlTq8DbRs4k",1],["N2jZryt607U",1],["usSfgHGEGxQ",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["q7K0vRUKXKc",1],["LNjJTgXujno",1],["ZK3O402wf1c",1],["aB8zcAttP1E",1],["1e8VZlPBx_0",1],["id1WShzzMCQ",1],["ZTidn2dBYbY",1],["3wMKoSRbGVs",1],["fNk_zzaMoSs",1]]},"wASCH_gPnDw":{"wy":"HN commenters call it a fantastic introduction to Clojure and the thinking behind its design.","q":{"x":"It's probably the best technical video I've ever watched on YouTube.","i":7699478,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":18366337,"author":"nocman","month":"2018-11","story":"Minesweeper in Clojure","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rich Hickey interview worth rewatching","quote":"both of those are worth watching multiple times","text":"Um, destructuring is a very old concept in Lisp circles. [LINK] Don't know if Rich first got the idea from Lisp or not, but it seems likely since he did a bunch of development in Common Lisp (generating C++, of all things :-D ) ages ago. I don't remember if I heard him talk about that in the \"Expert to Expert\" video ( [VIDEO] ), or in \"Simple Made Easy\" ( [LINK] ). I think it was the former, but both of those are worth watching multiple times. It's also entirely possible that it was in an entirely different video of his -- but it was him . I've yet to be disappointed in any of his talks, so if I'm wrong about the exact one, just keep watching any of his talks on YouTube till you find it. xD"},{"id":7699478,"author":"pk2200","month":"2014-05","story":"Why I am excited about Clojure","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hour-long Rich Hickey interview, fantastic intro to Clojure and its design goals","quote":"It's probably the best technical video I've ever watched on YouTube.","text":"I'd like to learn Clojure. Any suggestions on how to get started? It's my first time using a Lisp dialect. I figured I'd tackle the Clojure Koans, and mix in a few Project Euler problems. I'd also like to do some code reading. I know the Clojure source code is on GitHub, but maybe something smaller to start? If anyone else is interested in getting started, here are a few links I visited over the weekend: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] The last link is an hour-long interview with Rich Hickey - a fantastic introduction to the language and his design goals. It's probably the best technical video I've ever watched on YouTube."},{"id":42663904,"author":"knubie","month":"2025-01","story":"Why we built Vade Studio in Clojure","stance":"rec","why":"Rich explains Clojure reference types and atoms","quote":"Rich does an excellent job explaining them in this video","text":"> What I don’t understand the advantage for “state management”. Like, you build a new state object, and then mutate some pointer from prevState to nextState… that’s what everyone else is doing too. Clojure's real super power is its reference type(s) (in particular the atom). Rich does an excellent job explaining them in this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33466617,"author":"hxegon","month":"2022-11","story":"Clojure from a Schemer's perspective (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Explains how Clojure's underlying data structures work","quote":"The actual details of how the underlying datastructures work isn't that hard to grasp","text":"The actual details of how the underlying datastructures work isn't that hard to grasp (see: [VIDEO] ), but in practice you don't even need to go that far. At a high level there's really just a few things you need to keep in mind, the biggest being the big O guarantees of the core DS interfaces, which are listed here [LINK] . Those guarantees hold across the different underlying implementations, and there are plenty of things you can tune to get a 2x, 10x, 50x improvement in performance before resorting to things that depend on which specific DS is being used under the hood Edit: Fixed timestamp for video link"},{"id":14954491,"author":"chipset","month":"2017-08","story":"Ferret – A free software Clojure implementation","stance":"reference","why":"Hickey on persistent data structures","quote":"","text":"I'm not sure how it's implemented in Ferret but Clojure's data structures are implemented as trees so there's structural sharing that happens when a vector or hash map is updated or when a new version is produced. So the whole data structure isn't copied but only the relevant parts are added / updated in the tree and the rest is shared with the \"new\" data structure. Mutable data structures are still more memory effective but I'm assuming its fine since the author has a blog post on using ferret on an Arduino Uno which has 32k of ram. [LINK] Clojure data Sturucture links: Persistent Vectors [LINK] Video with Rick hick on them [VIDEO]"}]},"SIOQgY1tqrU":{"wy":"HN recommends it, and the whole course, for understanding the brain and language acquisition.","q":{"x":"If you want to understand better how the brain works, I really recommend watching the whole course.","i":18259909,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":39120099,"author":"i2shar","month":"2024-01","story":"Gene therapy allows an 11-year-old boy to hear","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Sapolsky on language acquisition and the brain","quote":"I highly recommend Sapolksy’s lecture on this","text":"This is well known and well studied. I highly recommend Sapolksy’s lecture on this: [VIDEO] Start at 12:14 for the relevant topic, but the entire lecture is a good watch."},{"id":18259909,"author":"SlySherZ","month":"2018-10","story":"I woke up unable to speak English","stance":"rec","why":"Brain encoding of second languages; whole course recommended","quote":"If you want to understand better how the brain works, I really recommend watching the whole course.","text":"If I remember correctly, when you learn a new language after age 10, the brain encodes the new language in a region adjacent to the one where your mother language is encoded, which means that any localized injury on this part of the brain may impact your ability to speak just one of them. Source: Sapolsky's lecture about language: [VIDEO] If you want to understand better how the brain works, I really recommend watching the whole course."},{"id":10063622,"author":"nerfhammer","month":"2015-08","story":"Apes may be closer to speaking than many scientists think","stance":"rec","why":"Sapolsky discusses ape language projects, Koko and Kanzi","quote":"is a good watch","text":"Robert Sapolsky's lecture, mentioned in the article, wherein he discusses ape language projects, is a good watch: [VIDEO] He completely dismisses Koko but considers Kanzi to be more credible."},{"id":11006129,"author":"belorn","month":"2016-01","story":"Lilli, the German Adult-Themed Cartoon Character Who Became the Barbie Doll","stance":"reference","why":"Motherese teaching language and expressing emotion","quote":"he talks about motherese being both about teaching language and expressing emotion.","text":"On the recorded lectures from Robert Sapolsky, he talks about motherese being both about teaching language and expressing emotion. The aspect of language is absent when people \"talk\" to their pets. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NNnIGh9g6fA",1],["jzMFoNZeZm0",1]]},"lsDc1YVxHA0":{"wy":"Recommended as an entertaining, hilarious view of developers watching a speedrunner break their game.","q":{"x":"They had a speedrunner running their game in front of the development team which is hilarious.","i":20337751,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":20337751,"author":"franknine","month":"2019-07","story":"I thought I made a hard game and then speedrunners destroyed it","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Speedrunner plays Psychonauts while the dev team watches, hilarious.","quote":"They had a speedrunner running their game in front of the development team which is hilarious.","text":"For people who like this post, I highly recommend \"Devs Play\" S1E06 - Psychonauts \"The SMK Speedrun\" by Double Fine Production: [VIDEO] They had a speedrunner running their game in front of the development team which is hilarious."},{"id":31104906,"author":"8K832d7tNmiQ","month":"2022-04","story":"Overhauling Mario 64's code to reach 30 FPS and render 6x faster on N64 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Speedrunner explains every exploit used to get fastest time to devs.","quote":"Psychonaut's dev reaction is my favorite one","text":"Psychonaut's dev reaction is my favorite one because they had one-to-one with a speedrunner talking about every game exploits used to get the fastest time possible.[1] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22628267,"author":"radnor","month":"2020-03","story":"Why Adventure Games Suck (1989)","stance":"rec","why":"Speedrunner plays Psychonauts with entire dev team watching.","quote":"I found it entertaining and much more fun than trying to play it myself.","text":"You might be interested in watching this speedrunner go through Psychonauts while the entire dev team watches. I found it entertaining and much more fun than trying to play it myself. :) [VIDEO]"}]},"sPFmKu2S5XY":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it to people with ADHD for helping them understand their behavior and how their brain works.","q":{"x":"Watching that video helped me put my behaviors in perspective and really understand how my brain works.","i":24713025,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24713025,"author":"sosodev","month":"2020-10","story":"Ask HN: How to deal with ADHD on your own? (If you have to)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Helped put ADHD behaviors in perspective; notebook advice.","quote":"Watching that video helped me put my behaviors in perspective and really understand how my brain works.","text":"ADHD teens tend to be rather oppositional. Personally, I always had a really hard time accepting unsolicited advice from anybody and that includes loved ones. The best thing somebody with ADHD can do is externalize everything they want/need to remember. Preferably in something centralized and always accessible like a notebook or planner app. Then make it a routine to read through and take care of the stuff in it daily. [VIDEO] Watching that video helped me put my behaviors in perspective and really understand how my brain works. He even says in the video that “chaining a notebook to yourself” is the best thing you can do if you have ADHD. If your son believes that he has ADHD that video, or others like it, might help him understand what that means and he’ll probably start to draw the correct conclusions. I wish you and your son nothing but the best. Growing up with undiagnosed ADHD was both the best and worst experience of my life. The highs are high and lows are so unfortunately low."},{"id":33493821,"author":"sadhd","month":"2022-11","story":"Launch HN: Shimmer (YC S21) – ADHD coaching for adults","stance":"rec","why":"Barkley's explanation matches physiological psychology of executive function.","quote":"His explanation lines up with the physiological psychology that I have learned.","text":"Have you watched Dr. Russell Barkleys series? [VIDEO] . I have actually done professional research into executive function and working memory--not a PhD but have one masters and nearly through another. His explanation lines up with the physiological psychology that I have learned."},{"id":31391171,"author":"estevaoam","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: Senior Engineers with ADHD, any mental/physical structures for work?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains ADHD in more detail than the tips given.","quote":"If you haven’t seen this video yet, it explains better and in much more detail","text":"I was diagnosed with somewhat severe ADHD, hyperactive and attention subtype. I have few tips to share: 1. Find your medication. After I found mine with the correct dosage, my life improved ten fold. Our dopamine system is wired differently and medication is the basis of treatment to cope with it. 2. Externalize everything: thoughts, events, planning, even your life’s values if you feel like it. Write it all down and build the discipline to keep those updated. Use todo-lists for everything. The poor executive functions we experience can be compensated by externalizing everything, so always organize your thoughts in a notepad. I use NotePlan mac/iOS app and highly recommend to check it out. 3. A lesser commonly known part of being ADHD is that it can also cause emotional unbalance. We can get overwhelmed, overexcited and overanxious easily. Keep awareness of your emotions. Again, write them down. 4. SLOW DOWN. Especially if you are also hyperactive. Try to think and react more slowly to the world around you. Give time for things to sink in your head before taking action. 5. Last but not least: be kind to yourself. You can’t always do everything perfectly. Mistakes are going to happen and that’s normal for everyone. Focus on improving yourself a little everyday and enjoy the present. If you haven’t seen this video yet, it explains better and in much more detail: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22811482,"author":"Ezku","month":"2020-04","story":"Picture This? Some Just Can’t (2015)","stance":"reference","why":"Barkley links Vygotsky's theories to ADHD executive function.","quote":"ADHD researcher Dr Russell Barkley links Vygotsky’s theories of childhood development to the mechanism by which ADHD interferes with executive function","text":"I have a hypothesis that this condition and ADHD are related. ADHD researcher Dr Russell Barkley links Vygotsky’s theories of childhood development to the mechanism by which ADHD interferes with executive function [0]. An ADHD brain would be less capable of imagining things and holding those images, which would lead to failure in development of self-directed action via those imagined images. Is this ”aphantasia”? I’m not sure, but I find it plausible. The prevalence for ADHD has been cited at 5% in children and half of that in adults. A commenter here says aphantasia has been guessed to be at 1-3% [1], which would be a rough match. Another commenter describes their ADHD and aphantasia [2], and I am under the impression this has been reported often. If there was a causal relation I would expect to see these kinds of overlaps in the data, but it’s really the researcher’s description of a failure of imagination that makes this hypothesis especially interesting to me. What do you think? Has anyone looked into this? [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"}]},"whb4unrhy44":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a well-done critique of the documentary, sometimes in preference to watching the film itself.","q":{"x":"I would recommend people not to watch it and instead watch this well done critique (and criticism)","i":32806378,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32806378,"author":"AnonC","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best documentaries you've seen?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well-done critique recommended over watching the documentary itself","quote":"I would recommend people not to watch it and instead watch this well done critique (and criticism)","text":"I would recommend people not to watch it and instead watch this well done critique (and criticism) titled “My Octopus Teacher & Environmental Horror” by Maggie Mae. [1] I could’ve spent my time better if I’d watched this and avoided “My Octopus Teacher” altogether. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30016575,"author":"shard","month":"2022-01","story":"The mystery of social behavior in octopuses","stance":"rec","why":"Critique of My Octopus Teacher as self-centered projection","quote":"Here is a critique of My Octopus Teacher as a movie of a self-centered man who projects his issues onto an unsuspecting wild animal","text":"Here is a critique of My Octopus Teacher as a movie of a self-centered man who projects his issues onto an unsuspecting wild animal: \"MY OCTOPUS TEACHER & Environmental Horror, An Analysis\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":27397701,"author":"whymauri","month":"2021-06","story":"Heroic rat retiring from detecting land mines","stance":"rec","why":"Eco-horror breakdown of My Octopus Teacher exploring language themes","quote":"Maggie Mae's breakdown of My Octopus Teacher as eco-horror film","text":"Love this analysis. It reminds me of Maggie Mae's breakdown of My Octopus Teacher as eco-horror film -- it ends up exploring the same themes around language and communication that you brought up here. [0] [0] [VIDEO] Specific time stamp for section on language (Gorillas in the Congo): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kH5APw_SLUU",1]]},"FjA0EQPeUGM":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a fascinating, Emmy-winning look at US chemical weapons stockpiles and their dangers.","q":{"x":"If you're interested in this type of stuff this documentary is incredible and apparently won an Emmy.","i":22672317,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22672317,"author":"xzel","month":"2020-03","story":"Why Don’t We Use Chemical Weapons Anymore?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Emmy-winning documentary on US nerve gas storage and its long-term hazards","quote":"If you're interested in this type of stuff this documentary is incredible and apparently won an Emmy.","text":"I stumbled onto this news documentary on the nerve gas stored in the US from 1973 very randomly maybe a year ago: [VIDEO] . If you're interested in this type of stuff this documentary is incredible and apparently won an Emmy. The number of problems with long term are mind boggling technologically, even if people don't know its near them. The whole idea if this place got bombed it would wipe out huge amount of people in the surrounding area based on wind patterns is nuts, frankly. The whole process to dismantle and then \"store\" the old gas is insane."},{"id":13731438,"author":"scrollaway","month":"2017-02","story":"Kim Jong-nam killing: Suspect 'was paid $90 for baby oil prank'","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fascinating 30-minute 80s documentary on nerve gas","quote":"This really fascinating documentary from the 80s about nerve gas","text":"This really fascinating documentary from the 80s about nerve gas was linked in yesterday's news posts: [VIDEO] Only tangentially related but I really recommend watching. It's only 30 minutes."},{"id":15976221,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-12","story":"US Federal Ban on Making Lethal Viruses Is Lifted","stance":"reference","why":"Documentary on US nerve gas stockpile sites","quote":"Here is the original documentary I watched.","text":"Here is the original documentary I watched. There is more information on the individual sites on more recent news and magazine sites, often referenced on wikipedia: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13811335,"author":"djsumdog","month":"2017-03","story":"CIA malware and hacking tools","stance":"reference","why":"US chemical weapons stockpiles and leaking storage","quote":"chemical weapons were stored in massive US facilities and many of them have started leaking over the years","text":"The US chemical weapons program is downright frightening. Unlike these exploits which you can just leave in an office and never use (and which con subsequently go stale as people find and patch exploits), chemical weapons were stored in massive US facilities and many of them have started leaking over the years: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["ZZ3F3zWiEmc",1],["wOlcB-JxkFw",1],["ytpRnRke6I0",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["9zinZmE3Ogk",1],["7J52mDjZzto",1],["Pae2AMnmUVA",1],["rwhUpu9MfZ0",1],["7NfX0vlCa4k",1],["PQccNdwm8Tw",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["90NsjKvz9Ns",1],["NVGuFdX5guE",1],["nSKp2StlS6s",1],["7Pq-S557XQU",1]]},"HStF1RJOyxI":{"wy":"HN points to it as the best explanation of why R is so hard to optimize, often paired with talks on Python and used to explain what makes Julia fast.","q":{"x":"Another talk on the difficulty of optimising R is this fantastic one by Jan Vitek","i":14801954,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":18193812,"author":"lottin","month":"2018-10","story":"Faster R with FastR","stance":"rec","why":"Why R will probably never run fast.","quote":"I recommend watching this video - Making R run fast","text":"I recommend watching this video - Making R run fast [VIDEO] It's a little disappointing, because the conclusion is that R will probably never \"run fast\", but very interesting nonetheless."},{"id":17210160,"author":"StefanKarpinski","month":"2018-06","story":"My favorite things that are coming with Julia 1.0","stance":"rec","why":"What makes R and Python hard to optimize.","quote":"Perhaps the best way to understand what makes Julia fast is to watch these two videos about Python and R","text":"Perhaps the best way to understand what makes Julia fast is to watch these two videos about Python and R and what makes them so hard to optimize: [LINK] [VIDEO] Take everything mentioned in these videos that make Python and R really hard to optimize and don't do those things :D"},{"id":14801954,"author":"simonbyrne","month":"2017-07","story":"Back to the Future: Lisp as a Base for a Statistical Computing System [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Why R is hard to optimize.","quote":"Another talk on the difficulty of optimising R is this fantastic one by Jan Vitek","text":"Another talk on the difficulty of optimising R is this fantastic one by Jan Vitek: [VIDEO] Also, my favourite R trick: foo <- function() {rm(foo, envir=environment(foo))}"},{"id":34896699,"author":"ChrisRackauckas","month":"2023-02","story":"ML would have advanced faster if another front-end language had been adopted?","stance":"reference","why":"Difficulty of writing a JIT compiler for R.","quote":"Jan Vitek for example has a great talk which describes how difficult it is to write a JIT compiler for R","text":"This is disregarding the development of said ecosystems though. The point is that Python has been quite inhibitory to the development of this ecosystem. There are many corpses of automatic differentiation libraries (starting from autograd and tangent and then to things like theano to finally tensorflow and pytorch) and many corpses of JIT compilers and accelerators (Cython, Numba, pypy, and TensorFlow XLA, now PyTorch v2's JIT, etc.). What has been found over the last decade is that a large part of that is due to the design of the languages. Jan Vitek for example has a great talk which describes how difficult it is to write a JIT compiler for R due to certain design choices in the language ( [LINK] , or the more detailed version [VIDEO] ). There are certain language constructs that void lots of optimizations which have to then be worked around, which is why Python JITs choose subsets of the language to avoid specific parts that are not easy to optimize or not possible to optimize. This is why each take a domain-specific subset, a different subset of the language for numba vs jax vs etc., to choose something that is nice for ML vs for more generic codes. With all of that, it's perfectly reasonable to point out that there have been languages which have been designed to not have the compilation difficulties, which have resulted having a single (JIT) compiler for the language. And by extension, it has made building machine learning and autodiff libraries not something that's a Go"}],"r":[["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["kc9HwsxE1OY",1],["YdiZa0Y3F3c",1]]},"MgrO5za0lSY":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a well-made, interesting and sad documentary on Bacon's troubled life.","q":{"x":"Pretty interesting and sad at the same time. It's worth a watch, IMO.","i":27264377,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27264377,"author":"throwaway29303","month":"2021-05","story":"Francis Bacon’s Frightening Beauty","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting and sad documentary about Bacon","quote":"Pretty interesting and sad at the same time. It's worth a watch, IMO.","text":"I had this morbid curiosity about Bacon's work and life a long while ago and I happened to find a YouTube documentary[0] about him. Pretty interesting and sad at the same time. It's worth a watch, IMO. [0] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":26432767,"author":"Gatsky","month":"2021-03","story":"A history of the art market in 35 record-breaking sales (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Good documentary on Bacon's life","quote":"There is a good doco about Bacon's life on YT","text":"Huh, Fancis Bacon's Three Studies of Lucian Freud is the most expensive on the list at $89 million. I can't comprehend these dollar numbers, but it's a stunning painting. There is a good doco about Bacon's life on YT: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26296933,"author":"pantelisk","month":"2021-02","story":"Francis Bacon: Revelations review – a landmark biography","stance":"rec","why":"Well-made BBC documentary on Bacon's troubled life","quote":"here's a very well done documentary on Francis by BBC","text":"The art style of Francis Bacon is fantastic, but the troubled life he led is maybe even more intriguing, mysterious and dark. For those curious, here's a very well done documentary on Francis by BBC - [VIDEO] (warning, graphic themes)"}],"r":[["D9H9qTdserM",1],["KxGRhd_iWuE",1]]},"OHPkdMGI6D4":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it in debates about practical effects versus CGI, as a fun comparison of old and new Dune.","q":{"x":"a really good example of this is this comparison between the old and new Dune movies","i":29223473,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":37356295,"author":"tedunangst","month":"2023-09","story":"The Practical Effects Wizardry of Dungeons and Dragons: Honor Among Thieves","stance":"rec","why":"How effects in old and new Dune were done","quote":"I like corridor crew on YouTube.","text":"If you like this, I like corridor crew on YouTube. Here's one discussing how effects in Dune, old and new, were done, some of which were pretty surprising. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30180661,"author":"michaelbuckbee","month":"2022-02","story":"Over-reliance on CGI in movies","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how the original Dune ship landing was filmed","quote":"For an insane example of this checkout this video of how they did the ship landing in the original Dune movie","text":"So much of this is just how _much_ work had to be done to get practical effects to work, and how practical and CGI are often good for different things. For an insane example of this checkout this video of how they did the ship landing in the original Dune movie (of which step 1 was \"rent a stadium\"). [VIDEO] Also, I think there's a very selective bias against CGI where when it looks _real_ we just assume it's real and don't notice so we're only criticizing the portion where it failed to live up to reality."},{"id":30182394,"author":"shagie","month":"2022-02","story":"Over-reliance on CGI in movies","stance":"rec","why":"Corridor Crew's take on both Dune versions","quote":"Another series that I've enjoyed watching is the Corridor Crew.","text":"Another series that I've enjoyed watching is the Corridor Crew. Their take on Dune (both versions) is [VIDEO]"},{"id":29223473,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2021-11","story":"The bullet effects in Terminator 2 weren’t CGI","stance":"rec","why":"Compares old and new Dune effects","quote":"a really good example of this is this comparison between the old and new Dune movies","text":"I think it really just comes down to the skill of the film makers, as it always has. I think a really good example of this is this comparison between the old and new Dune movies: [VIDEO] The new Dune is masterfully crafted, and I was never once bothered by the CG, even though there was clearly a ton of it. The old Dune, on the other hand...I don't think it's aged particularly gracefully. Then again, I've never been a huge Lynch fan."}],"r":[["4PaWFYm0kEw",1],["CbIZU8cQWXc",1],["yiw6_JakZFc",1],["9RUHJhskW00",1],["jPhJbKBuNnA",1],["_gXlauRB1EQ",1],["klaJqofCsu4",1],["LxgMdjyw8uw",1],["rvskMHn0sqQ",1],["TwRjO3kHxU4",1],["XVmsfL5LG90",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["hyCIpKAIFyo",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["tafGL02EUOA",1],["vTMF6xEiAaY",1],["fD-R35DSSZY",1],["1YFrISfN7jo",1],["bLk1cjZ4ll0",1]]},"TPg4Vcr56F0":{"wy":"HN points to it in threads on black MIDI and player pianos as a look at an artist writing tuneful music beyond human playability.","q":{"x":"He talks about it quite in a bit in a Pitchfork TV video that documented him working on one of his albums, Bromst","i":18992984,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":18992984,"author":"pimlottc","month":"2019-01","story":"Una Corda, a piano with one string per note","stance":"rec","why":"Pitchfork documentary of Dan Deacon making Bromst with MIDI pianos","quote":"He talks about it quite in a bit in a Pitchfork TV video that documented him working on one of his albums, Bromst","text":"+1 for Olafur Another artist who has used a specially-crafted MIDI-controlled piano is Dan Deacon. His stuff is not exactly New Classical, though, it's a bit more frenetic and goofy, but quite good. He talks about it quite in a bit in a Pitchfork TV video that documented him working on one of his albums, Bromst: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10549410,"author":"sprokolopolis","month":"2015-11","story":"The Impossible Music of Black MIDI","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary on recording Bromst with MIDI-driven player pianos","quote":"Here is a link to a documentary about the recording of the album linked to a visual of the pianos playing","text":"A normally electronic musician, Dan Deacon, has used some very complex player piano arrangements. When he recorded his album Bromst, they set up multiple MIDI player pianos to play the midi arrangements. What I found very interesting is that the density of the notes actually builds up to a very full and soothing (to me) sound, when played on the analogue instruments rather than on a computer. This is an album I play when driving through the woods or staring at the stars. There is texture to be heard from all of the hammering keys, but the notes all combine to a smoother and very meditative, calming, uplifting sound. I feel like like that fullness of sound from the analogue instrument allows the listener to here the overarching melodies, rather than focus on how many notes are being played. Here is a link to a documentary about the recording of the album linked to a visual of the pianos playing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6642193,"author":"hemmer","month":"2013-10","story":"The Impossible Music of Black MIDI","stance":"rec","why":"Dan Deacon recording tuneful pieces for MIDI player pianos","quote":"I would suggest Dan Deacon for someone who writes \"unplayable\" yet very tuneful pieces","text":"I would suggest Dan Deacon for someone who writes \"unplayable\" yet very tuneful pieces: eg. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["gLDYtH1RH-U",1],["Bm7L-2J52GU",1],["qoyKe-HxmFk",1]]},"Z20XNp-luNA":{"wy":"HN people cite it to show even technical users fall for phishing and that hardware security keys are the real fix.","q":{"x":"shows just how easy it is to phish even users who think they are too good to be fooled.","i":20045631,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":45184800,"author":"x0x0","month":"2025-09","story":"We all dodged a bullet","stance":"rec","why":"Concludes only yubikeys stop credential phishing.","quote":"Her conclusion, in a really well-done talk, was that it was impossible.","text":"I watched a presentation from Stripe internal eng that was given I forget where. An internal engineer there who did a bunch of security work phished like half of her own company (testing, obviously). Her conclusion, in a really well-done talk, was that it was impossible. No human measures will reduce it given her success at a very disciplined, highly security conscious place. The only thing that works is yubikeys which prevent this type of credential + 2fa theft phishing attack. edit: karla burnette / talk [VIDEO]"},{"id":27384463,"author":"procinct","month":"2021-06","story":"Can Apple change ads?","stance":"rec","why":"How Stripe trains staff against phishing; technical people still click.","quote":"There’s a great talk at Black Hat about how Stripe trains staff against these sorts of phishing campaigns","text":"Maybe if you’re referring to regular phishing sure, but spear phishing is specifically designed so it won’t go to your junk email and will look like something made for them personally, there won’t be any spelling mistakes, it will have been sent from a likely email source, it will most likely contain some personal information. The right phish at the right time could catch just about anyone out. There’s a great talk at Black Hat about how Stripe trains staff against these sorts of phishing campaigns and these are highly technical people and they still click the links. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20045631,"author":"dmbaggett","month":"2019-05","story":"Should Failing Phish Tests Be a Fireable Offense?","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how easily even sophisticated users get phished.","quote":"shows just how easy it is to phish even users who think they are too good to be fooled.","text":"Phishing is frankly an embarrassment for the mainstream security community. The temptation is to \"blame the stupid users\" -- but the truth is that even a script kiddie can take a real email from a mainstream brand, \"Save As HTML...\", change one link, and resend... and snare even sophisticated victims. This BlackHat talk ( [VIDEO] ) shows just how easy it is to phish even users who think they are too good to be fooled. At Inky ( [LINK] ) we're using a combination of computer vision, anomaly detection, and domain-specific hacks to identify zero-day phishing emails \"from first principles\" (as I like to say). And it works! But the pushback from the security establishment is impressive. I like to say that there are two widely-held but false beliefs about phishing: 1) phishing is solved; 2) phishing is unsolvable. The truth is that we can already see clearly that within 3-5 years machines will be good enough at identifying phishing emails that attackers will move to another vector... but you'd never know it listening to \"Security Thought Leaders.\""},{"id":17594026,"author":"SpaethCo","month":"2018-07","story":"Google: Security Keys Neutralized Employee Phishing","stance":"reference","why":"Stripe engineer's phishing campaigns fooled users into pasting password manager credentials.","quote":"she had phishing campaigns that had users ignore that autofill didn't work","text":"This is where it comes down to user behavior. One of the security engineers from Stripe gave a talk about this at Blackhat last year -- she had phishing campaigns that had users ignore that autofill didn't work and manually copied/pasted their password manager credentials into the phishing sites. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uL2D3qzHtqY",1]]},"r9rGX91rq5I":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a short, clear illustration of how first-past-the-post misrepresents voters.","q":{"x":"Great CGP Grey video on the 2015 General Election in the UK","i":31145414,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31145414,"author":"dmitriid","month":"2022-04","story":"UK-Morocco 10GW Renewable Electricity Interconnector Planned","stance":"rec","why":"2015 UK election showing disproportionate seat allocation","quote":"Great CGP Grey video on the 2015 General Election in the UK","text":"> creepy undemocratic bureaucracy of the EU > need for control and action on a local level Ah you mean that local control and democracy when the party with 13% of the votes gets 0.2% seats in the parliament\", right? [1] [1] Great CGP Grey video on the 2015 General Election in the UK: [VIDEO]"},{"id":19776936,"author":"NeedMoreTea","month":"2019-04","story":"Does the UK Think It Can Police the Internet?","stance":"rec","why":"Summarises problems of first past the post","quote":"CGP Grey summarises it well","text":"> suggests a lack of representation That's a big can of worms for HN. Suffice to say we have one of the least representative democracies and it's steadily getting worse. First past the post is no longer fit for an electorate that wants to vote increasingly for minor parties. It works where there are just two choices. Individual constituencies, especially safe seats, are often hugely unrepresentative. Finally add demographics that skew the ageing electorate ever more tory, until the baby boom finishes playing out. So that's less North-South more safe seats vs everywhere else. Yet it does often play out North-South most when seeing election results maps. Still Scotland votes overwhelmingly against Conservative, who barely exist in Scotland, yet gets English Tory government. Admittedly they won a couple back in 2017, but I can see why they inch toward independence. It's different this side of the border, but NW and NE England issues are equivalent in scale. CGP Grey summarises it well: [VIDEO] I'm trying hard to avoid a political argument. :) > spending does localise in high population areas, no? Well it does, but thanks to the ever increasing centralisation of funding - started in 1974 I think, but especially since replacement of rates with council tax, even high population areas can't compete. The funding Leeds or Manchester gets for infrastructure, services is declining compared to the SE. Even though both are booming, they are permitted to do less per capita. We've ended up "},{"id":9872891,"author":"Qwertious","month":"2015-07","story":"Why do some countries drive on the left and others on the right?","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how first past the post misrepresents UK voters","quote":"I think you'll appreciate this CGO Grey video","text":"I think you'll appreciate this CGO Grey video: [VIDEO] . You're not wrong , but the system is nonetheless extremely misrepresentative."},{"id":31361859,"author":"dijit","month":"2022-05","story":"Chat control: EU Commission presents mass surveillance plan on May 11","stance":"reference","why":"Shows UKIP winning few seats despite many votes","quote":"explained very nicely; here","text":"Thanks for taking the time to think about it. > Democracy itself always gives rights to the majority against the will of the minority. this is true and it has a name I believe; the \"Tyranny of the Majority\", I think. > The tyranny of the majority (or tyranny of the masses) is an inherent weakness to majority rule in which the majority of an electorate pursues exclusively its own objectives at the expense of those of the minority factions. Unfortunately, as you mentioned, being _more democratic_ ultimately lends itself to being this way. However one neat trick the EU has (and I promise I'm not trying to spin this as pro-EU, as it's only in one place): is that the direct votes are split proportionally. This means that lesser represented groups get a voice in the EU parliament. For the UK with their \"First past the post\" system, and winner take all government; where a party like UKIP can get 12.6% of all votes and win 0 representation in parliament.... well, lets say it's a stark contrast. explained very nicely; here: [VIDEO] The EU voting system (the way in which we elect MEPs to parliament) is detailed in this video by the BBC: [LINK] ; as you'll see it's much more representative. I know of other videos that speak pragmatically about the EU, its problems and if it's worth it (though with a slight pro-EU stance): [LINK] I also found this video that asks the question if the EU is democratic, which I think makes good points and you can draw different conclusions from it: [LINK] I"},{"id":29605073,"author":"tacocataco","month":"2021-12","story":"Pelosi defends stock trading by lawmakers. ‘We are a free-market economy’","stance":"reference","why":"First past the post problems in UK elections","quote":"IMO, it seems like First Past the Post voting is the problem still.","text":"I'm not from the UK, but CGP grey is AFAIK. He has a short video called \"Why the UK Election Results are the Worst in History\" (2015). IMO, it seems like First Past the Post voting is the problem still. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19597303,"author":"NeedMoreTea","month":"2019-04","story":"Poor public transport explains the UK’s productivity puzzle","stance":"reference","why":"Analysis of 2015 UK election unrepresentativeness","quote":"CGP Grey's \"Why the UK Election Results are the Worst in History\" on the 2015 results.","text":"> makes it seems like the UK is this corrupt, broke country when it couldn't be further from the truth. If so, how is it that we no longer fund $service adequately any more (where $service is any and all of them)? How is that possible after so much has been privatised leaving so many fewer to fund publicly? Why can't we afford enough police? Why can't we afford adequate library provision? Interesting that parliament keeps their public library though, isn't it? How is it that in a time of highest levels of employment food banks have grown from barely existing to being everywhere? Could it be that policy has resulted in deliberately inadequate welfare provision? How, in a time of such extensive employment, are wage rises barely noticeable? If your definition is a few incoming oligarchs buying up expensive property they never set foot in, or someone putting up a willy waving skyscraper sure UK is doing great. By every other measure, including levels of mental health, poverty, satisfaction etc, UK has effectively shot itself. Oh, and don't hold up Tories being elected as some sort of proof when our democracy is one of the least representative on the planet[1]. Our system is biased both systemically and demographically. It feels like a corrupt, broke country, especially after a visit to London. It feels like somewhere to be deeply embarrassed of, and I mostly am these days. [1] [VIDEO] CGP Grey's \"Why the UK Election Results are the Worst in History\" on the 2015 results."}],"r":[["agZ0xISi40E",2],["VVp8UGjECt4",1],["Mc_4Z1oiXhY",1],["5IUj1EZwpJY",1],["2u6HTG8LuXQ",1],["5cWck_xcH64",1],["KqFNbCcyFkk",1],["qSfdtmcZ4d0",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["MZvmYaFkNJI",1],["Z1PCtIaM_GQ",1]]},"s9fByRmAHgU":{"wy":"Recommended as a practical talk on privacy and anonymity options, including diskless laptops running Tails.","q":{"x":"Worth listening to Jake Appelbaum's 'digital anti repression workshop' [1].","i":13455334,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":13455334,"author":"ergot","month":"2017-01","story":"I Had My Electronics Seized by U.S. Customs and Border Protection","stance":"rec","why":"Appelbaum explains using Tails on a diskless laptop","quote":"Worth listening to Jake Appelbaum's 'digital anti repression workshop' [1].","text":"Worth listening to Jake Appelbaum's 'digital anti repression workshop' [1]. In this he explains why he takes the hard-drive out of his laptop and just uses a TailsOS thumb-drive for his computing. It would be actually hilarious when staff ask to peruse the contents of your computer for contraband, only to discover the laptop doesn't have a hard-drive. [1]: part 1 [LINK] [#]: part 2 [VIDEO]"},{"id":6043497,"author":"gnosis","month":"2013-07","story":"Poll: Do you encrypt your email?","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on privacy and anonymity options","quote":"a nice talk on privacy and anonymity options[6][7]","text":"You might find it interesting/useful to read about: steganography[1], anonymous remailers[2] (particularly the Mixminion[3] type), the Paranoid Security Guide[4] (which was on HN today[5]), and a nice talk on privacy and anonymity options[6][7] There were also a couple of lists of darknets on HN recently, but I can't find them right now. [1] - [LINK] [2] - [LINK] [3] - [LINK] [4] - [LINK] [5] - [LINK] [6] - Part 1 - [LINK] [7] - Part 2 - [VIDEO]"},{"id":4260269,"author":"locusm","month":"2012-07","story":"Whistleblower Binney says the NSA has dossiers on nearly every US citizen","stance":"rec","why":"Appelbaum workshop on digital anti-repression","quote":"Some stuff I recommend to have a look at... in no particular order.","text":"I live in the Southern Hemisphere and like most 40 something year olds I follow whats happening in the States from a distance. I've been reading and watching a fair bit of Jacob Appelbaum and reading Glenn Greenwald's posts which seem to be fair and well written. Democracy Now also does some interesting interviews with Jacob and William Binney. Some stuff I recommend to have a look at... in no particular order. Challenging the Surveillance State - Pt. 1 to Pt.4 [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] Jacob Appelbaum (Part 1 & 2) Digital Anti-Repression Workshop - April 26 2012 [LINK] [VIDEO] Glenn's Salon Posts [LINK]"}],"r":[["XuBdf9jYj7o",1],["Q3uvzcY2Xug",1]]},"1Y1kJpHBn50":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a well-researched primer when airport security theatre and Israeli-style screening come up.","q":{"x":"If you actually want to be informed on the subject I highly recommend","i":35439713,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35439713,"author":"HopenHeyHi","month":"2023-04","story":"Airport scraps 100ml liquid rule with new scanners","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well-researched 14-minute explanation of airport security design","quote":"If you actually want to be informed on the subject I highly recommend","text":"If you actually want to be informed on the subject I highly recommend: How to Design Impenetrable Airport Security by Wendover Productions ( [VIDEO] , 14 minutes, well researched) Doesn't appear to have any downsides, appears to be logical, effective, and necessary. Appeals to emotion not withstanding. If you have any good faith suggestions for improvement after watching this I'd be all ears."},{"id":35455651,"author":"HopenHeyHi","month":"2023-04","story":"Airport scraps 100ml liquid rule with new scanners","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the logic behind Israel's airport security","quote":"Places where it really matters, like Israel, cannot afford to engage in security theatre, it is all derived by logic.","text":"That's great that they came over to visit the beach but nobody can look into your soul and know that and sometimes people who are not so nice don't just come over to visit the beach. This presents a challenging problem. It sounds like you are under the impression that people are being inconvenienced simply out of spite or due to the discretion of some unfriendly TSA-type airport employee - nope. Understandable why you would perceive things as such but the reality is very different, there is quite a lot going on, before you even board the plane, that you don't see. Places where it really matters, like Israel, cannot afford to engage in security theatre, it is all derived by logic. How it works as is explained in great detail in this video: [VIDEO] Appeals to emotion mean nothing to me. I get that it feels bad and don't particularly care as being blown up feels worse, promise. If after watching that you have pragmatic improvements to suggest I am all ears."},{"id":19023540,"author":"qazqwert","month":"2019-01","story":"Israel airport security stole my laptop","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Israeli airport security and operations","quote":"this is a nice video i'll suggest you'd watch regarding israels airport security","text":"it's not rare in israel becuse they do have a very big terror problem... but the missing laptop is unusual and... mybe you'v had some iligal shit/... cracked software/... idk -or mybe it was just a mistake/missplacment have you asked about the laptop? btw this is a nice video i'll suggest you'd watch regarding israels airport security and oparetion... [VIDEO]"},{"id":24342711,"author":"chard_slicks_","month":"2020-09","story":"Police across Canada are using predictive policing algorithms, report finds","stance":"reference","why":"Profiling at Ben Gurion airport","quote":"Check out this video [0] which talks about profiling at Ben Gurion airport.","text":"I agree with the unethical part but I'm not so sure about the statement that they don't work. Check out this video [0] which talks about profiling at Ben Gurion airport. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19533975,"author":"chii","month":"2019-03","story":"Laptops to Stay in Bags as TSA Brings New Technology to Airports","stance":"reference","why":"Contrasts security theatre with Israel-style screening","quote":"These scans are theatre. Actual security is","text":"These scans are theatre. Actual security is [VIDEO] , and most people in the USA would not agree with this sort of checks."}],"r":[["0sHCQWjTrJ8",1]]},"Ii_pEXKKYUg":{"wy":"Cited as primary-source evidence that RISC-V code density is competitive with or better than ARM and x86.","q":{"x":"You should watch this video","i":12303599,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12303599,"author":"legulere","month":"2016-08","story":"Intel Licenses ARM Technology to Boost Foundry Business","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows code density differences among x86, ARM and RISC-V are minuscule.","quote":"You should watch this video","text":"> If you look at functionality per instruction byte, RISC is fairly low. You should watch this video: [VIDEO] The most important information therein is that the difference in code density is minuscule between x86, ARM and RISC-V. The differences between GCC versions is even bigger. > The equations that you have to resolve to issue an X86 instruction are simply more complex than for ARM, and all that bit-flipping consumes power. It's actually just 3-10% of power consumption [1] and I even heard it claimed that the instruction decoding on ARM is larger than x86 [1] [LINK]"},{"id":25488413,"author":"dkjaudyeqooe","month":"2020-12","story":"Apple M1 foreshadows Rise of RISC-V","stance":"rec","why":"In-depth analysis comparing RISC-V, ARM and x86.","quote":"see the following talk for an in depth analysis","text":"At best, that's a simplistic and misleading view, see the following talk for an in depth analysis: \"Comparison of RISC V, ARM, and x86\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":19165514,"author":"microcolonel","month":"2019-02","story":"RISC-V on the Verge of Broad Adoption","stance":"reference","why":"Source for RV64GC density versus AArch64 and AMD64.","quote":"","text":"As of early 2016, with the GCC port at that time, RV32GC was as dense as Thumb, and RV64GC was denser than AArch64 and every other major 64-bit ISA, including AMD64. Though RV64G (no C) was in some extreme cases up to 50% larger than AArch64 (due to inlining memcpy and memset, which are a bit larger without compressed instructions), but usually around the same (except MIPS64, which is way larger than the other 64-bit ISAs, probably because of exposed delay slots). [0] There's some indication that density should have increased somewhat since then, but I haven't looked at it myself. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18364174,"author":"rwmj","month":"2018-11","story":"HiFive1 – Arduino RISC-V Dev Board","stance":"reference","why":"Slide showing code density similar to x86 and better than Thumb.","quote":"Code density is claimed to be similar to x86 and better than ARM with thumb","text":"Code density is claimed to be similar to x86 and better than ARM with thumb, see the bottom of this slide: [VIDEO] More information on the original design (not quite the final design) of the Compressed extension can be found in the original research paper: [LINK] And ... SPARC. Seriously? Register windows were a failed experiment."},{"id":17611975,"author":"nickik","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: Does anyone have something negative to say about RISC-V?","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence RISC-V code density is very good.","quote":"That is just plain false.","text":"> poor vector instruction implementations All I have heard is that the people who know about vectors absolutely love the extension. Why is your problem with it? > bad code density That is just plain false. Its actually extremely good, RV32C and RV64C easily beat every other ISA. Even without the 'C' extension the difference to the other ISA are minimal. See: [VIDEO] > The other general point I really dislike is their focus on ISA as if that matters so much compared to uArch. You don't like that the people who are designing an ISA focus on the ISA? That seems an incredible strange argument. It is well known and the RISC-V people often repeat that ISA don't matter much for performance. That's why it makes 0 sense not to have an open one that is not restricted so that you can have competition and open source implementations."},{"id":15892715,"author":"nickik","month":"2017-12","story":"Boom v2: Open-source out-of-order RISC-V core [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Instruction counts favor RISC-V with C extension.","quote":"","text":"> because it turns out that getting high performance from an ISA with only simple and relatively large instructions is actually rather difficult That is not the reason. Its actually far easier to build a fast MISP or RISC-V Out-of-order CPU then it is a x86. Even if you do it for both the MIPS/RISC-V will run with lower energy and less area. If you look at a ISA countsy you will see that MISP on 64 Bit is not that far behind x86 and RISC-V is actually ahead in instruction count specially when including the C extension that is in the standard for the Linux ABI. See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13219572,"author":"rwmj","month":"2016-12","story":"Ask HN: What's the value proposition of RISC-V?","stance":"reference","why":"Slide showing compressed extension compares well with x86.","quote":"RISC-V has a compressed extension that compares favourably to even x86.","text":"RISC-V has a compressed extension that compares favourably to even x86. See [VIDEO] (bottom of the slide)."}]},"SEDuVF7kiPU":{"wy":"During the 2017 memo controversy, HN commenters pointed to it as Damore's own account and a detailed walk-through of the memo's sources.","q":{"x":"See Jordan Peterson's interview with James Damore, where he goes through the memo almost line-by-line.","i":14973094,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14975159,"author":"dolevyao","month":"2017-08","story":"Google engineer wrote his memo after he went to a shaming diversity program","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Damore's interview with Jordan Peterson.","quote":"I highly, highly recommend watching Damore's interview with Jordan Peterson.","text":"I highly, highly recommend watching Damore's interview with Jordan Peterson. I am starting to think that Damore might be on the right side of all of this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15425347,"author":"kukx","month":"2017-10","story":"Confessions of a Failed Female Coder","stance":"rec","why":"Damore's side of the story.","quote":"It is worth to listen to the other side","text":"> This is why I dislike Damore. I don't care what his manifesto says ... Playing games is pathetic, dude. Well, according to his statement he was a top performer at Google, so we can assume he was not afraid of being replaced by a woman. Besides he just started a discussion in a response to, according to him, shady moves in regards of equality performed by Google. It is worth to listen to the other side: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14970978,"author":"boona","month":"2017-08","story":"More than 60 women consider suing Google, claiming sexism and a pay gap","stance":"rec","why":"Interview of James Damore by Jordan Peterson.","quote":"James has a new interview with psychology professor Jordan Peterson.","text":"It seems that HN is flagging all articles that mention the Google Memo. Which speaks to the need to have this conversation even more. For those who are interested, James has a new interview with psychology professor Jordan Peterson. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14971048,"author":"coreyp_1","month":"2017-08","story":"Google Memo Author James Damore Talks to Jordan Peterson [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Full interview with links to the scientific papers discussed.","quote":"The full video is very well put together, including links to the scientific papers discussed.","text":"Why is this flagged, and why do the submitted links keep disappearing? (This video in this link is incomplete, the full video is here: [VIDEO] ) The full video is very well put together, including links to the scientific papers discussed. This is a high-profile event at one of the largest internet tech company in existance, and it has a bearing on every one of our lives. So why is it being flagged and removed everywhere?"},{"id":14973094,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2017-08","story":"The Google memo is poorly argued and reaches the wrong conclusion","stance":"rec","why":"Peterson walks through the memo almost line by line.","quote":"See Jordan Peterson's interview with James Damore, where he goes through the memo almost line-by-line.","text":"James's memo is a lot better argued, more nuanced and fair than this collection of personal opinions, bald assertions, non-sequiturs and inane hypotheticals. See Jordan Peterson's interview with James Damore, where he goes through the memo almost line-by-line. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14973353,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2017-08","story":"Google’s War Over the Sexes","stance":"rec","why":"Peterson walks through the memo almost line by line.","quote":"First, see Jordan Peterson's interview with James Damore, where he goes through the memo almost line-by-line.","text":"First, see Jordan Peterson's interview with James Damore, where he goes through the memo almost line-by-line. [VIDEO] Second, he doesn't say that \"female attributes/desires are not as compatible with tech jobs\", at least not as you define them. These are his suggestions for improving diversity: \"Women on average show a higher interest in people and men in things ○ We can make software engineering more people-oriented with pair programming and more collaboration. Unfortunately, there may be limits to how people-oriented certain roles at Google can be and we shouldn't deceive ourselves or students into thinking otherwise (some of our programs to get female students into coding might be doing this). ● Women on average are more cooperative ○ Allow those exhibiting cooperative behavior to thrive. Recent updates to Perf may be doing this to an extent, but maybe there's more we can do. \" You are saying this is already the case at your workplace. Awesome, you already have what he suggests. Why is what he suggest wrong-headed??"},{"id":14974289,"author":"cropsieboss","month":"2017-08","story":"Susan Wojcicki, CEO of YouTube Responds to Google Memo","stance":"rec","why":"Interview shows Damore's motives and cited sources.","quote":"Watched the interview above and cannot believe how media and almost every individual responding put this individual","text":"Why is everyone appealing to emotions and producing red herrings? No one refers to any points other than those which they interpret to be oppressive and offensive (mostly points having to do with female/male differences which are real and exist). The hypothesis put out are there to further the discussion, not to be taken as facts (there are words like \"may\", \"in part\" all over the place yet we interpret \"may\" as \"is\", and \"in part\" as \"mostly\"). What about Google's unscientific, hidden, irrational methods of fixing bias? Unconscious bias training is unscientific yet they practice it. It does not work, or at worst it is harmful. Facebook does it too. These methods come from one of the hardest fields of science - psychology. We know how much the studies aren't reproducible, repeatable, some results even depend on time (more known the methods the less they work). Why is no one looking at those points? [VIDEO] Watched the interview above and cannot believe how media and almost every individual responding put this individual in some kind of aggressive, oppressive, racist frame. Given the peer-reviewed sources in the video description of the interview no one sane enough could believe that the guy was being malevolent. The real guilt goes to the leaker, the dramatizer and the reactionist. I'm officially stopping with the tracking of this topic. It frustrates me that so many brilliant individuals become ideologues and witch hunters incapable of continuing the discussion. It saddens m"},{"id":14976808,"author":"orionblastar","month":"2017-08","story":"Fired Engineer James Damore: I Feel Google Betrayed Me","stance":"rec","why":"Full interview; description cites the scientific sources.","quote":"Look at the full text of that video to see the links cited","text":"TL;DR version, James Damore works for Google, researches scientific sources for gender definitions. Compiles each definition and factor into a document that uses neutral third party citations and peer review to back up his citations. Gets fired over SJW Politics, accusing him of making gender stereotypes by people using politics, culture, society for gender definitions, Google panics over the protesters, feminists, social justice warriors, etc upset over the document and under pressure fire James Damore. He was not a shitty or toxic employee, he was assigned to do scientific research on genders and compile it into a document using scientific proof. [VIDEO] Look at the full text of that video to see the links cited: Published on Aug 9, 2017 James Demore of Google recently wrote a memo detailing his thoughts about Google's various diversity initiatives. Inside the company, and then outside, it went viral. He lost his job, in consequence: for \"perpetuating gender stereotypes.\" The problem is that everything James claimed is solidly backed by well-developed scientific literatures. Thus, the company that is arguably in charge of more of the world's communication than any other has now fired a promising engineer for stating a series of established scientific truths. That's worth thinking about. In this full 50 minute interview, James and I discuss his motivations, and the consequences of his actions. We are joined (audio only) by another Google employee who wishes, for obvious reas"},{"id":15022705,"author":"jxramos","month":"2017-08","story":"Ask a Female Engineer: Thoughts on the Google Memo","stance":"rec","why":"Longer, more relaxed interview than other coverage.","quote":"Still an interesting discussion","text":"I watched some youtube interviews of him, one on Bloomberg where he seemed pretty nervous and not as articulate or as relaxed as on this longer interview [VIDEO] with some Canadian professor who apparently has a bad rap of his own. Still an interesting discussion with some odd 3rd party alternate Googler in the background who speaks up every now and then but who has an obscure role in the context of the overall discussion. I was pretty curious too who this fella was that stirred up the hornet's nest."},{"id":17413285,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2018-06","story":"Google Sets Rules to Curtail Employee Debate","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence that scientists disagree about the memo's science.","quote":"Not all scientists would agree.","text":"> It was a hodgepodge of cherry picked science Not all scientists would agree. [LINK] [VIDEO] > motivated reasoning and slander against all of his female workers. Only if you don't take what he says at face value and impute motivations which aren't directly stated in the text. The very need of this imputation to justify his treatment and the huge reaction, as well as the intellectually dishonest way in which the Google management and the left-leaning press engaged in hit pieces -- all of this smacks of ideological groupthink. The very fact that people can reasonably disagree about the interpretation of the memo, contrasted with the outsized reaction are pretty damning. Here's a feminist who disagrees with Damore's position in his memo, who lays out a cogent argument for why he's wrong, and there is systemic bias, but who thinks the reaction was a \"little off,\" [LINK] Also, note that my position is that there is also some cultural bias in the programming field -- as a field. (Hackers in the HN sense have model train clubs as their cultural forebears.) However, this doesn't preclude that women within our current cultural context may well decide not to become programmers for their own reasons, and that some of this motivation may be correlated with their gender -- without there being systemic bias within hiring organizations."},{"id":16403578,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2018-02","story":"Fired Google Engineer Loses Diversity Memo Challenge","stance":"reference","why":"Psychologist corroborates the memo's science.","quote":"What would you say if a prominent and widely cited academic and clinical Psychologist corroborated his interpretations?","text":"He's citing \"legitimate scientific sources\" but he doesn't understand them, so his citations are meaningless. You seemingly refuse to substantiate this. I made no claim that his interpretations are \"wrong\". I said that his interpretations are most likely wrong because he's not a psychologist What would you say if a prominent and widely cited academic and clinical Psychologist corroborated his interpretations? [VIDEO] I think your response would be to again be intellectually dishonest and tar him by association. However, in doing so, you abandon your former position. You keep insulting me. No, what you are doing semantically parallels what climate science deniers do. You are using the same techniques and tactics, seemingly counting on the audience to be too lazy to read the actual sources for themselves. My comparison is functional. If you wish to prevent me from making that point, then please start substantive responses, using facts, reasoning, and references. Otherwise you are just using the climate denier tactic of repeated assertion."},{"id":14972023,"author":"jshevek","month":"2017-08","story":"James Damore Discusses Google Memo with Jordan Peterson","stance":"reference","why":"Points to the complete version of the interview.","quote":"Maybe the link should be changed","text":"Maybe the link should be changed, as the text under the video reads: \"IF you have come here, go here instead: [VIDEO] The video here is incomplete.\""},{"id":14972090,"author":"skoocda","month":"2017-08","story":"The Google memo isn’t sexist or anti-diversity, it’s science","stance":"reference","why":"Full Damore interview with Peterson.","quote":"Here's the full interview[1]","text":"Here's the full interview[1] - the above comment linked a shortened version. For reference, this is Dr. Jordan Peterson, the University of Toronto professor who was fired a few months ago for his stance on using gender pronouns. He's a strong proponent of free speech, which he touches on in this discussion. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14978700,"author":"alexknvl","month":"2017-08","story":"Outraged about the Google diversity memo?","stance":"reference","why":"Source for how the memo was distributed.","quote":"","text":"It wasn't even meant for the entire company. He gave it to a small community of people within Google who later distributed it across the company AFAIU from his interview with Jordan Peterson ( [VIDEO] )."},{"id":14994367,"author":"TheAceOfHearts","month":"2017-08","story":"Why I Was Fired by Google","stance":"reference","why":"Peterson comments on the memo's accuracy.","quote":"Peterson points out a few issues in his writeup as they go over it.","text":"I'm not an expert on the subject. However, in his interview with Jordan Peterson [0], whom AFAIK is an expert on the field, the following claim was made: \"I would like to state for the record that I believe that what you said in there, if not accurate, was at least representative of the current state of the art among well-trained psychometrically informed psychologists who are experts in the field of individual difference.\" Peterson points out a few issues in his writeup as they go over it. Considering the author of the memo is not an expert, I think it's reasonable to be a bit more charitable with his writeup. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15005084,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2017-08","story":"Google Doesn't Want What's Best for Us","stance":"reference","why":"Supporting source for defending the memo.","quote":"","text":"How often is that post going to be bandied about as The Final Truth ?? 1. \"Argues for biologically determined sex differences... Not really. Argues that we can't rule out biologically influenced differences. 2. \"fails to understand the current state of research \" Well, there's a whole bunch of researchers who disagree with that. 3. \"argues cognitive sex differences influence performance\" Nope. Argues there are differences in preferences that come out at the population level. 3a) \"in software engineering, but presents no supporting evidence\" Sort of true. It doesn't present evidence for performance differences, because it doesn't make that claim. It presents evidence for differences in preference, which is that it does claim 4. \"fails to acknowledge ways in which sex differences violate the narrative of female inferiority;\" I don't even know what that is supposed to mean. 5. \"assumes effective meritocracy\" Nope. See above about preference vs. ability. In fact, does quite the opposite, by assuming preferences and not abilities are often a stronger influence. 6. \"makes repugnant attacks on compassion and empathy\" COMPLETELY WRONG. It references a plea for compassion instead of empathy, because empathy leads to bad outcomes, Against Empathy: The Case for Rational Compassion by Paul Bloom: \"Brilliantly argued, urgent and humane, AGAINST EMPATHY shows us that, when it comes to both major policy decisions and the choices we make in our everyday lives, limiting our impulse toward emp"},{"id":15022018,"author":"annexrichmond","month":"2017-08","story":"Ask a Female Engineer: Thoughts on the Google Memo","stance":"reference","why":"Damore explains why he shared the document.","quote":"He mentioned in an interview[1] that he was looking to be proven wrong","text":"I disagree. He mentioned in an interview[1] that he was looking to be proven wrong which is what led him to share it with the Skeptics group at Google, which is when the document propagated. He had actually wrote the document weeks prior but was unsatisfied with the lack of discussion on his document. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":14984728,"author":"danso","month":"2017-08","story":"Fired Google engineer compares high-paid tech job to Soviet forced labor","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I was struck by how naive and reactionary he seemed to be.","text":"Yeesh, this guy seems to lack a sense of proportion. I watched part of his interview with Jordan Peterson [0] and I was struck by how naive and reactionary he seemed to be. For example, he talks about how he was inspired to write his memo after going to one of Google's \"diversity summits\". He tells Peterson that the summit was \"unrecorded and super-secret\", which made him uncomfortable because other meetings/discussions at Google are open and recorded [1]. I worked at a past company in which we were mandated (I believe it might have been part of complying with to California law [2]) to do training that involved talking about diversity. It was also unrecorded, and for good reason. Part of the session included a group discussion about stereotypes and beliefs about diversity and other cultures, and the moderator asked us to be honest. Some people said things that would be considered very offensive (both with and without context) in airing their reservations about diversity and harassment. They would never have been open and honest and said those things in a session that was recorded . I've never worked at Google so I don't know how their diversity summits are recorded. But it's possible that what Damore sees as a secretive conspiracy was actually meant to be good practice when fostering honest internal discussions. The fact that he sees what he allegedly suffered as even remotely analogous to a Soviet gulag makes me give him less benefit of the doubt. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [VIDEO] [2]"}],"r":[["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1],["jMmUoZh3Hq4",1],["WpkDN78P884",1],["cbeJIwF1pVY",1],["DJFKl_5JTnA",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["VDfX44fZoMc",1],["NURiiQatBXA",1],["AYshVbcEmUc",1],["t705r8ICkRw",1],["bpAi70WWBlw",1]]},"UmIgNfp-MdI":{"wy":"HN treats it as a gold-standard concept demo and a what-might-have-been for dual-screen and digital notebook devices.","q":{"x":"It has also been my personal gold standard of a superb video demo.","i":2200253,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2200253,"author":"widgetycrank","month":"2011-02","story":"The Next Six Months","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Microsoft Courier concept demo, a gold standard video demo","quote":"It has also been my personal gold standard of a superb video demo.","text":"I'm not sure if anyone remembers the Microsoft Courier tablet demo, which came out before iPad's announcement: [VIDEO] This video left me with a strong impression of future general computing usage patterns. It has also been my personal gold standard of a superb video demo. Interestingly enough, they abandoned it right after iPad's release: [LINK]"},{"id":6266809,"author":"dhughes","month":"2013-08","story":"Ballmer forced out after $900M Surface RT debacle, analyst says","stance":"rec","why":"Dual-screen tablet concept with notes and scraps software","quote":"I thought the Courier was a great idea not just for the dual screens but the software as well","text":"I thought the Courier was a great idea not just for the dual screens but the software as well the scraps and notes seemed like a great idea. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29300028,"author":"Nition","month":"2021-11","story":"Thoughts on “E-Readers” (2009)","stance":"reference","why":"Microsoft's unreleased dual-screen notebook tablet","quote":"Microsoft was working on a dual-screen tablet that was essentially a notebook in computer form","text":"> But MOST importantly the way the user interacts with the interface would re-create the ease of use rendered by our interaction with print media. This last part, about the user interface, is why I believe Apple is best positioned to tackle this problem. > In one year's time we'll see. 2010 should be the year paper learns of it's destiny, the year we reach a tipping point on our way towards bits from the momentum of atoms. This is of course a prescient prediction of the iPad. But what it also reminded me of is a device that had already been shown publicly at the time: The Microsoft Courier. Microsoft was working on a dual-screen tablet that was essentially a notebook in computer form, complete with custom OS.[1] In the end it never happened. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21652990,"author":"Nition","month":"2019-11","story":"How to annotate everything","stance":"reference","why":"Courier as digital notebook with clippings and annotation","quote":"Microsoft Courier was designed to solve this ten years ago, by being essentially a digital version of a notebook","text":"Microsoft Courier was designed to solve this ten years ago, by being essentially a digital version of a notebook[1]. You'd fill your notebook with digital clippings from any media and freely annotate them. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21137671,"author":"jahlove","month":"2019-10","story":"Microsoft announces dual-screen Surface Neo","stance":"reference","why":"Original Courier concept video that generated hype","quote":"Here [1] is the original concept video for the Courier.","text":"Here [1] is the original concept video for the Courier. I remember it generated a ton of hype when that video came out. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21138074,"author":"wilsonthewhale","month":"2019-10","story":"Microsoft introduces Windows 10 X for dual-screen devices","stance":"reference","why":"Courier concept resembling later dual-screen devices","quote":"always wished Microsoft could have made that happen.","text":"That picture gave me a big Courier vibe[1], always wished Microsoft could have made that happen. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10073695,"author":"mmorris","month":"2015-08","story":"Ask: Do you still find any benefits in using handwriting?","stance":"reference","why":"Concept of a digital notebook tablet","quote":"I'm still dreaming of something like the Microsoft Courier Concept","text":"Like others have mentioned, I still do handwriting in a notebook, mainly because it's easier to sketch a diagram with a pen and paper than it is to do it digitally. Pros of handwriting/pen+paper: - Extremely high \"resolution\" (can fit a lot of information in a small space) - Flexibility - Direct input: tools rarely \"get in the way\" Cons of handwriting/pen + paper: - Editing is cumbersome - Storage/retrieval is annoying (no search!) - Sharing/collaboration is not as good as digital I'm still dreaming of something like the Microsoft Courier Concept [0]. We've gotten a lot closer since the concept came out (e.g., the MS Surface), but I think we're still not quite at the point where a digital notebook can feel just like paper + pen. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":1472996,"author":"muhfuhkuh","month":"2010-06","story":"Cisco Cius: Cisco Unveils Android-Based, Business-Focus Tablet Computer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"At least they got further along than MS Courier. They actually have a useless prototype, rather than a vaporvid[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"X1T3IQ4N-3g":{"wy":"Recommended as a clear, practical introduction to atomics and lock-free programming in C++.","q":{"x":"Jeff Preshing gave a really good introduction to atomics at CppCon 2014.[1]","i":9061485,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9061485,"author":"zamalek","month":"2015-02","story":"Comparison: Lockless programming with atomics in C++ 11 vs. mutex and RW-locks","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear introduction to atomics and lock-free programming","quote":"Jeff Preshing gave a really good introduction to atomics at CppCon 2014.[1]","text":"Jeff Preshing gave a really good introduction to atomics at CppCon 2014.[1] He explains it so clearly that 1 day later I had a fully tested lock-free work stealing queue and a queue. One of my favorite talks of last year. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10105220,"author":"dougbinks","month":"2015-08","story":"Implementing a lightweight task scheduler in C++","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how atomic operations are implemented","quote":"For those interested in the implementation of atomic operations see Jeff Preshing's","text":"For those interested in the implementation of atomic operations see Jeff Preshing's \"How Ubisoft Develops Games for Multicore - Before and After C++11\", especially after 15m55s in on the video, or slide 37 of the pdf on github. Video: [VIDEO] PDF: [LINK]"},{"id":25787828,"author":"gens","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: What are you working on?","stance":"reference","why":"Ubisoft talk matching the commenter's own engine design","quote":"found a talk from an Ubisoft employee that is mostly what i came up with","text":"I'm making a game/engine with efficiency/performance as a top goal. I'v spent years thinking about the design. And after i figured it out, i found a talk from an Ubisoft employee that is mostly what i came up with. The problems are many, especially since i want to support multiplayer. The biggest solutions are around how data is transformed and scheduling the code that transforms it, more or less. The talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11782095,"author":"corysama","month":"2016-05","story":"Parallelizing the Naughty Dog Engine Using Fibers [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Ubisoft talk going deep on atomic ops, compared with Naughty Dog's","quote":"Ubisoft's talk spends more time getting into the weeds with atomic ops.","text":"Similar talk: CppCon 2014: Jeff Preshing \"How Ubisoft Develops Games for Multicore - Before and After C++11\" [VIDEO] Ubisoft's talk spends more time getting into the weeds with atomic ops. Naughty Dog's is more of an architecture discussion. If you can only watch one, I'll recommend Naughty Dog's."}],"r":[["xP5-iIeKXE8",1],["QM1iUe6IofM",1]]},"sTu3LwpF6XI":{"wy":"HN people recommend Ben Eater's videos for easy, clear explanations of digital logic and building computers.","q":{"x":"All his videos are amazing, and super easy to understand.","i":19975987,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19975987,"author":"compiler-guy","month":"2019-05","story":"Integrated Circuits Can Be Easy to Understand with the Right Teachers","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear explanation of NOR gates from transistors","quote":"All his videos are amazing, and super easy to understand.","text":"This video was reasonably good, but still required a lot of background. If you want really terrific easily understood, yet totally in-depth information about integrated circuits, starting from the physics how semi-conductors actually work at the electron level, all the way up through gates and on to an entire computer, Ben Eater's videos are outstanding. Most people know his work from the Breadboard computer, [LINK] , where he builds an actual functioning computer, with instructions and microcode and output, from scratch on a breadboard, along with terrific explanations. But that is just the tip of the iceberg. All his videos are amazing, and super easy to understand. Here is his version of how nor gates work: which is way easier to understand to me than this video: [VIDEO] Here is his whole channel: [LINK] and his website: [LINK]"},{"id":27742492,"author":"z3ugma","month":"2021-07","story":"Ask HN: Recommend Some Logic Gate Tutorials and Examples?","stance":"rec","why":"Ben Eater series building a computer from scratch","quote":"series on building an 8-bit computer from scratch was very helpful to me","text":"Ben Eater’s series on building an 8-bit computer from scratch was very helpful to me in this regard: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36174146,"author":"somat","month":"2023-06","story":"Firmware Is Broken Terminology","stance":"reference","why":"Combinational logic from transistors","quote":"The thing that blew my mind was how you can replace discrete transistor logic with memory.","text":"The thing that blew my mind was how you can replace discrete transistor logic with memory. While it is sort of obvious in retrospect. I think it an important insight into the nature of computation. Making combinational logic gates from transistors: [VIDEO] Using an EEPROM to replace combinational logic: [LINK]"},{"id":36288713,"author":"pixelfarmer","month":"2023-06","story":"Modern CPUs have a backstage cast","stance":"reference","why":"Example of basic gate explainers","quote":"Something like that?","text":"Something like that? [LINK] or [VIDEO] and there are truckloads more of these. There used to be electronics sets for kids with breadboards (some of them larger scale for easier physical handling then standard breadboards), and they'd had manuals with them explaining all the things. I remember that they also contained descriptions of basic gates like (N)AND, (N)OR, NOT, plus flip flops for storing 1 bit of data."}],"r":[["rRi8LptvFZY",1],["ZLWMQLMiTPk",1]]},"5ZjhNTM8XU8":{"wy":"Recommended as a compact preview of Kleppmann's data systems material, including from his book on the subject.","q":{"x":"I recommend watching this talk from him to give a little glimpse of the book","i":19901785,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19901785,"author":"redisman","month":"2019-05","story":"Ask HN: What overlooked class of tools should a self-taught programmer look into","stance":"rec","why":"Condensed glimpse of Kleppmann's book content on data systems","quote":"I recommend watching this talk from him to give a little glimpse of the book","text":"It's great. Incredibly dense with useful information and it just blows my mind how much knowledge Martin has about the topic. I recommend watching this talk from him to give a little glimpse of the book: [VIDEO] This is just about a little part of one of the chapters."},{"id":16840291,"author":"geksilla","month":"2018-04","story":"Ask HN: What is your favourite tech talk?","stance":"rec","why":"Kleppmann talk on transactions, listed among favorite tech talks","quote":"","text":"Rich Hickey - \"simple made easy\" [LINK] Rob Pike - \"concurrency is not parallelism\" [LINK] Uncle Bob Martin - \"future of programming\" [LINK] Martin Kleppmann - \"transactions: myths, surprises and opportunities\" [VIDEO] Simon Brown - \"software architecture vs code\" [LINK]"},{"id":34366485,"author":"gexaha","month":"2023-01","story":"UC Berkeley researchers set new visions for making CRDT data stores practical [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Kleppmann talk on consistency guarantees","quote":"you can find talks of Martin Kleppmann on youtube about consistency guarantees","text":"jepsen.io has a neat diagram of various consistency models - [LINK] also, you can find talks of Martin Kleppmann on youtube about consistency guarantees, e. g., [VIDEO]"},{"id":25351996,"author":"vvern","month":"2020-12","story":"Kafka Is Not a Database","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the 'invert the database' idea","quote":"","text":"I mean, I hear you that people do that and it does allow you to avoid needing distributed transactions. When you're stuck with an underlying NoSQL database without transactions, this is the best thing you can do. This is the whole \"invert the database\" idea that ends up with [1]. I'm arguing that that usage pattern was painful and that if you can have horizontally scalable transaction processing and a stream of events due to committed transactions downstream, you can use that to power correct and easy to reason about materializations as well as asynchronous task processing. Transact to change state. React to state changes. Prosper. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["ecIWPzGEbFc",1],["rI8tNMsozo0",1]]},"5fbvquHSPJU":{"wy":"Mentioned as a one-package overview of heterodox views on money creation and debt, though some find it unsurprising.","q":{"x":"goes into these topics at length, especially money creation","i":10229430,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":10229430,"author":"jayfuerstenberg","month":"2015-09","story":"Can the Fed raise interest rates?","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary covering money creation and debt at length.","quote":"goes into these topics at length, especially money creation","text":"The documentary \"Four Horsemen\" (link below) goes into these topics at length, especially money creation. Most people's jaws drop when they learn that 97% of all money in the world isn't in paper/coin form but rather 0s and 1s in a database and that it is all willed into existence as debt by a bank. [VIDEO]"},{"id":6637356,"author":"kamjam","month":"2013-10","story":"The End of Hypocrisy","stance":"rec","why":"Documentary packaging the economic model suspicions into one overview.","quote":"It's pretty interesting, although nothing eye opening, but good to see it in one package","text":"Liberals tend to believe that other countries cooperate with the United States because American ideals are attractive and the U.S.-led international system is fair. I just watched The Four Horsemen on Youtube. It's pretty interesting, although nothing eye opening, but good to see it in one package (so to speak). The economic model to screw over everyone else to advance itself should have made it more than obvious everything you ever suspected was true. Now that more and more of these suspicions are being confirmed the US is on the back foot. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18273277,"author":"tareqak","month":"2018-10","story":"Why non-Muslims are converting to sharia finance","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"had a part in the \"Four Horsemen\" documentary","text":"I recognized person mentioned at the end of the article: Tarek El Diwany (same first name as me spelled differently) had a part in the \"Four Horsemen\" documentary ( [VIDEO] ). I found an article about him in [LINK] ."}],"r":[["Fgh2dFngFsg",1]]},"8k2AbqTBxao":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an insightful short explanation of why US and UK comedy differ.","q":{"x":"Actually very insightful, and explains why I have never been fond of the cool guy","i":32131960,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":43372612,"author":"rikroots","month":"2025-03","story":"So Long and Thanks for All the Words: A Toast to Douglas Adams","stance":"rec","why":"Fry's take on American versus British comedy.","quote":"I really like Stephen Fry's take on the difference between American and British comedy","text":"I really like Stephen Fry's take on the difference between American and British comedy: [VIDEO] (YouTube)"},{"id":32131960,"author":"sph","month":"2022-07","story":"Rowan Atkinson: Visual Comedy (1992) [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Insightful explanation of why US and UK comedy differ.","quote":"Actually very insightful, and explains why I have never been fond of the cool guy","text":"You must be a fan of American comedy then. Stephen Fry explains the difference here: [VIDEO] Actually very insightful, and explains why I have never been fond of the cool guy that gets all the girls US comedy loves to represent."},{"id":32132837,"author":"atombender","month":"2022-07","story":"Rowan Atkinson: Visual Comedy (1992) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Fry attributes the difference to optimism versus pessimism.","quote":"I'm more inclined to agree with Stephen Fry [1], who explains that it's ultimately a matter of optimism versus pessimism.","text":"That doesn't ring true to me at all. I'm more inclined to agree with Stephen Fry [1], who explains that it's ultimately a matter of optimism versus pessimism. The great American comics are people like John Belushi, the wisecracking charmer who gets the girl in the end, while the British comedian prefers to play the failure; British comedy comes from strife and tragedy and embarrassment and loss of dignity, which is why most British comedy focuses on lower/middle class people and their relationship with the hierarchy: Basil Fawlty (John Cleese's character in Fawlty Towers), Blackadder, David Brent (The Office), and so on. The types of comedic personalities you tend to see in American culture are the likes of Jim Carrey and Adam Sandler, featuring people who may be average, but generally don't start as failures, and if they do (or if they have an unfortunate fall from grace in the middle of the story), the central theme isn't the inevitability of their failure, but an optimistic path towards self-betterment and reclaiming control over their own destiny. The US also doesn't have the kind of history of tensions between the classes that Britain has (even though wealth inequality is objectively worse, and the system is simply not recognized as being a class system among ordinary people). The American style of comedy goes hand in hand with the idea of the American dream, that anyone can succeed if they try hard enough. The most \"British\" American comedy I've seen in recent times was"},{"id":29466385,"author":"datenhorst","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: What is your favorite piece of entertainment of the last three years?","stance":"reference","why":"Fry explains differences between US and UK comedians.","quote":"Stephen Fry's explanation of the differences between US and UK comedians[0]","text":"> although as usual the US version stunk I haven't watched the US version but I am reminded of Stephen Fry's explanation of the differences between US and UK comedians[0]. Taskmaster needs contestants willing to embarrass themselves. [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["xUezfuy8Qpc",1],["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["0PclgBd6_Zs",1],["ia8Q51ouA_s",1],["zBgCRJluWTc",1],["jpNU72dny2s",1],["mb3uK-_QkOo",1],["RTB5XhjbgZA",1],["9-IWMbJXoLM",1],["gp4U5aH_T6A",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["SqayDnQ2wmw",1],["JvBT4XBdoUE",1],["w70Xc9CStoE",1],["BFld4EBO2RE",1],["aMcJ1Jvtef0",1]]},"IiuVggWNqSA":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it for Forstall's firsthand anecdotes and his account of how the iPhone came about.","q":{"x":"The CHM interview tells Scott's","i":37560186,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":44229769,"author":"rweichler","month":"2025-06","story":"Apple introduces a universal design across platforms","stance":"rec","why":"Forstall anecdotes from the Computer History Museum interview.","quote":"I love me some Scott Forstall anecdotes.","text":"Mind throwing some links my way? I love me some Scott Forstall anecdotes. Here, I'll start: - [LINK] - [VIDEO] - [LINK]"},{"id":23267564,"author":"bangonkeyboard","month":"2020-05","story":"Scott Forstall tells story about Steve Jobs, Microsoft, and a dead fish [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Forstall interview at the Computer History Museum, with AI thoughts around 48:36.","quote":"He also related this story in his interview with the Computer History Museum","text":"He also related this story in his interview with the Computer History Museum: [VIDEO] I'm still upset that, at 48:36, Forstall offered to give his thoughts on what we could be doing with AI that we're not, but Markoff decided to move on."},{"id":37560186,"author":"paulgerhardt","month":"2023-09","story":"Paul McEnroe’s role in developing the UPC barcode hasn’t been fully acknowledged","stance":"reference","why":"Forstall's account of the iPhone's origin story.","quote":"The CHM interview tells Scott's","text":"There are two origin stories. The Verge article tells Tony's. The CHM interview tells Scott's: [VIDEO] Given the iPhone hardware came from skunkworks iPad team (and not iPod) and the software came from the Mac team (and not iPod) I personally take Tony's claims of inventing the iPhone with a grain of salt and opportunistic when Scott was later forced out over the unrelated Apple Maps launch issues. But you should judge for yourself. It's probably not clear cut."},{"id":22169540,"author":"ksec","month":"2020-01","story":"The iPad Awkwardly Turns 10","stance":"reference","why":"Forstall says tech people just do not get touch UX.","quote":"Scott Forstall said it [2] these people just dont get it.","text":"I would have thought Double Click was something \"Death would take care of it\" [1] by now, so may be not. I remember twenty years ago teaching my parents double click, they then spend years on computer and they still cant understand it. They have 4 modes in their mental model, Left Click, Right Click, and Double Click on both Left and Right. They dont understand when to use which. A lot of nerds / geeks/ tech people to this day still dont understand what makes the whole touch screen / iPhone UX so special. Scott Forstall said it [2] these people just dont get it. The single tap app opening may have been trivial for most of us, it was a world of difference to 95% of consumers. ( And that is why you should not put these people into consumer tech product design ) [1] Something Steve Jobs said with regards to people dont know how to use a keyboard, but I cant find the video with Google. It was during an interview with Walt Mossberg. [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"RijGNytjbOI":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the video version of the argument that JOSE/JWT is a flawed standard, and as a help in understanding token design choices.","q":{"x":"If you like video consumption, a DEFCON 2018 talk by the author Scott Arciszewski on this same topic","i":22366145,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":23662368,"author":"chriswarbo","month":"2020-06","story":"Hardcoded secrets, unverified tokens, and other common JWT mistakes","stance":"rec","why":"Helped commenter understand token design choices.","quote":"I especially enjoyed watching this video","text":"I've been doing a similar thing recently. I found PASETO to be interesting ( [LINK] ), which is a lot like JWT but is simpler and more opinionated (e.g. there is no plaintext option; symmetric and asymmetric operations are cleanly separated; etc.). I especially enjoyed watching this video [VIDEO] I still decided to go with JWT, since PASETO isn't yet standard and its software hasn't been around for long, but it definitely helped me understand more about the choice I was making. In particular, PASETO distinguishes between \"local\" tokens (producer and consumer can both use the same symmetric key) versus \"public\" tokens (producer and consumer can't use the same key, e.g. third party services run by different organisations; hence asymmetric keys are needed). PASETO encourages local tokens to be used when possible, since their keys can be shorter, they generate shorter tokens, they're faster to encrypt/decrypt, they're easier to rotate, etc.; public tokens are essentially a last resort when we must interact with a third party. I was originally considering 'public' JWTs (i.e. asymmetric keys) for my use case, since we're using AWS API Gateway and that has the option to check the signatures of 'public' JWTs, and reject invalid ones before they ever hit my code. However, I eventually went with 'local' JWTs (i.e. symmetric keys), since the convenience of having AWS discard some tokens for us wasn't worth the (mostly logistical) overhead of using asymmetric keys. My choice to use the J"},{"id":22366145,"author":"wadkar","month":"2020-02","story":"Javascript Object Signing and Encryption is a Bad Standard (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Video version of the author's JOSE critique.","quote":"If you like video consumption, a DEFCON 2018 talk by the author Scott Arciszewski on this same topic","text":"If you like video consumption, a DEFCON 2018 talk by the author Scott Arciszewski on this same topic can be found here: No Way JOSE! Designing Cryptography: [VIDEO] You're welcome :) Edit: Clarify that Scott is the author of both post and video."},{"id":18806073,"author":"CiPHPerCoder","month":"2019-01","story":"How to Use JSON Web Tokens","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on why JOSE standards are flawed.","quote":"DEFCON CPV talk","text":"Blog post: [LINK] DEFCON CPV talk: [LINK] + [VIDEO] Alternative design that isn't radioactive: [LINK] Apologies if this wasn't more readily available or commonly known. I'm worse at marketing than I am at engineering."}],"r":[["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["EZ05e7EMOLM",1]]},"V9K6gjR07Po":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a cool, eye-opening look at the hidden ultraviolet world, especially in sunscreen and vitamin D threads.","q":{"x":"Veritasium has a video using a special UV setup, it's pretty cool","i":24141281,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24141281,"author":"hrnnnnnn","month":"2020-08","story":"Vitamin D, part 1: back to basics","stance":"rec","why":"Uses a special UV camera setup.","quote":"Veritasium has a video using a special UV setup, it's pretty cool","text":"You need fancy lenses to do UV photography. From wikipedia: \"For UV photography it is necessary to use specially developed lenses having elements made from fused quartz or quartz and fluorite.\" Veritasium has a video using a special UV setup, it's pretty cool - [VIDEO]"},{"id":19246302,"author":"rjf72","month":"2019-02","story":"Sunlight through glass does not provide Vitamin D","stance":"rec","why":"Looking at the world in UV is quite interesting.","quote":"Looking at the world in UV [1] is quite interesting!","text":"Mildly amusing related aside from this. Because of this, glass is completely opaque in the UV spectrum. Looking at the world in UV [1] is quite interesting! Such a reminder that how and what we experience of the world is so largely a product of our physiological composition. What is perfectly transparent to us would be a great hiding place from the perspective of something that only saw in UV. [1] - [VIDEO]"},{"id":28285207,"author":"tasty_freeze","month":"2021-08","story":"The only skin care that works? science video response (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"UV filter camera shows sunscreen effectiveness and hidden skin damage.","quote":"they use a UV filter on a camera to show (a) the effectiveness of sunscreen","text":"I'm in my mid/late 50s, and had grandparents from Ireland, that is, I'm light skinned. As a kid my brothers and I would compete who could peel off the longest continuous strip of skin a day or two after going to the beach. Anyway, the skin on my exposed arms looks like \"old man\" skin -- lots of blotches, and more importantly not elastic. However, the skin that lives under my short sleeves is not only unblemished, it is as elastic as when I was a teenager. In short: yes, it will help against cosmetic damage as well as skin cancer. Bonus: the youtube channel veritasium did a video a few years ago where they use a UV filter on a camera to show (a) the effectiveness of sunscreen and (b) the damage which is visible in the UV years before it shows up in visible light. [VIDEO]"}]},"YdHTlUGN1zw":{"wy":"Recommended as a charming primary source on animation technique and for Walt Disney's warm on-camera presence.","q":{"x":"Here's a fascinating video in which Walt Disney showcases how his multiplane camera worked","i":21874302,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":39158090,"author":"BiteCode_dev","month":"2024-01","story":"Disney unveils the HoloTile floor","stance":"rec","why":"Warm, family-like Walt Disney presentation contrasted with HoloTile demo.","quote":"You feel like you are part of the family while watching this. It's a completely different vibe.","text":"That's a good example. Look at the video showing off this exact tech: [VIDEO] You feel like you are part of the family while watching this. It's a completely different vibe. Honestly, I feel vaguely threatened by the HoloTile Floor demo tone. The way he answers the question about what's it's going to be used for feels like a parent that doesn't want to reveal something to a child."},{"id":21874302,"author":"rahuldottech","month":"2019-12","story":"The Surprise and Wonder of Early Animation","stance":"rec","why":"Walt Disney demonstrates how the multiplane camera worked.","quote":"Here's a fascinating video in which Walt Disney showcases how his multiplane camera worked","text":"Here's a fascinating video in which Walt Disney showcases how his multiplane camera worked: [VIDEO] Truly incredible."},{"id":30271118,"author":"open-source-ux","month":"2022-02","story":"Disney Filmmaking Process","stance":"reference","why":"Disney behind-the-scenes film on the 2D multiplane camera.","quote":"Disney produced lots of behind-the-scenes for their 2D animated films in the 50s and 60s.","text":"This is really nice. Although Disney is only currently producing 3D animation films, it's a bit of a shame that nothing about 2D animation is included. The 3D animated film Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse (2018) has a refreshing 3D/2D hybrid visual look that breaks away from the 3D aesthetic common across the industry. I suspect in the future we'll see more animated films with a 2D aesthetic but created with 3D software. Aside : Disney produced lots of behind-the-scenes for their 2D animated films in the 50s and 60s. Here's one on the MultiPlane Camera - a camera setup that gave 2D animation greater depth: Walt Disney's MultiPlane Camera (1957): [VIDEO]"},{"id":30405429,"author":"Fricken","month":"2022-02","story":"Anime Photography in the Cel Era (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Short 1957 Disney documentary on inventing the first multiplane camera.","quote":"Here is a short doc from Disney (1957) about the invention of the first multi plane camera","text":"I built a multi-plane camera setup in high school for an animation project in, without knowing it was a thing used in the industry. My version was very simple. Instead of stacking the planes, I hung them from yardsticks spanned between two tables, so I could slide the planes forward toward the camera at different rates of speed. Here is a short doc from Disney (1957) about the invention of the first multi plane camera: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["7VG_s2PCH_c",1]]},"cxrkC-pMH_s":{"wy":"HN commenters share it in chip fab and industrial espionage threads as a well-researched account of East Germany's failed microelectronics effort.","q":{"x":"this is a well researched video by Asianometry, which actually contains a couple of details (unlike the Politico article) about the spycraft involved","i":41154342,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41154342,"author":"flohofwoe","month":"2024-08","story":"The Untold Story of How US Spies Sabotaged Soviet Technology","stance":"rec","why":"Well-researched account of East German microelectronics spycraft, based on a first-person book.","quote":"this is a well researched video by Asianometry, which actually contains a couple of details (unlike the Politico article) about the spycraft involved","text":"Regarding the microelectronics industry behind the Iron Curtain, this is a well researched video by Asianometry, which actually contains a couple of details (unlike the Politico article) about the spycraft involved: [VIDEO] (based on the first-person account book 'Deckname Saale' by Gerhardt Ronneberger)"},{"id":37047876,"author":"nntwozz","month":"2023-08","story":"TSMC, Bosch, Infineon, and NXP to build fab in Germany","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant Asianometry video on East German semiconductors.","quote":"Just want to share these relevant videos from Asianometry","text":"Just want to share these relevant videos from Asianometry: How Semiconductors Ruined East Germany [VIDEO] Why Europe Lost Semiconductors [LINK] Europe’s Lost Decades in Semiconductors [LINK]"},{"id":38406855,"author":"3seashells","month":"2023-11","story":"Chinese spies had acces to Dutch chip maker NXP's systems for over two years","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as an example of copying-over-innovation in chip espionage history.","quote":"","text":"I weep for the Chinese researchers never getting funding and a moment in the sun, cause a incompetent secret service/insecure government prefers copying. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37049078,"author":"MengerSponge","month":"2023-08","story":"TSMC, Bosch, Infineon, and NXP to build fab in Germany","stance":"reference","why":"Example of East Germany failing despite heavy investment.","quote":"East Germany famously tried really hard to develop a native capability, but failed","text":"China is willing to point a firehose of resouroces at development targets, but that doesn't guarantee success. Semiconductors are really stinking hard! East Germany famously tried really hard to develop a native capability, but failed and wound up smuggling parts from the west and running years behind western firms: [VIDEO] China is more willing to both reward and hold senior stakeholders responsible, as long as they aren't too closely allied with Xi"}],"r":[["kIoZDUd5DKw",1],["p83QUZ1-P10",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["sycgL3Qg_Ak",1]]},"f9vRcSAneiw":{"wy":"Recommended as a real-world case of Lisp in a cutting-edge industry, applied to quantum hardware.","q":{"x":"About the application of Lisp on quantum hardware.","i":16046384,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16046384,"author":"Vekz","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"rec","why":"Favorite 2017 talk on applying Lisp to quantum hardware.","quote":"About the application of Lisp on quantum hardware.","text":"Lisp at the Frontier of Computation, by Robert Smith at Rigetti Computing [VIDEO] About the application of Lisp on quantum hardware."},{"id":16228294,"author":"reikonomusha","month":"2018-01","story":"The Era of Quantum Computing Is Here. Outlook: Cloudy","stance":"rec","why":"One of a couple of neat videos about Rigetti's quantum tech.","quote":"There are a couple neat videos about the tech.","text":"Rigetti Quantum Computing [0] is a YC startup that actually manufactures real quantum computers that are accessible through Forest. There are a couple neat videos about the tech. [1,2] You can access the service through a Python API [3]. Disclaimer: I work there. [0] [LINK] [1] “Lisp at the Frontier of Computation” [VIDEO] [2] Rigetti talk at QIP 2017 [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":15903775,"author":"robertkrahn01","month":"2017-12","story":"An Interactive Introduction to Quantum Computing","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on using Common Lisp and building an assembler-like language for quantum computing.","quote":"Interesting in this regard might also be the talk that Robert Smith of Rigetti Computing recently gave","text":"Interesting in this regard might also be the talk that Robert Smith of Rigetti Computing recently gave: [VIDEO] Apart from praising Common Lisp he describes how they are building an assembler-like language for quantum computing. The HN thread: [LINK]"},{"id":19044040,"author":"phoe-krk","month":"2019-01","story":"Rigetti launches the public beta of its Quantum Cloud Services","stance":"reference","why":"Notes Rigetti uses Common Lisp, linking the talk.","quote":"Rigetti is one of the companies using Common Lisp in their research and development.","text":"Shameless smuglispweeniesm: Rigetti is one of the companies using Common Lisp in their research and development. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17623777,"author":"reikonomusha","month":"2018-07","story":"Ask HN: Who is using Common Lisp?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source about Rigetti's QVM simulator written in Lisp.","quote":"","text":"Rigetti Computing [1] uses it for a variety of their projects, including their high-performance quantum computer simulator called the QVM [2], and their quantum compiler. Some of their use of Lisp has been presented in more detail at the European Lisp Symposium. You can see it in the Cutting Edge section of the proceedings [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":16407953,"author":"reikonomusha","month":"2018-02","story":"What Made Lisp Different (2002)","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker's own talk on Rigetti using Common Lisp for its quantum stack.","quote":"Rigetti hosted one of the Lisp meetups to talk about their use of Lisp","text":"Rigetti Quantum Computing is a relatively recent YC startup that happens to use Common Lisp for building out their quantum programming language, compiler, and simulator stack. For those tasks, it has been an excellent choice, especially given the hugely exploratory and rapidly evolving space. Rigetti hosted one of the Lisp meetups to talk about their use of Lisp and it was recorded in a talk \"Lisp at the Frontier of Computation\" [0]. [0] [VIDEO] (Disclaimer: I'm the speaker in the video.)"}]},"hhiLw5Q_UFg":{"wy":"Recommended in LLM threads as an authoritative explanation from a ChatGPT architect of why models hallucinate and why imitation is not enough.","q":{"x":"There's a very good talk by John Schulman, chief architect of ChatGPT","i":36013980,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36013980,"author":"KKKKkkkk1","month":"2023-05","story":"Don Knuth Plays with ChatGPT","stance":"rec","why":"Schulman explains RL's role in reducing hallucination.","quote":"There's a very good talk by John Schulman, chief architect of ChatGPT","text":"There is this idea that the goal of RLHF is to make ChatGPT woke or as you put it to lobotomize it. I suspect that this is a conspiracy theory. There's a very good talk by John Schulman, chief architect of ChatGPT [0], where he explains that if you don't include a RL component in your training, you're essentially doing imitation learning. It's well known that imitation learning fails miserably when presented with conditions that are not in your training set, i.e., answering questions that don't exist on the Internet already. So the goal of RLHF is actually to reduce hallucination. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35702146,"author":"mike_hearn","month":"2023-04","story":"It's not only you, there's an explosion of ChatGPT on HN","stance":"rec","why":"Schulman on truthfulness, uncertainty, and why models hallucinate.","quote":"Sure, here you go","text":"Sure, here you go: [VIDEO] Summarizing: 1. Obviously you can't completely \"solve\" truthfulness because people disagree on what is and is not true. But you can go a long way. 2. The models do know what they don't know. Their level of uncertainty is not only expressed in the final token logprobs but also seems to be reified somehow, such that they can express their own level of certainty in words. 3. Many of the problems are introduced by subtle issues introduced during training which effectively teach the AI to guess. One example is by training on QA data sets where the answer is never \"I don't know\". RLHF introduces its own problems because the human trainers don't know what the model knows, so may reward it for getting a correct answer by guessing. 4. Many failures are caused by lack of access to information. Giving the LLM tools to let it search the web and access other databases can help a lot, and it's relatively easy to do. 5. In some cases you want it to guess. Like, it's much more useful when coding for it to spit out a screenful of code that has one or two minor errors, than to just refuse to try at all because the result might not be perfect."},{"id":36682860,"author":"chongli","month":"2023-07","story":"AI Safety and the Age of Dislightenment","stance":"reference","why":"Schulman on hallucination and models' knowledge of uncertainty.","quote":"John Schulman from OpenAI recently gave a talk about the hallucination and uncertainty issues","text":"How is that \"as good as an intern?\" Interns, for the most part, do not hallucinate and confidently lie on topics they know nothing about. Good interns tend to ask a lot of questions and quickly learn the cultural cues around the office, allowing them to fit in and become productive. LLMs are incapable of any of this. John Schulman from OpenAI recently gave a talk about the hallucination and uncertainty issues [1]. He claimed that models already have information about their uncertainty but that it remains an open problem as to how to express that uncertainty in natural language. One of the big issues with trying to prevent the model from hallucinating is that you can err too much on the side of caution, causing the model to lie about things it actually does know the correct answer to. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36079491,"author":"airgapstopgap","month":"2023-05","story":"The False Promise of Imitating Proprietary LLMs","stance":"reference","why":"Schulman on the problem with behavior cloning.","quote":"Recently, John Schulman explained the issue with behavior cloning and it's a very typical ML problem.","text":"This is exactly the reason OpenAI isn't afraid of the open-source community, like many kneejerk opponents of regulatory capture assume (they are probably still afraid of Google). Also why they still do the expensive and cumbersome RLHF training, instead of those deceptively cheap and fast finetunes. They understand their own tech and why there isn't free lunch. Recently, John Schulman explained the issue with behavior cloning and it's a very typical ML problem.[1] Basically: what are we training the model to do? The model updates after finetuning in a holistic manner, based on the sum total of its content and capability. Suppose GPT-4 can correctly answer to many requests because it knows correct answers, in the sense that it has something isomorphic to an internal knowledge graph and tools for querying it, and that graph contains sufficient data for its tools to derive an answer at inference. RLHF reinforces this behavior by constraining the distribution of outputs (essentially, steering the model away from applying inappropriate tools for respective inputs, e.g. employing fantasy-narrative or bad-yahoo-answers cognitive routines when asked something that looks like a straightforward factual question). Now suppose you teach LLaMA-13B to imitate those responses by SFTing it on a dump of successful GPT-4 conversations. But LLaMA doesn't have internals that would have enabled it to find the same answers; so on the object level it shallowly memorizes specific items of the post-t"},{"id":35683819,"author":"lysozyme","month":"2023-04","story":"Training open-source LLMs on ChatGPT output is a really bad idea.","stance":"reference","why":"Schulman explains why RL beats supervised training for truthfulness.","quote":"The author says he’s summarizing a talk by John Schulman of OpenAI [2] but I haven’t personally watched the video.","text":"Do yourself a favor and skip right through to the Twitter link to another link to this excellent post [1] on the actual reason that training new models on ChatGPT output in the manner of supervised learning (in contrast to reinforcement learning) will not produce a model as good as ChatGPT >For this type of interaction, we must use RL training, as supervised training teaches the model to lie. The core issue is that we want to encourage the model to answer based on its internal knowledge, but we don't know what this internal knowledge contains. In supervised training, we present the model with a question and its correct answer, and train the model to replicate the provided answer. The author says he’s summarizing a talk by John Schulman of OpenAI [2] but I haven’t personally watched the video. In any case, this is an interesting insight. Say we set up a supervised learning scenario where we ask the model to use its internal knowledge to answer a question and compare its answer to one written by a human. If the two answers essentially say the same thing, but in different words, in the supervised learning case the model is penalized. In the RL case, it’s rewarded. That’s the difference. 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"}]},"mhJRzQsLZGg":{"wy":"Linked during Starship launches and testing as a live view that needs no login and shows the rockets actually being built.","q":{"x":"You can also watch them actually build.","i":47617414,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47617414,"author":"chasd00","month":"2026-04","story":"The SpaceX IPO: retail investor notes","stance":"rec","why":"Livecam view of rockets being built and flown.","quote":"You can also watch them actually build.","text":"The difference with spacex is you can watch the rockets fly. You can also watch them actually build. That's where the goosebumps come from one livecam view, there are many [VIDEO] this guy releases videos about once a week or so [LINK] this aerial photography channel documents bocachica every week or so too. Spacex is absolutely watched from every angle. [LINK]"},{"id":38318794,"author":"capableweb","month":"2023-11","story":"Starship Integrated Flight Test 2 at 7 Am Central Time","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible without login wall or account.","quote":"it's accessible without any login-wall and doesn't require creating an account to view.","text":"Maybe [VIDEO] (NASASpaceflight) would be better, as it's accessible without any login-wall and doesn't require creating an account to view."},{"id":38317248,"author":"BenoitP","month":"2023-11","story":"Starship Integrated Flight Test 2 at 7 Am Central Time","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among live launch coverage channels.","quote":"","text":"When (launch window opening) * 4 hours from now * 5 AM Pacific Time * 7 AM Central Time (local) * 8 AM Eastern Time * 2 PM Central European Time Live Media * Official yet channel: [LINK] * Labpadre: [LINK] * NASASpaceflight: [VIDEO] * EverydayAstronaut: [LINK] * TheLaunchPad: [LINK] * TechniquesSpatiales(FR): [LINK] Ressources * Mission sheet: [LINK] * Intro video: [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":38318764,"author":"BenoitP","month":"2023-11","story":"","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among live launch coverage channels.","quote":"","text":"When (launch window opening) * 5 AM Pacific Time * 7 AM Central Time (local) * 8 AM Eastern Time * 2 PM Central European Time Live Media * Official YT channel: [LINK] * Labpadre: [LINK] * NASASpaceflight: [VIDEO] * EverydayAstronaut: [LINK] * TheLaunchPad: [LINK] * TechniquesSpatiales(FR): [LINK] Ressources * Mission sheet: [LINK] * Intro video: [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK] * [LINK]"},{"id":35592093,"author":"verdverm","month":"2023-04","story":"SpaceX's First Starship Launch Tomorrow","stance":"reference","why":"24/7 live stream of Starbase ahead of launch.","quote":"(24/7 live stream, apparently they already have a countdown timer lol)","text":"The folks a NasaSpaceFlight have/will be doing live pre-shows up until the launch - [LINK] (this sunday show will catch you up) - [VIDEO] (24/7 live stream, apparently they already have a countdown timer lol)"},{"id":35640472,"author":"falsenapkin","month":"2023-04","story":"SpaceX giant rocket explodes minutes after launch from Texas","stance":"reference","why":"Multi-panel view showing debris near the pad during launch.","quote":"See the bottom right panel","text":"There was another view at the pad that had sizable bits of what looked like rock or concrete flying past it, camera went off line when the vehicle had barely left the ground. See the bottom right panel [VIDEO]"},{"id":31345785,"author":"staunch","month":"2022-05","story":"I/O 2022","stance":"reference","why":"Live stream showing Starship development progress.","quote":"anyone can watch its developmental progress live","text":"The claim was that Musk's companies haven't \"built\" anything. Versions of Starship have clearly been built and launched, even if the project is still in development. It's a very real project and anyone can watch its developmental progress live: [VIDEO] I doubt you or anyone else would bet significant money that Starship won't reach orbit. If so, I'd be happy to accept. For the record, I don't think you can point to any project even remotely equivalent in terms of capability or progress from Boeing, Lockheed, or ULA. I'd be happy if they were doing nearly as well as SpaceX. My other examples: Starlink, Tesla, and Dragon have clearly been \"built\" and shipped. Starlink is the hands of consumers, is actively helping Ukraine for military and humanitarian purposes, Tesla has shipped millions of vehicles, and Dragon Crew had another successful rendezvous with ISS days ago. I could spend a lot more time providing examples but consider these to be more than sufficient to disprove the claim that Elon Musk's companies haven't \"actually built anything he announces\" in \"years\"."}],"r":[["qPKj0YnKANw",1]]},"rGqiswuJuQI":{"wy":"HN cites it for Lanier's ideas on the circle of empathy and paying people whose data and work train AI.","q":{"x":"The best proposal I’ve heard to deal with the societal/economic problems this sort of A.I. poses were made by Jaron Lanier","i":33227552,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33227552,"author":"vghfgk1000","month":"2022-10","story":"GitHub Copilot, with “public code” blocked, emits my copyrighted code","stance":"rec","why":"Lanier's proposal of micro-compensation for people whose work trains AI","quote":"The best proposal I’ve heard to deal with the societal/economic problems this sort of A.I. poses were made by Jaron Lanier","text":"The best proposal I’ve heard to deal with the societal/economic problems this sort of A.I. poses were made by Jaron Lanier: [VIDEO] …I can see why his proposals of providing (micro-)compensation to people whose tremendous efforts end up being mined by these algorithms would not be popular with researchers/companies who stand to benefit vastly (…presumably including the investors who own this site?). The lobbying power/political power/ awareness/financial resources of your average (atomised) artist/programmer/musician etc. is pretty much nil in comparison… Forgive the clumsy analogy, but I have a feeling the whole thing might end up something like a haulage company that doesn’t want to pay any taxes to help fix the roads though?"},{"id":29583757,"author":"animal_spirits","month":"2021-12","story":"Pelosi defends stock trading by lawmakers. ‘We are a free-market economy’","stance":"rec","why":"Lanier's 'circle of empathy' for who benefits from your work","quote":"I recently came across a lecture by Jaron Lanier that discusses this kind of thinking and how to reason about it.","text":"First I wanted to say I'm not trying to guilt trip you or anything, I admit I wrote my response quickly and didn't think it would attract this much attention. So just wanted to let you know I value your views and am not trying to put you down! > That doesn't really answer the question though. If knowingly making society worse were the standard for feeling shame, I think I'd be too depressed and shame-ridden to even get out of bed. I mean, you want to talk about damaging society: every single day I get into my car and burn fossil fuels on my commute to/from work, which is essentially slowly killing the planet. I know it's bad for society, but I do it anyway. Knowingly. On purpose. Can you imagine how psychopathic I am to treat society with such casual disregard? I think you bring up a good idea here. I recently came across a lecture by Jaron Lanier that discusses this kind of thinking and how to reason about it. > I came up with an image to help me think about who the beneficiary is of our activities, whether we're engineers or writers or anything else, and I called this the 'circle of empaty'.... Those who are inside the circle are the beneficiaries of what you do, and anything inside the circle deserves your empathy. Things outside the circle perhaps don't deserve your antagonism but on the other hand they're not the beneficiaries of what you do. So I hope that people believe that all human beings should be inside the circle. That's not always the case; there are racists and"},{"id":39849386,"author":"zone411","month":"2024-03","story":"We've been here before: AI promised humanlike machines – in 1958","stance":"reference","why":"Lanier recalls AI research hype was storytelling to win grants","quote":"","text":"About Minsky being too optimistic: \"I would tell Marvin this whole AI thing is just horrible. Why are we doing it? And he would say it will be effective for getting our grants, so shut up and just play along and in fact, it was true. It was very good for getting grants back in the early days, in the 70's was when I started at this, you would go to a grant making organization such as the Defense Department, you'd say we're going to build this super smart thing and if we don't do it, our enemies might and it'll get smarter than people and it was Okay, here's your money, here's your money, oh my God, you better... and so it was very effective. And actually, the whole thing started off as storytelling to get grants\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["RQY6WGOoYis",1],["_njf8jwEGRo",1]]},"upzKj-1HaKw":{"wy":"Cited when HN discusses taste and vision in product companies.","q":{"x":"This summarize it very well, lack of taste and lack of vision, it's that simple","i":32110511,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32110511,"author":"Shadonototra","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: Why no company is able to deliver Apple M1/M2 performance per watt","stance":"rec","why":"Jobs on lack of taste and vision at companies.","quote":"This summarize it very well, lack of taste and lack of vision, it's that simple","text":"Because they lack taste for quality and efficiency, they don't want to invest in R&D and they don't know how to retain talents So you get companies that stagnate and higher ups are too focused on building a huge wealth and work hard to secure a golden parachute, maintaining their position and salary is higher priority [VIDEO] This summarize it very well, lack of taste and lack of vision, it's that simple and has huge impact on the products you release as a result Pigeons too busy eating random shit in this particular parking lot, they don't know any better This also shows why [LINK] Pure lack of taste It translates to: \"They are stupid for trying to do better, you should hate them too\""},{"id":3078219,"author":"kemayo","month":"2011-10","story":"Steve Jobs has died","stance":"rec","why":"1996 Jobs clip on taste and making great things.","quote":"Here's a clip from back in 1996, before his return to Apple, where he talks briefly about Microsoft","text":"One thing that he really forced down all our throats was the importance of taste. He knew what he wanted, and he made us all see how great it was. Here's a clip from back in 1996, before his return to Apple, where he talks briefly about Microsoft, and in it you can really see his continuing obsession with making insanely great things: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4527662,"author":"DeepDuh","month":"2012-09","story":"Elon Musk: \"I would like to die on Mars\"","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"It was 1996 ( [VIDEO] ). No, Jobs was certainly never as humble as Musk and I never said that, they definitely have different characters. My point was that we still have to wait on Musk, this man and his companies are far away from their goals and it remains to be seen whether they can afford to keep being idealistic. And don't get me wrong, I'm a space freak, I root for that guy as much as anyone."},{"id":1791093,"author":"gvb","month":"2010-10","story":"John Sculley: The Secrets of Steve Jobs’ Success","stance":"reference","why":"Interview where Jobs says Microsoft has no taste.","quote":"I didn't verify this link","text":"[LINK] The only problem with Microsoft is they just have no taste. They have absolutely no taste. And I don't mean that in a small way, I mean that in a big way, in the sense that they don't think of original ideas, and they don't bring much culture into their products. * Triumph of the Nerds (1996) I didn't verify this link, but google says the interview where Jobs says Microsoft has no taste can be seen here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":634204,"author":"ashot","month":"2009-05","story":" Microsoft Silverlight vs Google Wave: Why Karma Matters ","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs says Microsoft has no taste.","quote":"They are not a creative company","text":"I'm surprised you didn't make the larger point: this is true for every product Microsoft has ever made. Copy, improve, persist. Basic, DOS, Windows, Office, IE, etc. They are not a creative company (or as Jobs would have it they have no taste - [VIDEO] ). Their business does not revolve around creating new things but about copying and improving things that already exist in the market. And to be fair, this strategy has worked out quite well for them so far, so why would they change course?"}],"r":[["3KdlJlHAAbQ",1]]},"wIC-iGDTU40":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it in lighting threads for its pros and cons of LED versus sodium lamps, though one calls it almost right.","q":{"x":"Here's a quite good video about the pros and cons of LED vs high/low-pressure sodium street lamps","i":24073493,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24073493,"author":"kibwen","month":"2020-08","story":"Germany plans to dim lights at night to save insects","stance":"rec","why":"Pros and cons of LED vs sodium street lamps","quote":"Here's a quite good video about the pros and cons of LED vs high/low-pressure sodium street lamps","text":"Here's a quite good video about the pros and cons of LED vs high/low-pressure sodium street lamps: [VIDEO] . It goes into lamp efficiency, how our eyes see differently at night, response time under different sorts of light, color temperature, circadian rhythm disruption, and light pollution."},{"id":17702287,"author":"news_to_me","month":"2018-08","story":"Energy Saved, Sleep Lost: The Unintended Consequences of LED Lighting","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting LED vs sodium-vapor streetlight coverage","quote":"most if his videos are pretty interesting to explore","text":"The youtube channel Technology Connections has a couple of interesting articles about LED (and sodium-vapor lamp) bulbs in street lighting: [VIDEO] (Actually, most if his videos are pretty interesting to explore, especially the ones about early TV)"},{"id":26504735,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2021-03","story":"A call to improve London's lighting at night","stance":"reference","why":"Related Technology Connections video on sodium vapour lights","quote":"","text":"I'm reminded of a Technology Connections video about what happened when his town switched from incandescent to LED stop lights < [LINK] > Of course, while I was looking that up I found out he made a video about sodium vapour lights! < [VIDEO] >"},{"id":19854556,"author":"p1mrx","month":"2019-05","story":"Detroit’s LED streetlights going dark after a few years","stance":"reference","why":"Explains LED color temperature choices in cities","quote":"Relevant Technology Connections video","text":"Warm white LEDs exist, with color quality exceeding that of sodium vapor in every way, but cities often use blueish LEDs because they're cheaper and more efficient. Relevant Technology Connections video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24079049,"author":"teslabox","month":"2020-08","story":"Germany plans to dim lights at night to save insects","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's a good video, but it's also a case study in being 'almost right'.","text":"It's a good video, but it's also a case study in being 'almost right'. The almost-correct interpretations cascade and result in a faulty conclusion: that blue-white LED streetlights are better than orange-white streetlights. Various comments object to the faulty conclusion ([1], for example). At the end he at least addresses his ignorance: > [18:39] You may have noticed that I didn't talk about the blue light from LEDs, and how this is supposedly ruining our eyes. That's because the \"science\" behind this is questionable, at best. > [18:55] [discussion of chart with percentage blue light of various light sources] > [19:04] [...] but considering that our eyes can withstand the intensity of sunlight , which is far far greater than any normal artificial light source, and also has a lot of blue light, and ultraviolet, which definitely is harmful, I think the 'blue light' thing is just fear-mongering. If someone can point to some verified, peer-reviewed research supporting this, and not a dodgy website, I'll consider changing my stance. In any case, the high flexibility of LED technology means it can be tuned in pretty much any way we like. The research on the harm of blue light is out there -- For example, one of the comments links to \"Effects of blue light on the circadian system and eye physiology\" [0]. (only partially read at this point - I don't know if this paper gets into the mechanism of damage done by blue light, as I understand it...) The sun hangs high in the sky and cas"}],"r":[["QUcTsFe1PVs",1],["5TFDG-y-EHs",1],["s1i-dnAH9Y4",1],["hB6eY73sLV0",1],["GhsZUZmJvaM",1],["M4AhABManTw",1],["qL0BB2PRY7k",1],["uzkD5SeuwzM",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["dqwpQarrDwk",1],["DXvAlwMAxiA",1]]},"K8gV05nS7mc":{"wy":"Used as a go-to reference for cosmology questions about the beginning of time and the universe's scale.","m":[{"id":25652591,"author":"davidwf","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: What YouTube channels do you subscribe to?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great first video of PBS Space Time","quote":"Space Time: PBS Physics, sooo good","text":"I keep a Google Document of these to share just in case people ask about my YouTube interests. I Actually Watched All of Them I have actually watched ALL of the videos on these channels and I don’t even feel bad about it :-) I actually like new videos from some of the ones below (like NativLang, for example) but they maybe don’t hold up in the early videos. - Space Time: PBS Physics, sooo good -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [VIDEO] - Lindsay Ellis: Really, really intelligent video essays about film and culture -- [LINK] -- Great First Video: [LINK] - Periodic Videos: Chemistry, including a video for every element -- [LINK] -- Great first video (obviously): [LINK] Other Math and Science - 3Blue1Brown: Amazing math visualizations -- [LINK] -- Great first video, how e^pi*i works: [LINK] - NumberPhile: Fun math videos -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - PBS Eons: Biology! -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - PBS Infinite Series: Math math math, really really hard math -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - SciShow: Science! More poppy but fun -- [LINK] -- Silly first show: [LINK] -- Actual physics: [LINK] - VSauce: Science-y? -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] - Practical Engineering: -- [LINK] -- Great first video: [LINK] Other Humanities - Alliterative: Connections via language -- [LINK] -- Great first video about gin and gimlets: [LINK] - Bon Appetit: Such fun food stuff! -- Gourmet Makes: [LINK] -- It’s Alive: [LINK] - Masaman: Genetics and human migration (an"},{"id":40205451,"author":"irjustin","month":"2024-04","story":"Webb captures iconic Horsehead Nebula in unprecedented detail","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses universe in terms of scale factor","quote":"PBS Space time talks about it in terms of \"scale factor\"[0] instead of absolute diameter.","text":"> Well, no. The density in the observed universe is used to extrapolate the number of galaxies in the non-observed universe. The size of that universe is extrapolated from the rate of expansion and the time since the big bang. > We may not know the exact size at the start, but we know it was infinitesimally smaller than it is today. So the size of the initial universe isn't a big factor in the equations about how big it likely is today. Weather it started as a few centimeters across or a few thousand light years across, both are functionally zero compared to the current size. Most things you're saying are correctly rooted except for what's beyond the observable universe. I'm not sure why the staunch belief that you can confidently claim this. To be clear, you aren't provably wrong - likewise not provably right either. The replies to you are just fine, they represent a significant portion of the scientific community that says our universe is likely infinitely big and that, possibly, the big bang was infinitely small, yet still, still infinitely large. An infinite expanding into infinite still results not knowing what's out there. PBS Space time talks about it in terms of \"scale factor\"[0] instead of absolute diameter. Still, these are all debatable theories, so your take _could_ be valid, but generally, it points infinitely large. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":36717788,"author":"bb88","month":"2023-07","story":"Age of universe at 26.7B years, nearly twice as old as previously believed","stance":"reference","why":"Covers whether time stretches infinitely back","quote":"Matt from PBS space time covered this here.","text":"> wouldn't it make more sense that time actually stretches infinitely the further back we go, thus nullifying the concept of a beginning? Matt from PBS space time covered this here. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24222757,"author":"dkersten","month":"2020-08","story":"Big Bounce Simulations Challenge the Big Bang","stance":"reference","why":"Whether time began at the big bang","quote":"Here are some PBS Space Time videos on the subject","text":"It was my understanding that physicists have never claimed that the big bang happened. That the big bang itself isn’t really part of the theory at all and that its simply a singularity point, but that some moments before that point, ohysics as we know it breaks down, so we don’t know what happened before and therefore do ‘t know if there was a bang or not, just that after that time, things happened that we can measure. Put another way, the big bang theory doesn’t include the big bang itself (the something from nothing part) but is about the inflation and expansion of the universe. Here are some PBS Space Time videos on the subject: Why the big bang definitely happened: [LINK] Did time start at the big bang: [VIDEO] Whats wrong with the big bang theory: [LINK] “In the beginning, there was nothing, which exploded”, now, a lot of non-scientists like to repeat this statement, but no credible scientist has ever said this, nor believes it. So its unfortunate that this statement is used to deride the big bang theory. The big bang describes a series of events that happened to the universe following its existence in an extremely hot, dense state. We have a ton of evidence that the universe was once in such a state. Perhaps our understanding of this state will eventually lead to a theory of the origin of the universe, but the big bang theory as it stands does not claim to explain such an origin. — Matt O’Dowd, PBS Space Time"}],"r":[["62tIvfP9A2w",1],["v0YEaeIClKY",1],["0olpSN6_TCc",1]]},"OP0U62oiBlM":{"wy":"Recommended as a fascinating watch in threads on leaking wrecks and oil spills.","q":{"x":"The DW documentary on the matter. A fascinating watch.","i":24357132,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":24357132,"author":"mchem","month":"2020-09","story":"Russia’s ‘slow-motion Chernobyl’ at sea","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Documentary on oil leaking from wrecks","quote":"The DW documentary on the matter. A fascinating watch.","text":"[VIDEO] - The DW documentary on the matter. A fascinating watch. It is a shame that the economics do not play out in such a way that recovery and resale is feasible. Sadly, I do not expect most governments to be as proactive as Norway’s on the matter. The documentary suggests that even covering a wreck with sand as a preventative measure costs 10s of millions of €s. Surely an opportunity for a lower cost solution exists here?"},{"id":28140865,"author":"emn13","month":"2021-08","story":"Plants, heavy metals, and the lingering scars of World War I","stance":"reference","why":"Related to worldwide problem of wartime shipwrecks","quote":"A similar (worldwide) problem are all the maritime shipwrecks stemming from that period","text":"Fortunately, it's pretty likely that many of those toxins won't be particularly harmful in low doses; but the stress isn't good, that's for sure. A similar (worldwide) problem are all the maritime shipwrecks stemming from that period: [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24812148,"author":"kd913","month":"2020-10","story":"An oil tanker with 60M gallons of oil aboard is currently sinking [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Source for thousands of sunken ships as ticking time bombs","quote":"there are thousands of ships (6300) sunk from the past which did not have oil leached from their tanks","text":"I was watching a video about how there are thousands of ships (6300) sunk from the past which did not have oil leached from their tanks. These have been converted to basically ticking time bombs as the metal and fuel tanks corrode. The amount of oil in these sunk ships is difficult to estimate, but even on a light estimate resulted in a wide-spread ecological disaster. As usual the response from 95% of governments out there is simply to bury their heads to the problem. Source from DW Documentaries ( [VIDEO] )"}]},"Rybpaqhg5Qg":{"wy":"HN cites it for Orkney's surplus renewable energy, hydrogen facilities and EV uptake.","q":{"x":"Orkney in particular has so much renewable energy that they're encouraging people to get EVs to soak up the excess","i":15190383,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":18246701,"author":"Theodores","month":"2018-10","story":"Orkney islands could get first electric plane service","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Series on wind, hydrogen and big batteries in Orkney","quote":"I highly recommend liking, subscribing and giving a thumbs up to Fully Charged Show.","text":"The Orkney Isles are setting the example in the UK. Going green and making progress with it is also quite good for the community, not to mention the balance sheet. Another future is definitely possible and the folks in the Orkney Isles are an inspiration. The Fully Charged Show on YouTube has been following progress for years, here is a recent three part series on wind, hydrogen and big batteries in the Orkney Isles: [VIDEO] I highly recommend liking, subscribing and giving a thumbs up to Fully Charged Show. Anyone that enjoyed Red Dwarf or Scrapheap Challenge will know the presenter."},{"id":24471600,"author":"alex_duf","month":"2020-09","story":"Microsoft's underwater data centre resurfaces after two years","stance":"reference","why":"Orkney's hydrogen facilities using surplus renewables","quote":"","text":"I've heard about Orkney quite a lot, I think it must simply be an innovation hub. That I can think off the top of my head: - Orbital marine, making tidal turbine [LINK] - Their grid [LINK] - Their Hydrogen facilities, using extra renewable [VIDEO]"},{"id":15190383,"author":"ZeroGravitas","month":"2017-09","story":"Scotland plans to make petrol and diesel cars obsolete by 2032","stance":"reference","why":"Orkney renewables surplus encouraging EV uptake","quote":"Orkney in particular has so much renewable energy that they're encouraging people to get EVs to soak up the excess","text":"Islands are generally good for EVs, precisely because you can't drive that far. Orkney in particular has so much renewable energy that they're encouraging people to get EVs to soak up the excess because they have limited connectivity to export the excess back to the mainland: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PlJkgQZb0VU",1],["wSF82AwSDiU",1]]},"zQTfuI-9jIo":{"wy":"Cited as a source for the feasibility of colonizing the universe with self-replicating probes and for building a Dyson swarm from Mercury.","q":{"x":"I highly recommend this video","i":12204623,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12204623,"author":"Teever","month":"2016-08","story":"Tesla and Solar City Combine","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Presents feasibility of colonizing the universe with self-replicating probes.","quote":"I highly recommend this video","text":"If you found the above clip interesting and would like to learn more about the implications of such craft I highly recommend this video: [VIDEO] video. The presentation covers the viability of constructing Von Neumann probes for colonizing the entire universe and illustrates the feasibility of it with a few different technological scenarios. All in all it seems feasible and the implications of that are enormous."},{"id":47630808,"author":"ethmarks","month":"2026-04","story":"Mercurial Dyson – a plan for the disassembly of planet Mercury","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier lecture proposing disassembling Mercury for a Dyson swarm.","quote":"Stuart Armstrong proposed it in a lecture in 2012[0].","text":"Kurzgesagt didn't invent the concept of disassembling Mercury to build a Dyson swarm. Stuart Armstrong proposed it in a lecture in 2012[0]. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30705231,"author":"ben_w","month":"2022-03","story":"Are there limits to economic growth?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I'm not sure which definition of 'growth' you're referring to here. One in which brain uploads are relevant, because you mentioned it. Literally any kind of efficiency has a maximum, an economy of pure minds was just the most extreme case which I took specifically to avoid any possible debate: there is (according to currently known laws of reality) definitely an absolute maximum in this universe, no matter what we do. Limit the population to current number or lower, and any other kind of efficiency also has a limit — lights can’t convert less than 0% into heat, resistance can’t be less than in a superconductor, reversible computers can’t beat the Landauer limit. You might fully automate manufacturing and give everyone alive today their own personal galaxy cluster [0] and it’s still finite. Big, still finite. > If it's GDP (which the article linked earlier did), well, in many developed countries, population has been decreasing despite increasing GDP. So this issue does not prevent growth. Indeed it is decreasing. Yet you may have to enforce that, because subgroups that grow faster rapidly dominate. This is unavoidable unless you take active measures to prevent it, because that’s what evolution does. (You can because evolution is dumb, but you have to). (I’d argue that in such a case, the license to reproduce would constitute a currency and in that currency humanity today world seem unimaginably wealthy; however this is not something I am prepared to make a keystone argument)"},{"id":26505213,"author":"ben_w","month":"2021-03","story":"Grabby Aliens","stance":"reference","why":"Argues a Dyson swarm enables colonizing the future light cone.","quote":"it could make a Dyson swarm in a human lifetime","text":"Space is big, but Helios isn’t us trying to go as fast as possible, it’s going the speed it is because that’s a requirement of orbital dynamics. If we really really wanted, nuclear options could get us to a few percent of c with current tech and no breakthroughs. On the other hand, if someone builds a von Neumann probe that eats sunlight and Mercury (the planet), it could make a Dyson swarm in a human lifetime, and that would enable us to colonise most of our causal future light cone directly from Sol: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23124047,"author":"ben_w","month":"2020-05","story":"The emergence of heat and humidity too severe for human tolerance","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for building a Dyson swarm from Mercury.","quote":"","text":"2100 is way too far out to predict anything . The cost of gene synthesis is roughly 20 years behind gene sequencing [0]. Extrapolated, by 2040, arbitrarily modifying your offspring will represent a trivial fraction (~0.5%) of the total cost of starting a family. Follow Moore’s law (considering lower costs rather than boosted performance) and assuming the Kurzweil estimate for full brain emulation, then by 2047, uploaded minds would be cheaper than the food eaten by subsistence farmers. [1] The first thing most people suggest when they hear about 3D printers is printing another 3D printer. It is definitely possible because that’s what every living cell does; while nobody knows how to do this now , if we can make it happen with the rocks of Mercury, it only takes four decades to build a Dyson swarm. [2] The questions are, what happens first, and how does humanity collectively react to that? [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7093238,"author":"humbert","month":"2014-01","story":"Rosetta: ESA’s ‘sleeping beauty’ wakes up from deep space hibernation","stance":"reference","why":"Source for intergalactic expansion estimates.","quote":"a million years has virtually no impact on us reaching ~4.8 billion galaxies at 0.99c.","text":"Regarding cosmological expansion, a million years has virtually no impact on us reaching ~4.8 billion galaxies at 0.99c. [1] [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"1GhNXHCQGsM":{"wy":"HN found it a nifty demo and cites it as a primitive but effective learning tracker that predates modern deep-learning trackers.","q":{"x":"Very nifty.","i":2427088,"y":"2011"},"m":[{"id":2427088,"author":"tom_b","month":"2011-04","story":"OpenTLD - The predator TLD source code is online","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of a self-improving object tracker.","quote":"Very nifty.","text":"My friend forwarded me the video demo link for this: [VIDEO] Very nifty. I'm looking forward to reading the machine learning code involved in this."},{"id":37325533,"author":"rasz","month":"2023-08","story":"Meta AI releases CoTracker, a model for tracking any points (pixels) on a video","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier primitive ML tracker, good enough for commercialization.","quote":"This time with some very primitive ML: OpenTLD","text":"OK, 11 years ago then. This time with some very primitive ML: OpenTLD [VIDEO] Was good enough for commercialization [LINK] . I think their tech is powering this [LINK]"},{"id":27794502,"author":"jackcviers3","month":"2021-07","story":"Automating programming: AI is transforming the coding of computer programs","stance":"reference","why":"Example that object tracking has existed for a decade.","quote":"","text":"Incorrect. Recognition applications have been able to track objects across disparate frames of video even when obscures from camera view, for at least a decade now [1]. 1. [VIDEO]"}]},"5BXtgq0Nhsc":{"wy":"HN links it as the source for the claim that every post-war president would be indictable under Nuremberg standards.","q":{"x":"If the Nuremberg principles were applied, every post-World War 2 president would be indictable.","i":29220163,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":24228269,"author":"sebmellen","month":"2020-08","story":"Assange, Manning and Snowden Nominated for the 2020 Nobel Peace Prize","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting Chomsky piece on the topic","quote":"This is an interesting piece from Chomsky on that matter","text":"This is an interesting piece from Chomsky on that matter: \"Noam Chomsky - The Crimes of U.S. Presidents\" - [VIDEO] ."},{"id":43860662,"author":"SequoiaHope","month":"2025-05","story":"How the US defense secretary circumvents official DoD communications equipment","stance":"reference","why":"Clip of Chomsky describing war crimes of post-WW2 presidents","quote":"Here’s a clip [2] of Noam Chomsky describing the war crimes of every post WW2 president.","text":"Trump is awful and so was G.W. Bush and Reagan and Nixon and Andrew Jackson and even George Washington[1]. We don’t learn much in school about George Washington burning down Iroquois villages because we focus on other things. It’s entirely possible that Trump becomes so bad that the people who remember his misdeeds get erased like so many Iroquois or Vietnamese people or gays with HIV under these other presidents. Here’s a clip [2] of Noam Chomsky describing the war crimes of every post WW2 president. Many people still regard those people as good presidents because they ignore their misdeeds. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":37241758,"author":"avgcorrection","month":"2023-08","story":"New York City bans TikTok for government employees","stance":"reference","why":"Source for claim presidents could be indicted under Nuremberg","quote":"","text":"> In democracies, politicians have to care about what the public thinks. The US is not a democracy for 90% of Americans (Princeton study). > Americans as a whole are extremely judgmental about moral issues, and any president that stands by and allows 25 million Taiwanese to be crushed under the boot of the CCP will go down in history as the biggest coward since Neville Chamberlain. Sure. Except America will support “bad guys” and oppose “good guys” according to what suits them (like natural resources). Like how Saddam Hussein was an ally of the US until he went against their interests. The American people protested Reagan's interference in Latin America. That made them stop doing it. Right? No. They just went clandestine. > By and large yes, I do think democracies tend to operate (highly imperfectly) out of the goodness of their hearts (as they perceive it). Every post-WWII president up to 2003 (at least) could be indicted under the Nuremberg principles if they were upheld.[1] It's easy to have good intentions according to yourself when there are no one else around who can challenge your worldview. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":29220163,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2021-11","story":"How the U.S. hid an airstrike that killed dozens of civilians in Syria","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Nuremberg principles claim","quote":"If the Nuremberg principles were applied, every post-World War 2 president would be indictable.","text":"If the Nuremberg principles were applied, every post-World War 2 president would be indictable. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16407828,"author":"wz1000","month":"2018-02","story":"Like Peter Thiel, Tech Workers Feel Alienated by Silicon Valley ‘Echo Chamber’","stance":"reference","why":"Chomsky on presidents and Nuremberg principles","quote":"if US Presidents were held to the standards of the Nuremberg principles, they would all be executed","text":"Supporting any US President of the last century or so is morally dubious(if you consider mass murder and toppling democratic regimes morally dubious). Like Chomsky likes to say, if US Presidents were held to the standards of the Nuremberg principles, they would all be executed. In that regard, Trump has so far done less harm than most recent Presidents. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wAIP6fI0NAI",1],["4-oV42OMQoE",1],["ORp3q1Oaezw",1],["QmOF0crdyRU",1]]},"7tScAyNaRdQ":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a well-made version of the story and quote it jokingly whenever discussions touch on biology versus machine intelligence.","q":{"x":"The short-film adaptation of this was very well done.","i":8152272,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":8152272,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2014-08","story":"They're Made out of Meat","stance":"rec","why":"Well-done short film adaptation with Lynch-influenced directing.","quote":"The short-film adaptation of this was very well done.","text":"[VIDEO] The short-film adaptation of this was very well done. The David Lynch-influenced directing style worked well."},{"id":32214054,"author":"belter","month":"2022-07","story":"A new way to predict when software jobs will become automated","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"And who decides the task is done, complete and works? The Meat does [2]","text":"We need another \"No Silver Bullet\" [1] for these modern times of ridiculous hype. Contain your ego and all these Matrices Statistical manipulations you decide to call AI...Somebody please volunteer. \"OpenAI estimates that OpenAI Codex (what powers Github’s Copilot) can already complete 37% of coding tasks.\" And who decides the task is done, complete and works? The Meat does [2]...The Meat and nobody else. [1] \"No Silver Bullet\": [LINK] [2] \"They're Made Out of Meat\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":24740381,"author":"c22","month":"2020-10","story":"They're Made Out of Meat (1991)","stance":"reference","why":"Film adaptation won a 2006 festival grand prize.","quote":"the Stephen O'Regan film adaptation [1] someone else already posted here won the 2006 Grand Prize at the SIFF Science Fiction Short Film Festival [2].","text":"> I'll grant that it's not going to win any awards... For what it's worth it was one of six nominations for Best Short Story in the 1991 Nebula awards [0] and the Stephen O'Regan film adaptation [1] someone else already posted here won the 2006 Grand Prize at the SIFF Science Fiction Short Film Festival [2]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":22150907,"author":"Izkata","month":"2020-01","story":"If we weren’t the first industrial civilization on Earth, would we ever know?","stance":"reference","why":"Short story reminded by AI discussion; video version.","quote":"reminds me of the short story They're Made out of Meat [0] (and a version on youtube [1]).","text":"A universe of what we perceive as AI, reminds me of the short story They're Made out of Meat [0] (and a version on youtube [1]). [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9707954,"author":"tjradcliffe","month":"2015-06","story":"On Disliking Poetry","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"People are made out of meat, and we communicate by flapping peices of meat","text":"This analysis of poetry is like declaring that the purpose of physics is to build perpetual motion machines and then saying we all dislike physics because it fails in this transcendent purpose. The author trots out uncritically the entire corpus of Romantic twaddle, swathed in sufficient technical language that most poeple will feel too intimidated to challenge it. But twaddle it is. The failure of poetry in the modern world is a result of the rejection of concrete experience as the foundational matter of poems. This has been accompanied by the abandonment of the physical rhythmicities of speech (including rhyme) as the basis of poetic structure. There's nothing transcendent about good poetry. People are made out of meat, and we communicate by flapping peices of meat: [VIDEO] Poetry reflects our meat-based lives, the physicality of our meat. When we abandon that, when we abandon concrete, sensual experience for floating abstraction, as the Romantics did, or for purely internal emotional states as modern confessional free verse does, we abandon the very thing that makes poetry possible and necessary: meat. Poetry is speech, specifically speech where the rhythmical structure dominates the gramatical structure. The rhythmical structure can be strong and overt (rap, Kipling, etc) or subtle (even the best confessional free verse manages this at times, or the L A N G U A G E poets at their most surreal, although I don't have any use for their political programme.) The rhythmical st"},{"id":34422902,"author":"qup","month":"2023-01","story":"Let's build GPT: from scratch, in code, spelled out by Andrej Karpathy","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"We'll selectively breed humans until one can just compute it in...meat[0].","text":"Outside tools are not required. We'll selectively breed humans until one can just compute it in...meat[0]. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31796541,"author":"bryanrasmussen","month":"2022-06","story":"Meat made from recycled carbon dioxide","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"we might be able to fib and convince them the meat is made out of carbon dioxide?","text":"So, in a possible future overhearing of two aliens discussing us being made out of meat [VIDEO] we might be able to fib and convince them the meat is made out of carbon dioxide? Universe, here we come!"},{"id":24237118,"author":"triangleman","month":"2020-08","story":"AI slays top F-16 pilot in DARPA dogfight simulation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"They're made out of meat","text":"They're made out of meat [VIDEO] By the way, I agree with you, it is unsettling to hear human beings reduced to such terms."},{"id":17955254,"author":"paulmd","month":"2018-09","story":"How Discord Handles Two and Half Million Concurrent Voice Users Using WebRTC","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"They talk by flapping their meat at each other.","text":"> \"They actually do talk, then. They use words, ideas, concepts?\" > \"Oh, yes. Except they do it with meat.\" > \"I thought you just told me they used radio.\" > \"They do, but what do you think is on the radio? Meat sounds. You know how when you slap or flap meat it makes a noise? They talk by flapping their meat at each other. They can even sing by squirting air through their meat.\" [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["46ehrFk-gLk",1],["RqQhWitJ1As",1],["spUNpyF58BY",1],["nT-TGvYOBpI",1],["Bkm2Vfj42FY",1],["FfDa8tR25dk",1],["2gFsOoKAHZg",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1],["5f9R9MtkpqM",1],["QEJ9HrZq7Ro",1],["Ylomy1Aw9Hk",1],["4loZGYqaZ7I",1],["eXR1olg_I0w",1],["fCUTX1jurJ4",1],["CaPgDQkmqqM",1],["14wqBA5Q1yc",1],["tcdVC4e6EV4",1],["PGLYEDpNu60",1],["MBnnXbOM5S4",1],["BYRTvoZ3Rho",1]]},"9LMr5XTgeyI":{"wy":"Referenced as a fun side tangent whenever the name Tiffany comes up in a thread.","m":[{"id":31252039,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2022-05","story":"Considered \"18+\"","stance":"rec","why":"CGP Grey investigates the origin of the name Tiffany","quote":"Wait till you find out about \"Tiffany","text":"Wait till you find out about \"Tiffany\" [VIDEO] (CGP Grey) (spoiler: he couldn't find out where it came from, and did this video on his research to explain more: [LINK] )"},{"id":38734282,"author":"throw0101a","month":"2023-12","story":"In the long run, we're all Dad","stance":"reference","why":"CGP Grey on the name Tiffany","quote":"See also \"The Tale of Tiffany\" from CGP Grey","text":"> Sad to see \"Jennifer\" listed as a \"newer name\". See also \"The Tale of Tiffany\" from CGP Grey: * [VIDEO]"},{"id":37362722,"author":"colinchartier","month":"2023-09","story":"The Mystery of the Bloomfield Bridge","stance":"reference","why":"Video about the origin of the name Tiffany","quote":"Reminds me of this video about the origin of the name Tiffany","text":"Reminds me of this video about the origin of the name Tiffany: [VIDEO] \"I didn't fly across the Atlantic to... Of course I did\""},{"id":31436640,"author":"yabones","month":"2022-05","story":"Top passwords used by business executives","stance":"reference","why":"Background on the name Tiffany","quote":"","text":"Interesting that the top name used in passwords is Tiffany. Culturally it has a strong connection to the 80s decade, which points to two specific demographics: ~35 year olds, who might be in a relationship to one, or ~55 year olds who might have one as a daughter. [VIDEO] Makes me wonder if it's time to start generating rainbow tables for the top female names of the 90s and 2000s soon..."}],"r":[["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1],["uxykI30fS54",1]]},"FBeSKL9zVro":{"wy":"HN points to it as the authoritative explanation of why fractal wood burning with MOTs is deadly and how to reduce the risk.","q":{"x":"BigCliveDotCom has an excellent video about this","i":31946874,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31945531,"author":"dazzawazza","month":"2022-07","story":"YouTube removes criticism of dangerous fractal wood burning, but leaves up tips","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why fractal wood burning is dangerous and how to reduce risk","quote":"Big Clive has a good video on why this is dangerous and how to make it more safe.","text":"Big Clive has a good video on why this is dangerous and how to make it more safe. Note: not safe safe, just more safe. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31946874,"author":"geocrasher","month":"2022-07","story":"YouTube removes criticism of dangerous fractal wood burning, but leaves up tips","stance":"rec","why":"Excellent video on the dangers of fractal wood burning","quote":"BigCliveDotCom has an excellent video about this","text":"BigCliveDotCom has an excellent video about this: \"The most deadly project on the Internet\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":39857005,"author":"1letterunixname","month":"2024-03","story":"We made a hot dog talk with RF [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among related classics on dangerous electrical hacks","quote":"Related classics","text":"Related classics: (2019) BigClive overclocks a 120v Presto hot dogger to 250v [LINK] (2019) Veratasium grape microwave plasma [LINK] (2022) BigClive The dangers of microwave transformers [VIDEO] (2016) BigClive Electrocution risk of cheap USB chargers [LINK] (2020) ElectroBOOM proves 5G isn't going to give you cancer or eat your children [LINK]"},{"id":31648849,"author":"JohnTHaller","month":"2022-06","story":"What are Lichtenberg figures, and how do we make them?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"please remember that it can be (and has been) deadly.","text":"If you or someone you know are thinking about trying this, please remember that it can be (and has been) deadly. [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"JzIWdJVP-wo":{"wy":"HN mentions it for its Perl 6 content, though some note the delivery is less lively than Wall's usual talks.","q":{"x":"here's a Google Tech Talk from last year, which is nowhere near as funny as Larry usually is, but is still quite informative","i":551330,"y":"2009"},"m":[{"id":551330,"author":"SwellJoe","month":"2009-04","story":"Lisp's reputation is so bad that many people don't even take a look at Lisp","stance":"rec","why":"Informative Larry Wall talk on Perl 6 grammar and macros.","quote":"here's a Google Tech Talk from last year, which is nowhere near as funny as Larry usually is, but is still quite informative","text":"Maybe Perl 6 will have meta-language power enough to equal the macro system? Not only does it have full-on raging macros, you can redefine the languages own syntax inline. Check out one of Larry's recent talks about STD.pm (here's a Google Tech Talk from last year, which is nowhere near as funny as Larry usually is, but is still quite informative: [VIDEO] )for a real \"when you see it, you'll shit bricks\" moment. Perl 6 is far more intimidating than I ever realized. They seem to have looked at everything good about Perl (and a handful of other languages) and said, \"Let's do a lot more of that.\" It's somewhat scary how much more of \"that\" they've done. So, there's macros from Lisp, lazy evaluation from Haskell, redefine-able grammar and syntax from I dunno where, a new regex language, etc. It takes TMTOWTDI to obscene new heights (kinda like Lisp). If power is the sole determining factor in what language you choose, there's a strong case to be made that Perl 6 is the one you want. I don't know that I entirely buy that (if I did, I guess I'd be using Lisp), but I'm hopeful that the real result of Perl 6 will be a language as capable as Lisp with a community and library culture as strong as Perl 5."},{"id":16154922,"author":"raiph","month":"2018-01","story":"The art of the error message","stance":"reference","why":"Larry Wall talks a bit about error messages.","quote":"He talks a bit about error messages.","text":"There's a youtube of a 2008 Google talk at [VIDEO] He talks a bit about error messages. I found that by searching for `message` in the transcript generated by selecting the 'Open transcript\" menu option of the ... button. Hth."},{"id":1279548,"author":"jerf","month":"2010-04","story":"Why Perl 6 is different","stance":"reference","why":"Content on Perl 6 internals interesting though delivery dull.","quote":"Actually, to be honest, I don't recommend it, I found it surprisingly dull in delivery (sorry, Larry), but the contents were very interesting.","text":"There's a presentation that Larry Wall gave at Google that has a lot to do with the internals of Perl 6: [VIDEO] Actually, to be honest, I don't recommend it, I found it surprisingly dull in delivery (sorry, Larry), but the contents were very interesting. I actually think describing Perl 6 as \"having macros\" is accurate, but gives the wrong impression. It doesn't \"have\" macros, it's made of macros. The whole language is made of macros and you are nearly begged to extend it with more grammar. Or you can throw theirs away and write your own for something. Or you can throw theirs away, take some of it as a base, and extend it. It's a wild, wacky experiment that needs to be done. Whatever happens, we'll learn a lot. My intuition says \"collapses under its own complexity\" (especially after trying to add a few grammar-modifying CPAN modules) is the most likely result, but I fully support with no sarcasm someone trying this out in case my intuition is wrong. It's not just Lisp redux, it's something new."}],"r":[["KTJs-0EInW8",1]]},"Kp7FkLBRpKg":{"wy":"HN links it as the primary source for Assange's remarks hinting at Seth Rich as the leak source.","m":[{"id":18469363,"author":"sbliny","month":"2018-11","story":"Prosecutors Have Prepared Indictment of Julian Assange, a Filing Reveals","stance":"rec","why":"Assange in his own words on a murdered DNC staffer","quote":"It's a very interesting interview regardless.","text":"I don't know about that. Assange alludes it was Seth Rich (calls him out by name) and says: \"Whistle blowers go through significant efforts to get us materials and often significant risks. There's a 27 year old who works for the DNC that was shot in the back, murdered, just two weeks ago for unknown reasons as he was walking down the street in Washington...\" [VIDEO] Right from his mouth, not Russian propaganda - unless you're suggesting Assange himself is a Russian propagandist. It's a very interesting interview regardless."},{"id":24286460,"author":"onedognight","month":"2020-08","story":"US indictments of piracy group members throw top-tier piracy world into chaos","stance":"reference","why":"Assange alluding to Seth Rich as source","quote":"Here he is alluding to it[2].","text":"Which part? Dotcom claimed personal knowledge that Seth Rich gave the hacked emails to Wikileaks? Here he’s saying it [1]. That Assange did the same? Here he is alluding to it[2]. Mueller and the recent Senate Intelligence Committee Report said Seth Rich has nothing to do with it[3]. Or do you think the Seth Rich conspiracy didn’t come from the Russian state? Here’s a story pointing out that it came from the Russian SVR[4]. So now we are left with the question, why would Dotcom claim personal knowledge of a Russian State created conspiracy theory that provided cover for the Assange and the GRU? He sounds like a person of interest to me. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":19642400,"author":"belltaco","month":"2019-04","story":"Julian Assange Deserves First Amendment Protection","stance":"reference","why":"Interview where Assange announced a reward over Seth Rich's death","quote":"There's a full video somewhere of this.","text":">Assange is not responsible for the conspiracy theories that other people generate from his information. There's a full video somewhere of this. [LINK] Edit: The video, I think [VIDEO] He also announced a 20 or 25K dollar reward for information leading to Seth Rich's murder being solved, in order to further imply he was the leaker. >I also fully intend on \"election meddling\" by sharing my opinions on the internet. As should you. It's on you to prove why this is bad for the american people. The DNC and Podesta email leaks were probably worth a billion or so dollars in campaign money influence. That's not even counting the IRA trolls. You and me and probably all of HN readers combined couldn't come up to such a level of influence. I believe it was done in bad faith by Russia and Assange in order to divide and destabilize the US, regardless of the outcome of the elections. The IRA tried to amplify both the extremes, and heavily pushed actual fake news including Pizzagate."},{"id":15820643,"author":"juliangoldsmith","month":"2017-11","story":"Elite colleges are making it easy for conservatives to dislike them","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as Assange nearly naming Seth Rich as source","quote":"","text":"Nixon (most likely) wasn't complicit in the break-in, he covered it up after he learned of it. In any case, the alleged collusion isn't Watergate. The coziness of the investigators with President Trump's political enemies and the amount of false information that the media has reported should be cause to question the purpose of the investigation. As far as the DNC \"hack\", the evidence the Russia did it was a hasty, blatant forgery [0]. Julian Assange and Wikileaks have done literally everything short of outright claiming that Seth Rich was their source [1][2]. EDIT: The comment chain has gotten too long for replies, so I'll have to reply here. @dragonwriter: The Robert Mueller, the special investigator, has ties to James Comey [3]. James Comey has been part of three investigations on the Clintons (the Whitewater scandal, the Marc Rich pardon, and the email server); in two of those, he personally was responsible for dropping the charges, both times with little explanation. I'd consider that to be suspicious at best. The fact that Mueller hasn't recused himself, as a friend of one of the witnesses (Comey) in the investigation, is good reason to question the validity of the investigation. I'll concede that the point about the media wasn't a good one. A better one would have been that the Russia investigation is based on the Guccifer 2.0 forgery and a fabricated dossier by Fusion GPS. The Fusion GPS dossier was paid for first by Paul Singer, an anti-Trump conservative, and later b"},{"id":13811866,"author":"sparkling","month":"2017-03","story":"CIA malware and hacking tools","stance":"reference","why":"Interview where Assange hints at the source","quote":"in a other interview has hinted that the source may be Seth Rich [2]","text":"There is no hard evidence where the DNC/Podesta leaks came from. However, Julian Assange has repeatedly said that the source is not the russian goverment or a affiliated state party [1] and in a other interview has hinted that the source may be Seth Rich [2], a former DNC staff member that was murdered in Washington DC. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12261538,"author":"DominikR","month":"2016-08","story":"WikiLeaks offers $20,000 for information on former DNC staffer's murder","stance":"reference","why":"Assange interview hinting at Seth Rich as source","quote":"Assange has clearly suggested in a interview recently that DNC staffer Seth Rich was indeed the person responsible for the DNC leak.","text":"Assange has clearly suggested in a interview recently that DNC staffer Seth Rich was indeed the person responsible for the DNC leak. (and not the Russians as the DNC would like us to believe) [VIDEO] He was shot dead in a \"robbery\" where nothing was stolen from him."}]},"Ne1ApyqSvm0":{"wy":"Cited as a firsthand account of 6502 origins and of how engineers were treated at Motorola and Commodore.","q":{"x":"You might also like the Computer History Museum interview w/Bill Mensch","i":27424218,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27424218,"author":"vidarh","month":"2021-06","story":"6502 “Illegal” Opcodes Demystified","stance":"rec","why":"Further interview with Bill Mensch on 6502 origins.","quote":"You might also like the Computer History Museum interview w/Bill Mensch","text":"Most of what I know about that comes from Brian Bagnall's books (e.g. Commodore: A company on the edge). It's covered in the first chapter. I don't know of any preserved documents - if they exist they'd belong to Motorola, and I'd expect the leavers would have preferred to ditch any such documents (though part of the reason for the settlement with Motorola was that one of the engineers stupidly taking 6800 documentation with him). This [1] summarizes it. Here [2] is an interview by Computer History Museum of Chuck Peddle. It matches up with the first chapters in Bagnalls book very closely. From about 50 minutes in he talks about his meetings w/Motorola customers that told him the 6800 was too expensive. From about 58m in they talk about basically setting a size metric for the chip to meet a low enough price point. So the starting point was what they could fit in small enough space. You might also like the Computer History Museum interview w/Bill Mensch [3] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20670488,"author":"rasz","month":"2019-08","story":"Commodore 900","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates Commodore culture of mistreating engineers.","quote":"Those two videos touch on the Commodore culture of curb stomping until only the weak and dumb survive","text":"Tramiel had a habit of firing good engineers 3 months after product release. Either directly, or by screwing them by moving to useless positions or taking bonuses away until people 'got the message'. Those who didnt get what was going on or didnt care about money/dignity were the ones who stayed as long term employees. Jack had an eye for suckers and people putting passion before common sense and self respect. Whole C64 design team was effed out of promised bonuses and fired/left. People like Bruce Crockett (manufacturing), Al Charpentier (VIC) and Robert Yannes (SID) went to start Ensoniq (later sold to Creative), Chuck Peddle went to Apple, Bill Mensch hung around for two years of abuse. Same with Amiga team (Jay,RJ,Needle). Those two videos touch on the Commodore culture of curb stomping until only the weak and dumb survive: Oral History of Chuck Peddle [LINK] Oral History of William David \"Bill\" Mensch Jr. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11328418,"author":"rasz_pl","month":"2016-03","story":"My Passion Was My Weak Spot","stance":"reference","why":"Life story of Bill Mensch, mistreated by Motorola and Tramiel.","quote":"Reminds me of Bill Mensch (PIA,6502,65816) life story","text":"Reminds me of Bill Mensch (PIA,6502,65816) life story: [VIDEO] he also was very passionate which led to getting screwed by Motorola management, some pointless fake consulting firm, and grand daddy of scammers Jack Tramiel. He worked his whole life for other peoples success :(."}],"r":[["enHF9lMseP8",1],["bS5P_LAqiVg",1]]},"RBBiri3CD6w":{"wy":"Whenever HN asks whether you can program with steno, this is the go-to demo showing it working.","q":{"x":"There was a nice demo of using stenography for programming by one of the Open Steno Project developers","i":12771968,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12771968,"author":"walrus","month":"2016-10","story":"The Open Steno Project","stance":"rec","why":"Demo of using stenography for programming","quote":"There was a nice demo of using stenography for programming by one of the Open Steno Project developers","text":"There was a nice demo of using stenography for programming by one of the Open Steno Project developers: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37345063,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2023-08","story":"Show HN: I automated 1/2 of my typing","stance":"reference","why":"JavaScript steno coding video from years earlier","quote":"Some random YouTube video, JavaScript steno seven years ago","text":"The Plover / OpenSteno founder Mirabai Knight was working on Python steno a decade ago: [LINK] Longer video eight years ago: [LINK] Some random YouTube video, JavaScript steno seven years ago: [VIDEO] Steno coding in Ruby, four months ago: [LINK]"},{"id":24186586,"author":"morinted","month":"2020-08","story":"Art of chording","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own scripted coding demo","quote":"I'm the author of Art of Chording—I program full-time with steno in JavaScript","text":"I'm the author of Art of Chording—I program full-time with steno in JavaScript (working mainly with React.) I'd love ideas on how to demonstrate coding in steno. I struggle with it sometimes because the slowest part about coding is not the input rate… it's the brain. I guess if people are looking to code \"quicker\"… it's not the rate of input that one would want to explore. I will say that writing comments became a lot easier when the words started to just flow onto the screen. Here are my existing videos: Unscripted: [LINK] Scripted: [VIDEO] Looking forward to any suggestions on how to improve or even just the big open questions you'd want answered on this subject."},{"id":22979615,"author":"DavidVoid","month":"2020-04","story":"Time is the only real currency we have","stance":"reference","why":"Example of writing JavaScript on a steno keyboard","quote":"Here's an example of someone writing some javascript code with a steno keyboard","text":"You can make custom chords for special chars and type them just as quickly as any other \"word\". Here's an example of someone writing some javascript code with a steno keyboard: [VIDEO] I don't use it myself but I assume that it would be faster than regular QWERTY typing, but only once you've used it for several years."},{"id":21490316,"author":"iod","month":"2019-11","story":"Open Steno Project: Bringing Stenography to Everyone","stance":"reference","why":"Example of programming FizzBuzz with steno","quote":"Here is an example video someone did of using steno for programming a simple FizzBuzz","text":"(Repost of previous comment from about a month ago [LINK] as it seemed relevant, although I haven't progressed much in my proficiency yet) I have begun the path to learning stenography. Steno involves chording, or pressing multiple keys at once. Multiple keys at once means greatly improving the information density of when you type allowing professionals to type 240 words per minute (realtime), which is just not possible on single-key-at-a-time keyboards. Unfortunately most commonly available, non-gaming keyboards do not natively support multiple keys at once which is also known as n-key rollover (nkro). I ended up buying a pre-assembled, fully opensource hacker keyboard Ergodox EZ⁰, and have a custom layout firmware that matches up with the open steno project¹ . From here I am using Querty Steno² to practice my chording. Here is an example video someone did of using steno for programming a simple FizzBuzz on a different keyboard on YouTube³. In my opinion, if anyone is looking to really take their typing to the next level, chording is the only way and Dvorak/Colmak/single-key-at-a-time-layouts will never really get you there. ⁰ [LINK] ¹ [LINK] ² [LINK] ³ [VIDEO]"},{"id":21233836,"author":"iod","month":"2019-10","story":"Relearning to Type","stance":"reference","why":"Example of programming FizzBuzz with steno","quote":"Here is an example video someone did of using steno for programming a simple FizzBuzz","text":"I have begun the path to learning stenography. Steno involves chording, or pressing multiple keys at once. Multiple keys at once means greatly improving the information density of when you type allowing professionals to type 240 words per minute (realtime), which is just not possible on single-key-at-a-time keyboards. Unfortunately most commonly available, non-gaming keyboards do not natively support multiple keys at once which is also known as n-key rollover (nkro). I ended up buying a pre-assembled, fully opensource hacker keyboard Ergodox EZ⁰, and have a custom layout firmware that matches up with the open steno project¹ . From here I am using Querty Steno² to practice my chording. Here is an example video someone did of using steno for programming a simple FizzBuzz on a different keyboard on YouTube³. In my opinion, if anyone is looking to really take their typing to the next level, chording is the only way and Dvorak/Colmak/single-key-at-a-time-layouts will never really get you there. ⁰ [LINK] ¹ [LINK] ² [LINK] ³ [VIDEO]"}]},"S9kn8_oztsA":{"wy":"HN cites it as a first-hand account of Microsoft's QA cuts and reliance on automated tests in threads about buggy Windows updates.","q":{"x":"Hear about it first hand from an ex windows software tester","i":41003356,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":25483297,"author":"game_the0ry","month":"2020-12","story":"Windows 10 20H2: ChkDsk damages filesystem on SSDs with KB4592438 installed","stance":"rec","why":"Ex-Microsoft tester says QA layoffs led to unstable Windows updates.","quote":"MS basically laid off the staff that tested Windows, and much of it is automated nowadays","text":"This article immediately reminded me of a video I saw a while ago.[1] The long and short - MS basically laid off the staff that tested Windows, and much of it is automated nowadays (I bet the MBA educated executive at MS who was responsible for that got PHAT bonus, at the expense of Windows customers and laid off testing staff, of course). Seems like it happened in 2015, which is why it seems that windows updates have been more unstable in the last couple of years.[2] I really want to like windows, but Apple's M1 and Linux's community make it hard to ever love windows again. Oh well, I guess I will be using Macbooks for the rest of my life. Which, actually, isn't so bad I guess. FWIW - I would say this is unacceptable on microsoft's part. Windows is the default operating system of corporate america, department of defense, etc. Imagine if an update would brick Wells Fargo or the US Army. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":41003356,"author":"alt227","month":"2024-07","story":"CrowdStrike Update: Windows Bluescreen and Boot Loops","stance":"reference","why":"First-hand account from an ex Windows tester about QA cuts.","quote":"Hear about it first hand from an ex windows software tester","text":"> Microsoft did not \"cut off most of its QA\" Yes they did. Hear about it first hand from an ex windows software tester: [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":28967507,"author":"0dayz","month":"2021-10","story":"It's not your imagination – Microsoft's sofware has gone downhill","stance":"reference","why":"Says Microsoft relied too much on tests, not real usage.","quote":"MSFT relying too much on tests and not real-life usage[1]","text":"Whenever I see these kind of headlines, I expect some deep analysis of said topic, but it then it disappoints with your average article. With that said I remember Barnacules Nerdgasm talking about this and essentially it came down to MSFT relying too much on tests and not real-life usage[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["oVfHeWTKjag",1]]},"_-agl0pOQfs":{"wy":"Mostly posted as a joke in threads on magnets and physics, echoing the song's 'magnets' lyric.","m":[{"id":39391761,"author":"user_7832","month":"2024-02","story":"The existence of a new kind of magnet has been confirmed","stance":"rec","why":"Song with a nice groove and nature-appreciating lyrics.","quote":"It's got a nice groove, the lyrics are actually quite appreciative of nature","text":"I highly recommend watching its video if anyone hasn't. It's got a nice groove, the lyrics are actually quite appreciative of nature... but the magnet line is a real weird inclusion. Link - [VIDEO]"},{"id":34362127,"author":"notjulianjaynes","month":"2023-01","story":"Niron Magnetics – powerful, rare earth-free permanent magnets","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Off topic and probably another rule: Insane Clown Posse agrees with you. \"Fuckin' magnets, how do they work?\" From their hit single, \"Miracles,\" on a Psychopathic Records. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19368146,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2019-03","story":"Tesla – Introducing V3 Supercharging","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Last I heard, electric fields and magnetic fields were inextricably related. [LINK] >The power of the Tesla coil lies in a process called electromagnetic induction, i.e., a changing magnetic field creates an electric potential that compels current to flow. Conversely, flowing electric current generates a magnetic field. When electricity flows through a wound up coil of wire, it generates a magnetic field that fills the area around the coil in a particular pattern, shown with lines below: [...] >Eventually so much charge has accumulated that it breaks down the electrical neutrality of the air in the middle of the spark gap. The circuit closes for a fleeting second and a huge amount of current blasts out of the capacitor and through the coils. This produces a very strong magnetic field in the primary coil. Which leads us to ask: Fucking magnets: How do they work? [VIDEO]"},{"id":6789711,"author":"joshguthrie","month":"2013-11","story":"Things I Wish Someone Had Told Me When I Was Learning How to Code","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I'm a little bit further on that point and I think you should slow down a bit. (All that follows is my humble opinion, apply grain of salt and critique as needed) Writing a framework for the first time is hard. You said you don't really know what happens server-side and this may get in your way. A framework is often designed to address specific problems/needs in a specific context. These problems/needs don't have to be shared by anyone else, it could just be you thinking \"It's hard to build RESTful urls, I should build something to do it for me\" and have a CLI util or a script handler that would register them for you in your app server. And some can be everyday problems: \"F*ckin' databases, how do they work?\"[1]. However, you need to know these problems to address them. Which is a part of my long-term feud with frameworks: \"If you always use a framework, you will never have (too much) problems and will never learn how to solve them\" (writing my own active-record-like ORM for node.js is a lot of fun actually). Which is why my advice to you is: Don't write a framework. Instead, write an app, get stuck a lot of times and find a way. Identify your problems. Then write a different app. This time, look at what you are doing the same way, what problems you are solving another way and what you'd like your framework to automate. There, for this second app, you can start writing your framework because you'll have a better idea of what you need to address. You don't really need to make "}]},"agMrewRJTow":{"wy":"HN points to it to explain how steer-by-wire is implemented and why variable steering ratio is its benefit.","q":{"x":"It's explained really well here","i":38485522,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38485522,"author":"gambiting","month":"2023-12","story":"Tesla Cybertruck Pricing and Specs","stance":"rec","why":"Explains variable steering ratio benefit of steer-by-wire.","quote":"It's explained really well here","text":">>What's the upside? A much easier to manouver car, because you can get true variable speed ratio steering. It's explained really well here: [VIDEO] Tbh I still think it's a bit of a silly idea. But it's not completely without its merits(the one done by Tesla was though - that was completely entirely pointless)."},{"id":39529251,"author":"gambiting","month":"2024-02","story":"Apple to wind down electric car effort after decadelong odyssey","stance":"reference","why":"Shows no mechanical connection and triple redundancy.","quote":"There isn't a mechanical connection of any kind - the car just relies on triple(!) Backup for every component","text":"Does it have a backup link? According to this video: [VIDEO] There isn't a mechanical connection of any kind - the car just relies on triple(!) Backup for every component, including three fully independent power sources."},{"id":38480104,"author":"ed_balls","month":"2023-11","story":"Tesla Cybertruck Pricing and Specs","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how steer-by-wire is implemented.","quote":"Steer by wire is ok. We had all pedals by wire for ages.","text":"Steer by wire is ok. We had all pedals by wire for ages. Hopefully they won't screw up the implementation again [VIDEO]"},{"id":35520723,"author":"xyst","month":"2023-04","story":"I Fixed a Parasitic Drain on My Car in 408 Days","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"there are so many additional motors, sensors, and actuators to simulate the same experience as mechanical based steering","text":"Personal vehicles are getting way too complicated now. Too many electronic components that can be points of failure. Recently viewed a video on “steer by wire yoke” [VIDEO] It’s very interesting but there are so many additional motors, sensors, and actuators to simulate the same experience as mechanical based steering. If you lose electronic assistance in traditional mechanical based steering, you have to apply more force as a driver and makes the experience difficult. What happens if I lose one or more components in this “steer by wire yoke”? Seems like as a driver you are shit out of luck. If you are a pedestrian, bye bye. All I want is a walkable city with public and alternative forms of transportation. All of these “car innovations” are mostly useless."}],"r":[["8FpigqfcvlM",1],["92w5doU68D8",1],["Ufmu1WD2TSk",1]]},"d99_h30swtM":{"wy":"Linked as the accessible explanation of how moire navigation lights work.","q":{"x":"the one that Tom Scott visits here","i":39319896,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":39319896,"author":"wgrover","month":"2024-02","story":"Light characteristic","stance":"rec","why":"Tom Scott visits moire navigation beacon","quote":"the one that Tom Scott visits here","text":"You might also find moiré-effect navigation beacons interesting, like the one that Tom Scott visits here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":49318416,"author":"notpushkin","month":"2026-08","story":"Guiding Ships with Moire Patterns","stance":"reference","why":"Tom Scott explains the guiding arrows","quote":"Tom Scott has made a video on those","text":"It is used and I guess it works largely the same way as in author’s first picture: you get arrows pointing towards where you should correct your course to. Tom Scott has made a video on those: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41418686,"author":"dublinben","month":"2024-09","story":"Using the moiré effect to show different arrows to each observer (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This is nothing but blogspam of the embedded Tom Scott video","text":"This is nothing but blogspam of the embedded Tom Scott video: [VIDEO] . This article adds nothing that is not already included in the video itself."}]},"fRMMSyCcTDI":{"wy":"Cited as an approachable hobbyist look at RDEs in threads on detonation-powered rockets.","m":[{"id":43994202,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2025-05","story":"In the US, a rotating detonation rocket engine takes flight","stance":"rec","why":"Lab visit with explanations, animations and slow-mos of an RDE prototype.","quote":"at least the video is informative.","text":"Integza made a YouTube video[1] where he visited a lab where they had a RDE, and includes some nice explanations, animations and slow-mos of the prototype in action. His channel has a certain flavor, but at least the video is informative. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44397646,"author":"ben_w","month":"2025-06","story":"A Review of Aerospike Nozzles: Current Trends in Aerospace Applications","stance":"reference","why":"Creator was unsure he achieved a rotating detonation wave.","quote":"I'm not sure if I got a rotating detonation wave","text":"I know Integza tried , but did he ever confirm his engine was doing a rotating detonation? He said near the end of the video 2 years ago, \"I'm not sure if I got a rotating detonation wave\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":33047908,"author":"zackmorris","month":"2022-10","story":"Japan tests explosion-powered rocket for the first time in space (2021)","stance":"reference","why":"Hobbyist video on rotating detonation engines, prompting personal reflection.","quote":"I was just watching a hobbyist video about rotating detonation engines (RDEs)","text":"That's funny, I was just watching a hobbyist video about rotating detonation engines (RDEs): [VIDEO] I live vicariously through channels like Integza and Tom Stanton. I've wanted to do some of this stuff for over 20 years, specifically electrohydrodynamic (EHD) engines, but spent nearly the entirety of the last 2 decades merely surviving. It's a strange feeling to watch the world make basically no progress over many years, then suddenly go through an inflection point where suddenly everything is solved and there's no way to catch up. Like with flying cars, reusable rockets, AI.. I dunno, I just feel really inspired, but am also mourning the passing of my own life and all that wasted potential. These are all great inventions, super simple, easy to build, but why the heck were we forced to do everything the hard way for so long and still not make rent? These individual projects are really cool, but I worry that we aren't solving the fundamental problem of underemployment that forces fish to ride bicycles instead of swimming freely. Still just a rat in a cage and all that."}],"r":[["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HUMGc8hEkpc",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["jhicDUgXyNg",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["ghUs-84NAAU",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["85ZCTuekjGA",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1]]},"joMK1WZjP7g":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as stark evidence that modern cars are far safer than older ones, countering nostalgia for 'built like they used to'.","q":{"x":"This 2009 vs. 1959 offset crash shows how much things have changed","i":6241224,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":14704817,"author":"cwyers","month":"2017-07","story":"EU Parliament calls for longer lifetime for products","stance":"rec","why":"Crash test shows modern cars are safer and more efficient.","quote":"Watch this video of a crash test between a 1959 Chevy and a 2009 Chevy","text":"Watch this video of a crash test between a 1959 Chevy and a 2009 Chevy: [VIDEO] The '59 Chevy gets 13.5 miles per gallon. [1] The '09 Chevy gets 25. [2] Yes, modern cars are more difficult to maintain than older cars -- they require more specialized equipment to maintain, parts are more difficult to replace, so on and so forth. Modern cars are also incredibly more fuel efficient and safer to ride in, and much of that is enabled by the same engineering efforts that make cars more difficult to maintain. 1) [LINK] 2) [LINK]"},{"id":12726392,"author":"saulrh","month":"2016-10","story":"How fear of nuclear power is hurting the environment","stance":"reference","why":"Used as analogy: old reactors versus old cars.","quote":"","text":"Fukushima was fifty years old. Compared to modern reactors, it's approximately as unsafe as a car from 1971 is compared to a modern car [1]. Which is to say, it's pants-shittingly unsafe and we should've replaced it 20 years ago but idiots say we're not allowed to \"because it's dangerous oooh noooo\". If you want to badmouth nuclear by spouting off its worst-case scenarios rather than its average or expected scenarios, I invite you to do the same for the alternatives. Coal's worst case causes the Great Smog and Beijing and New Delhi. Oil's worst case causes the Exxon Valdez and Piper Alpha and the Texas City Refinery explosion and oh yeah global climate change. Hydro's worst case is the Banqiao Dam burst, which directly killed 200,000 people and forced 11 million out of their homes. Badmouthing nuclear over fukushima and chernobyl is like badmouthing cars because a Fluorine tanker crashed in downtown Seattle around 1935. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11057344,"author":"omegaham","month":"2016-02","story":"Driving Is Losing Its Allure for More Americans","stance":"reference","why":"Basic modern cars survive crashes much better.","quote":"","text":"> 2. More prone to breakage due to complexity I agree with a lot of the newer features, but it's a fact that old luxury features are now standard, (and more reliable than the old luxury features) and the regular features are more reliable than ever. Transmissions last longer, brakes last longer, engines last longer, tires last longer. Hell, tire technology has gotten so good that you actually have to do something to get a flat. It used to be that you just got flats every now and then. Not anymore. Basically, if you want luxury features that will break at about the same rate as the luxury features on cars in the 80s, you can get them. If you want a really basic model car that doesn't have motorized doors and electrically controlled seats, you can get that, too. And it'll get fantastic gas mileage, and it won't kill you in a car crash, either. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7396258,"author":"parfe","month":"2014-03","story":"Why Cars Will Kill 30,000 Americans This Year","stance":"reference","why":"Frontal offset crash of 1959 versus 2009 Chevrolet.","quote":"I know which car I would rather ride in.","text":"[VIDEO] Frontal offset crash test between a 1959 Chevrolet Bel Air vs 2009 Chevrolet Malibu. I know which car I would rather ride in."},{"id":6241224,"author":"ISL","month":"2013-08","story":"Tesla Model S achieves best safety rating of any car ever tested","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how much crash engineering changed from 1959 to 2009.","quote":"This 2009 vs. 1959 offset crash shows how much things have changed","text":"Modern crash engineering is really impressive (that under-car view is really neat!) This 2009 vs. 1959 offset crash shows how much things have changed: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6241340,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2013-08","story":"Tesla Model S achieves best safety rating of any car ever tested","stance":"reference","why":"Comparison tests of classic versus modern vehicles.","quote":"","text":"First: SUVs don't survive crashes particularly well -- they're very prone to rollover, which generally makes for a bad day for the occupant. I've witnessed multiple large-SUV vs. Camry (mid-sized sedan) accidents where the Toyota fared far better (walk away vs. ambulance or worse). Weight can be a factor, especially in multiple-vehicle accidents, where the heavier vehicle simply has more momentum and kinetic energy. In a multiple-vehicle accident, the larger vehicle experiences lower g-forces, and the occupants lower forces. Collisions with a stationary object (the basis for the NHTSA tests) largely takes this factor out of the equation. More mass can _still_ result in better survivability if it means more material around the driver, but here the choice of materials and engineering matter far more. Look at the comparison tests of classic (1950s/1960s) and modern (post 1980 and more recent) vehicles: [VIDEO] I don't know the GVW of the Bel Aire, but I suspect it's rather higher than that of the Malibu. Of course, modern vehicles with poor engineering and materials can also fare badly: [LINK]"},{"id":6242340,"author":"jacquesm","month":"2013-08","story":"Tesla Model S achieves best safety rating of any car ever tested","stance":"reference","why":"Counters claim modern cars crash worse than 1950s cars.","quote":"I don't think that is true.","text":"> On \"keep the car intact\", today's cars do way more badly than those of the 1950's. I don't think that is true. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3315328,"author":"CWuestefeld","month":"2011-12","story":"It's now illegal to throw out rechargeable batteries in New York State","stance":"reference","why":"Old Bel Air driver dummy is killed in crash versus modern Malibu.","quote":"In it, the dummy driver of the big iron Bel Air is pretty thoroughly killed.","text":"lighter, deadlier cars Lighter <> Deadlier For one thing, lighter frequently means \"more agile\", i.e., better able to avoid a crash. But even given a crash, modern engineering can yield great safety with light weight. See the results of a \"Crash Test 1959 Chevrolet Bel Air VS. 2009 Chevrolet Malibu (Frontal Offset)\" [1]. In it, the dummy driver of the big iron Bel Air is pretty thoroughly killed. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"nWTvXbQHwWs":{"wy":"Mentioned as an interesting Lattner conversation whenever Swift, LLVM or language design come up.","q":{"x":"I watched a couple of Lex Fridman podcasts this week with Chris Lattner (creator of Swift) that I thought were interesting","i":29621117,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29621117,"author":"mwattsun","month":"2021-12","story":"Apple’s use of Swift and SwiftUI in iOS 15","stance":"rec","why":"Chris Lattner discussing computing and programming languages.","quote":"I watched a couple of Lex Fridman podcasts this week with Chris Lattner (creator of Swift) that I thought were interesting","text":"I always wondered why the creator of Rust switched to Swift and just now found my answer by Graydon Hoare himself: [LINK] tl;dr it has nothing to do with the relative merits of the two languages --- Edit: I watched a couple of Lex Fridman podcasts this week with Chris Lattner (creator of Swift) that I thought were interesting Chris Lattner: Compilers, LLVM, Swift, TPU, and ML Accelerators | Lex Fridman Podcast #21 [LINK] Chris Lattner: The Future of Computing and Programming Languages | Lex Fridman Podcast #131 [VIDEO]"},{"id":45142698,"author":"tony","month":"2025-09","story":"ML needs a new programming language – Interview with Chris Lattner","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier Lex Fridman episode with Chris Lattner.","quote":"Chris Lattner also has a very good episode on Lex Fridman","text":"Chris Lattner also has a very good episode on Lex Fridman (Episode 381, June 2nd, 2023): - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] He has two other episodes on the show: - [VIDEO] (Episode 131, October 18th, 2020) - [LINK] (Episode 21, May 13th, 2019)"},{"id":25874201,"author":"sxp","month":"2021-01","story":"Ask HN: What companies are you excited about?","stance":"reference","why":"Interview with Chris Lattner about the future of computing.","quote":"RISC-V & SiFive looks like the next step once Moore's Law ends","text":"[VIDEO] is an interview with SiFive's Chris Lattner. RISC-V & SiFive looks like the next step once Moore's Law ends and we need another option to increase perf/watt and perf/$. Creating device-specific chips that drop the unwanted general purpose silicon seems like the way forward."}],"r":[["yCd3CzGSte8",1],["WXuK6gekU1Y",1],["mZyvIHYn2zk",1],["kFT54hO1X8M",1],["5AqbCQuK0gM",1]]},"sqIz-RNUL1g":{"wy":"HN points to it as a prescient talk that discussed, in 2012, much of what Snowden's leaks later confirmed.","q":{"x":"lot of the stuff which was later leaked by Snowden is discussed here","i":9679664,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9679664,"author":"jmkni","month":"2015-06","story":"NSA whistleblower warns of surveillance state","stance":"rec","why":"Binney panel anticipated later Snowden leaks","quote":"lot of the stuff which was later leaked by Snowden is discussed here","text":"A year before Snowden took off to Hong Kong, William Binney was on a panel at Def Con where NSA surveillance was talked about at length, worth a watch - [VIDEO] lot of the stuff which was later leaked by Snowden is discussed here, nice to see Binney vindicated. Interestingly, the NSA facility in Hawaii where Snowden was working at the time is mentioned by name. I often wonder if this Defcon talk inspired him to do what he did."},{"id":20944480,"author":"jmkni","month":"2019-09","story":"Edward Snowden: Permanent Record","stance":"reference","why":"2012 Binney talk possibly inspired Snowden","quote":"I always wondered if he saw this talk and was motivated by it.","text":"I remember the DefCon 20 talk, Bigger Monsters, Weaker Chains with William Binney - [VIDEO] This was in 2012, 2013 was when Snowden did what he did. I always wondered if he saw this talk and was motivated by it."},{"id":5948393,"author":"dram","month":"2013-06","story":"Could Glenn Greenwald go to jail? The law is alarmingly murky","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for NSA collection and secret court queries","quote":"Bamford, Binney and the ACLU at Defcon 2012","text":"The NSA is wholesale collecting data and later using a secret court to authorize them to query their own databases [1]. PRISM is not just an interface. It is officially known as SIGAD US-984XN which means it intercepts and collects electronic communications. [1] Bamford, Binney and the ACLU at Defcon 2012 [VIDEO]"},{"id":4875209,"author":"saber_taylor","month":"2012-12","story":"'Everyone in US under virtual surveillance' - NSA whistleblower","stance":"reference","why":"Similar Defcon talk with Binney and ACLU","quote":"The Defcon video is similar","text":"The Defcon video is similar: [VIDEO] (not sure if this link is bootleg) [LINK] (just the audio) has the ACLU and the same guy if that makes it more legit. The 10 zetabyte storage capacity estimate is based on building size. I think we need more details."}]},"tg5RFeSfBM4":{"wy":"Cited in HN debates on testing and clean architecture as a concrete example of keeping domain code separate from Rails so tests run fast.","q":{"x":"was quite interesting, and argued a complete separation of business logic from the model code","i":13060350,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":13060350,"author":"theptip","month":"2016-11","story":"Test Induced Design Damage? (2014)","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for separating business logic from models for testing.","quote":"was quite interesting, and argued a complete separation of business logic from the model code","text":"I'm interested to hear the patterns that users of MVC frameworks like Rails and Django have adopted when \"fat models\" start to become unwieldy. The video \"Decoupling from Rails\"[1] that's linked in this article was quite interesting, and argued a complete separation of business logic from the model code, to allow the business logic to be tested (and reused) more easily. I've definitely found fat-model code to be tricky to mock and test at times in Python/Django, as mocking out the ORM is gnarly. But if the business logic operates on proxy objects, then it would be much easier to do so. On the other hand, DHH's retort to this approach also makes some good points regarding the introduction of excess complexity[2]. It's a fair point that architectural decisions should not be motivated by the speed of the UTs. But perhaps there are other benefits to the Hexagonal architecture, as well. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":14221211,"author":"theptip","month":"2017-04","story":"The Clean Architecture (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Advocacy for decoupling from the DB to speed tests.","quote":"an advantage touted by advocates of complete decoupling from the DB is that your UT suite can run in O(one minute)","text":"This is a powerful technique, though I'd say it's more useful for the build pipeline than for developers running UTs while coding (since the schema can change more frequently while doing so). To provide a counterpoint though, an advantage touted by advocates of complete decoupling from the DB is that your UT suite can run in O(one minute), rather than O(ten minutes). E.g. see [VIDEO] . I'm not 100% sold on this approach yet (separating your domain objects entirely from the ORM wrapper is an uphill struggle), but it's interesting."},{"id":13876931,"author":"theptip","month":"2017-03","story":"Zen and the Art of Unit Testing","stance":"reference","why":"Weirich's architecture separating DB models from domain models.","quote":"it was an architecture that Weirich suggested","text":"Here's a retort to that argument from the source (Uncle Bob): [LINK] DHH wrote an article with the title \"test induced design damage\", not sure if he coined the term. I think there's a grain of truth there; it is possible, but not necessary, to mangle your architecture to enable testability. The case that DHH was complaining about is quite interesting in itself; it was an architecture that Weirich suggested ( [VIDEO] ) where the framework DB models (in his case Rails) are separated from the Domain Models, to allow you to 1) run your tests of the business logic faster (without spinning up a DB) and 2) decouple your business logic from your choice of framework. I've felt the need for this factoring in my own code, but actually making it happen is tricky, since you lose a lot of the magic that makes your life easier (but that also couples every level of your architecture to the framework's concepts)."},{"id":7708325,"author":"brudgers","month":"2014-05","story":"Ask HN: Which are good computer science problems to work on right now?","stance":"reference","why":"Weirich separating business logic from the framework manually.","quote":"Here's Jim Weirich going about it manually in Rails","text":"Decoupling business logic from web frameworks might be an interesting architectural problem that could use better tooling. Here's Jim Weirich going about it manually in Rails: [VIDEO]"},{"id":7673752,"author":"kentwistle","month":"2014-04","story":"Slow database test fallacy","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on decoupling from Rails, listed among resources.","quote":"Imagine just having a few wrapper classes that spoke to Rails and only having to adapt them.","text":"Am I only the only one that doesn't find 'All tests in 4 minutes, all model tests in 80 seconds' very impressive? It sounds like a really long time to me. You know what could increase the speed dramatically.... decoupling. I also think decoupling phrased in the context of the Rails 2 to Rails 3 upgrade, where pretty much everything changed, makes perfect sense. Imagine just having a few wrapper classes that spoke to Rails and only having to adapt them. Sounds good to me! Bernhardt: Boundaries [LINK] Weirich: Decoupling from Rails [VIDEO] Wynne: Hexagonal Rails [LINK]"}]},"z2_dhUv_CrI":{"wy":"HN brings it up as a funny parody of odd Eastern European cartoons and as a joke about automation or replaced entertainment.","q":{"x":"is absolutely hilarious","i":40781877,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40781877,"author":"troad","month":"2024-06","story":"The case for not sanitising fairy tales","stance":"rec","why":"Hilarious Simpsons parody of weird Eastern European cartoons.","quote":"is absolutely hilarious","text":"The Czechs have historically had a fairly significant animation industry, and the vast majority are very cute, e.g.: [0][1]. There also exist a few stop motion animation films intended for adults, and those can be more arthouse (e.g. Alice). [2] In America, Czech cartoons somewhat unfairly have the reputation for being weird, because a dozen Tom & Jerry episodes were made in Prague in the early 1960s in very weird circumstances, on a shoe-string budget by animators unaccustomed to the very idea of violent cartoons. [3] These were received with some confusion by the American audience in the midst of the Cuban Missile Crisis. From this you then have consequent parodies like The Simpsons' Worker and Parasite , which - though not exactly a fair reflection of Czech animation - is absolutely hilarious. [4] [0] Krtek a telefon / The Little Mole and the Telephone : [LINK] [1] Krtek chemikem / The Little Mole as the Chemist : [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] The story of this is told here: [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47881547,"author":"SyneRyder","month":"2026-04","story":"People Do Not Yearn for Automation","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"my first thought went straight to this","text":"I shouldn't admit it, but when you described a czechoslovakian cartoon about automation, my first thought went straight to this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":33860470,"author":"tzs","month":"2022-12","story":"Moog Dancers Prove That TV Was a Lot More Adventurous in the 70s","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The Simpsons had a bit about cartoons being replaced by weird Eastern European cartoons","text":"> I can't describe the disappointment of rushing home from school to find the Looney Tunes slot on BBC 2 be replaced by a \"cultural exchange\" cartoon from Eastern Europe. Interesting. The Simpsons had a bit about cartoons being replaced by weird Eastern European cartoons [1]. I didn't know that actually happened in real life. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"-C_jPcUkVrM":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as a striking example of humans doing extremely dangerous, precise things for fun.","q":{"x":"Constant pushing of the envelope in a incredibly dangerous sport.","i":26340650,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29897688,"author":"kragen","month":"2022-01","story":"Monumental Record: the lives of the workers building the Great Pyramid of Giza","stance":"reference","why":"Example of people flying like birds.","quote":"Here you can see people flying like birds without being canned in metal tubes","text":"\"Antiquity\" is conventionally considered to end about 1500 years ago, though this really only applies to Europe: [LINK] The reason for this is the very sparse historical record from the European Dark Ages. Scholars in the High Middle Ages like Petrarch could look back on their own recent history, and then a vast gap of mostly unknown, and then the impressive cultures of Antiquity among whose ruins they lived. We know more about the Dark Ages now than we did then but still not that much. Here you can see people flying like birds without being canned in metal tubes: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] It seems likely that we have changed. I suspect Merer's workers, even if they had grown up in modern civilization, would not tolerate being employees, being disarmed, and having their children taken away for education; and probably more of them were dyslexic than in modern populations. But I don't really know. Generally the rule is that domestic animals are stupider, smaller-skulled, more submissive, weaker, more neotenous, less aggressive, fatter, lower in testosterone, less sexually dimorphic, and possessed of smaller teeth than the wild type ( [LINK] ). Modern humans have these traits to a very notable degree compared to, as you allude to, chimpanzees and even Neandertals or Denisovans; but perhaps we have also been substantially domesticated over the last 4500 years, maybe even over the last 2000. Not because of driving cars, but because of having kings. Are we the Eloi? I don't thin"},{"id":26340650,"author":"saberdancer","month":"2021-03","story":"Why Are So Many Base Jumpers Dying? (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the extreme precision and danger of wingsuit flying.","quote":"Constant pushing of the envelope in a incredibly dangerous sport.","text":"[VIDEO] this is why. Constant pushing of the envelope in a incredibly dangerous sport. Judging how fast you are falling and whether you'll end up a meter above or below your target is hard and it probably cannot be predicted in a persistent manner. One time you'll get a gust of wind that pushes you slightly off target and you catch a rock wall or a tree at 100mph+."},{"id":12890694,"author":"onion2k","month":"2016-11","story":"Forget self-driving car anxiety: In the early days human drivers were the fear","stance":"reference","why":"Example of humans doing immensely dangerous things for fun.","quote":"Humans do immensely dangerous things for fun all the time[1].","text":"Statistically too dangerous. Humans do immensely dangerous things for fun all the time[1]. That isn't going to stop. People will continue to drive on private roads pretty much forever. [1] For example [VIDEO]"}]},"-zVgWpVXb64":{"wy":"HN invokes it as a joke or reference whenever voice cloning or voice authentication comes up.","m":[{"id":36799809,"author":"larntz","month":"2023-07","story":"Kevin Mitnick has died","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"At first I thought this was a reference to the movie Sneakers","text":"At first I thought this was a reference to the movie Sneakers ( [VIDEO] ), but after searching it seems Wells Fargo also does this, [LINK] ."},{"id":40095818,"author":"supercheetah","month":"2024-04","story":"VASA-1: Lifelike audio-driven talking faces generated in real time","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That scene from Sneakers would be so different nowadays.","text":"That scene from Sneakers would be so different nowadays. [1] \"My voice is my passport. Verify me.\" [2] 1. [LINK] 2. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36718740,"author":"NoZebra120vClip","month":"2023-07","story":"Hype Will Turn Your Security Key into Junk","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I will not be silent until authentication works like this","text":"I will not be silent until authentication works like this: [VIDEO] Or until it works like this: [LINK] And then logging in will look exactly like this: [LINK]"},{"id":16659463,"author":"fsargent","month":"2018-03","story":"Open Speech Recording","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(Hint: )","text":"Huh. This is strange. It's asking me to say \"My voice is my passport. Authorize me.\" (Hint: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":14877989,"author":"malandrew","month":"2017-07","story":"Inside Andy Rubin's Quest to Create an OS for Everything","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Such a system should prompt two-factor auth.","text":"Such a system should prompt two-factor auth. Once the license plate is recognized, an auth request is pushed to Andy's phone and then he verifies by voice. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14182790,"author":"simcop2387","month":"2017-04","story":"Lyrebird – An API to copy the voice of anyone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"That was one of my first thoughts too [1].","text":"That was one of my first thoughts too [1]. I doubt it currently will do so as it does sound fairly robotic. It does sound closer however than other demos I've heard so i think we're probably on the way. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"0SYpUSjSgFg":{"wy":"It is used only as a joke when threads discuss absurd or unworkable climate fixes.","m":[{"id":35704717,"author":"sillywalk","month":"2023-04","story":"A Heat Shield for the Most Important Ice on Earth","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"seems about as viable as this Futurama solution.","text":"Coating the arctic ice in glass micro-bubbles, seems about as viable as this Futurama solution. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23377541,"author":"dTal","month":"2020-06","story":"UVB radiation was the Devonian-Carboniferous extinction kill mechanism","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"...thus solving the problem once and for all!","text":"\"...thus solving the problem once and for all!\" \"But-\" \"ONCE AND FOR ALL!\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":21024441,"author":"acqq","month":"2019-09","story":"A man who linked CO2 to climate change in 1895","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> And why is it that modifying such a small part of the atmosphere (0.04%) can cause such a huge problem? The modification is invisible to you, but not invisible to the photons which are supposed to leave the atmosphere and cool off the earth a bit. It it were visible to you, it would look like this: [LINK] Regarding the photons of thermal frequencies leaving the Earth atmosphere, or remaining there, the simplest you can imagine is a blanket: if it's cold outside the blanket keeps the heat not leaving the area under the blanket, keeping you warm. Here, the CO2 is actually a \"one-way\" blanket, it doesn't block the incoming radiation : The heat coming from Sun has other frequencies as it comes than the photons blocked that would cool off the Earth. The key is: CO2 is an ink-black \"blanket\" for exactly a part of the radiation that cools off the Earth. It's not too big, but big enough to make in sum that 0.8 C degree change since 1880. Or to produce even more warming in the coming years as we burn always more and we haven't managed to change that. A little more precise: \"Energy arrives from the sun in the form of visible light and ultraviolet radiation . The Earth then emits some of this energy as infrared radiation . Greenhouse gases in the atmosphere 'capture' some of this heat, then re-emit it in all directions - including back to the Earth's surface.\" (There's of course infrared radiation coming from the Sun, but the one making problem is the radiation from the Earth) Scienti"}]},"2cMYfxOFBBM":{"wy":"Cited as footage of the Evergreen controversy in debates about campus politics.","m":[{"id":17167505,"author":"Reedx","month":"2018-05","story":"Pornography and the Butterfly Effect","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> so it seems his appeal is primarily limited to the kinda-sorta-far-right. That's actually not the case (just see what kind of questions he gets from his actual audiences to see for yourself). Though on a surface level some of the alt-right likes him because he pisses off SJWs. But they're mistaken to think he's any kind of ally. He's spent much of his career opposing their views and sees both the alt-right and extreme left as big problems. The idea that he's alt-right is really nonsense. He taught for decades at Harvard and the University of Toronto. As he says himself, you can go and look through his hundreds of hours of lectures and try to find where he's promoted anything alt-right. It's quite the opposite. It's largely a false narrative driven by the extreme left, who are trying to dismiss and ruin him because he's critical of what they're doing. This also happened with the progressive professor Bret Weinstein - [VIDEO] They tried/are trying to destroy him. It's a real problem."},{"id":15176978,"author":"stcredzero","month":"2017-09","story":"We Are At War For A Free And Open Internet","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Evergreen protesters confronting Bret Weinstein","quote":"One of the demonstrators from the Bret Weinstein debacle at Evergreen can be seen and heard on the Vice video","text":"Absolutely no one cares to suppress palatable, well meaning, everyone agrees to it speech. One of the demonstrators from the Bret Weinstein debacle at Evergreen can be seen and heard on the Vice video saying, \"...F### your Free Speech!\" That is pretty much what the demonstrators and those aligned with them did to Bret Weinstein -- all just for saying, Maybe there's a problem when you demand that certain ethnic groups \"absent\" themselves? Why would anyone who stands against racism ask that of a Jewish man? [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":19935831,"author":"manfredo","month":"2019-05","story":"SAT to Add ‘Adversity Score’ That Rates Students’ Hardships","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Public universities aren't immune from going off the deep end regardless of constitutional protection","text":"> That's just religious discrimination cloaked in something less intolerable: academic elitism. I don't necessarily agree with tossing such resumes in the garbage, but it is evidence that universities that enforce religious dogma on their students do suffer a hit to their reputation because of that. > how do you explain the strong performance (and reputation) of private institutions vs. \"state schools\"? Not every private school is Stanford, MIT, etc. Plenty of private schools are shitty for-profit enterprises (especially online universities) that essentially scam customers out of their money. Purely on the basis of return on investment many studies also conclude that public university is better than private universities. Also most reputable private university do respect freedom of speech and expression to a similar degree as public universities. If you factor in the reactions of students, perhaps even better. When Berkeley hosted Milo Y students rioted, smashed up cars, etc. When Stanford hosted Dinesh D'Souza it was very tame with protesters being non-disruptive. Public universities aren't immune from going off the deep end regardless of constitutional protection: [VIDEO] Again, the private schools with good reputations do behave almost as if the First Amendment applied to them. The ones that don't generally aren't reputable. So your question is based on false premise."}]},"9s3EbSKDA3o":{"wy":"HN cites it in Monero and Zcash debates as a critique of decoy-based privacy and its passive and active attacks.","q":{"x":"core argument starts at around 10 minutes.","i":25863232,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":41558400,"author":"madars","month":"2024-09","story":"How Chainalysis  made their way into popular Monero wallets","stance":"reference","why":"Describes multiple attacks on Monero's small ring signature anonymity set","quote":"There are multiple attacks in that video.","text":"There are multiple attacks in that video. IP is one, but a much bigger one is knowing the real output (or maybe real output plus one other output) among 16 ring signature members. They do not explain how they achieved it but one could guess that maybe by just doing a lot of tx themselves - 15 decoys is just way too small and flooding blockchain with transactions all but ensures that someone picking random ring members will pick a lot of your outputs (and thus have little privacy from you). It is also for sure too small for targeted active attacks (e.g., it is not safe to have repeat interactions with the same entity), see [VIDEO] You really want more than 16 ring members, preferably all outputs ever created like in Zcash. FCMP work promises to bring similar privacy to Monero but is years on the roadmap."},{"id":26808935,"author":"ianmiers","month":"2021-04","story":"Bitcoin is a ‘boon for surveillance’, says former CIA director","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Sure. Starting from the academic beginning and then going to commercial usage: Zerocash[0], a paper from 2014 (I'm an author on it), proposes using zksnarks to get a public ledger + privacy with no centralized parties. Instead of identifying the origin of your money on the blockchain and moving it to someone, you just prove in zero-knowledge your payment is correct. This showed strong privacy on a public blockchain was feasible. But slow (2 minutes per payment IIRC) Three years and much work by others later, it takes ~2 to 3 seconds on a Pixel three to make a zk-proof for a payment. This can be optimized down to 1 second fairly easily (on that you have only my assertion currently) This is commercially deployed in Zcash (the above mentioned coin with usability and adoption issues), a few other straight up forks, and a new coin called IronFish. And related protocols are in a few things on Ethereum (e.g., Aztec). If you want to test performance numbers, you can download one of these systems and try it yourself (Nighthawk is a decent mobile wallet for Zcash) Current technical objections (again, beyond criticisms of Zcash as a coin itself) 1) Current ZK proofs require trusted setup. New ZK proofs developed by engineers at Zcash removed this[1]. So its not longer an issue for the technology (or for Zcash once it's deployed) 2) you have to scan the blockchain to get notified of payments. No, this happens to be how Zcash does it. As I said in a separate comment, it's easy to send pay"},{"id":25826020,"author":"gruez","month":"2021-01","story":"With the death of cash, privacy faces a deeply uncertain future","stance":"reference","why":"Explains deanonymizing users across repeated Monero transactions","quote":"A single transaction might be untraceable but over repeated transactions it's possible to deanonymize people with a high degree of confidence.","text":">Monero is a [...] untraceable digital cash that preserves privacy Not exactly. A single transaction might be untraceable but over repeated transactions it's possible to deanonymize people with a high degree of confidence. See: [VIDEO] the good part starts at 10 minutes."},{"id":25863232,"author":"gruez","month":"2021-01","story":"Bitcoincore.org removes Satoshi's whitepaper from website after threats from CSW","stance":"reference","why":"Argues Monero's privacy fails over repeated transactions to the same party","quote":"core argument starts at around 10 minutes.","text":">But for normal usage-as-a-currency, I am a huge believer in XMR (monero), which masks who you're sending money to There was a talk[1] a few years ago that basically argued that this property only really holds if you look at individual transactions in isolation. It basically boils down to: if you make one transaction to a darknet market, it's impossible to know whether you were actually sending to a darknet market, or whether that was just a decoy transaction. However, if you make repeated transactions to a darknet market, the chances that all of your transactions had a darknet market decoy approaches zero, and you'll be considered suspicious. At that point the police can get a warrant to search your house, or put surveillance on you so they can catch you slipping up irl. [1] [VIDEO] core argument starts at around 10 minutes."},{"id":25338981,"author":"ianmiers","month":"2020-12","story":"Zcash Open Major Grants","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on fundamental attacks against decoy-based privacy like Monero's","quote":"The decoy based approaches Monero uses are subject to all kinds of passive and active attacks.","text":"Crypto tribalism aside, you got two technical points fundamentally wrong about the cryptography: 1) snarks are fast now. It takes 3 seconds on a pixel phone for Zcash. 2)The decoy based approaches Monero uses are subject to all kinds of passive and active attacks. Both in practice ( [LINK] ) and just fundamentally: [VIDEO] . There are ways to mitigate some of these problems to a degree. This is why you hear Monero people talk about coin control and churning (both of which can make you stand out in other ways). But the fact that they have to mention that should tell you its far from a perfect solution cryptographically. There's a reason projects go with the large anonymity set approach. And if you're a cryptographer at ConsenSys, you should know that: it's what Ethereum is doing with Aztec. You also should know several projects, including Zcash, are working on trustless setup zk proofs. (Disclosure: I am involved in Zcash.)"}]},"AQ0P7R9CfCY":{"wy":"Linked in threads about the demon core and criticality accidents as a dramatic recreation.","m":[{"id":36943430,"author":"JoeDaDude","month":"2023-07","story":"The Blue Flash: A careless slip led to a fatal accident in the Manhattan Project","stance":"reference","why":"Film dramatization of the fatal accident","quote":"A dramatization of the accident was part of the film Fat Man and Little Boy, clip below","text":"A dramatization of the accident was part of the film Fat Man and Little Boy, clip below: [VIDEO] A little more factual dramatization was presented in the TV show Dark Matters titled \"Risky Radiation\" but I am unable to locate a video clip atm. [LINK]"},{"id":36032997,"author":"ortusdux","month":"2023-05","story":"The Demon Core (2016)","stance":"reference","why":"Cusack's dramatization of the accident","quote":"I do like John Cusack's rendition","text":"I wonder if this event will be in the new Oppenheimer movie. I do like John Cusack's rendition: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15325370,"author":"chiph","month":"2017-09","story":"‘Criticality safety event’ occurred at LANL’s plutonium facility","stance":"reference","why":"Film dramatization of the 1945 criticality accident","quote":"Recreation of the criticality event in 1945 from the movie Fat Man and Little Boy , with John Cusack","text":"Recreation of the criticality event in 1945 from the movie Fat Man and Little Boy , with John Cusack [VIDEO]"}]},"DWpBNm6lBU4":{"wy":"HN commenters, including a Googler, cite it as the public explanation of how Google serves traffic, including DNS-based load balancing.","q":{"x":"the video by Simon Newton which describes the global footprint and some of the services","i":11145035,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11145035,"author":"chuhnk","month":"2016-02","story":"Linkerd: Twitter-Style Operability for Microservices","stance":"reference","why":"Describes Google's global footprint and some services.","quote":"the video by Simon Newton which describes the global footprint and some of the services","text":"I've only ever found two things publicly available on this. The patent related to DNS GSLB [LINK] and the video by Simon Newton which describes the global footprint and some of the services [VIDEO] . Most of what I know about GSLB is from my time working at Google."},{"id":9194744,"author":"asuffield","month":"2015-03","story":"What happens when you type Google.com into your browser's address box","stance":"reference","why":"Googler's pointer to public explanation of Google's serving infrastructure.","quote":"I can't tell you anything that's not in that video.","text":"Disclaimer: not representing anybody, my opinion not anybody else's. I work at Google, and my team looks after this. Here: [VIDEO] I can't tell you anything that's not in that video."},{"id":8676789,"author":"simonlbn","month":"2014-11","story":"My DNS queries are being intercepted and altered in-flight","stance":"reference","why":"Details on how Google uses DNS for load balancing.","quote":"If you want to see some details of how it works have a look at","text":"As others have mentioned this seems entirely expected based on how Google uses DNS for load balancing and serving your query from a datacenter as close to you as possible. If you want to see some details of how it works have a look at [VIDEO] . Disclaimer: I work for Google. I speak for myself etc. blah."}]},"EShUeudtaFg":{"wy":"Referenced as a legendary internet joke, usually when misspellings or Yahoo Answers come up.","m":[{"id":48595050,"author":"dlcarrier","month":"2026-06","story":"The First Website Killed by Google: Their Online Answer Marketplace","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It would be a great place to discuss, pergnancy, pregananancy, prgnency, pargnanciy, and so on: [VIDEO] Also, how baby is formed: [LINK]"},{"id":38438356,"author":"epiccoleman","month":"2023-11","story":"Show HN: Dalle-3 and GPT4-Vision feedback loop","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this video still makes me laugh years later","text":"The inability of AI images to spell has always amused me, and it's especially funny here. I got a special kick out \"IDEDA ENGINEEER\" and \"BUZSTEAND.\" The image where the one guy's hat just says \"HISPANIC\" is also oddly hilarious. Idk what it is, but I have a special soft spot for humor based around odd spelling (this video still makes me laugh years later: [VIDEO] )."},{"id":36673484,"author":"rvba","month":"2023-07","story":"Show HN: Throw – A space for asking and answering questions anonymously","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"How do you defend the service against answers copied from chatGPT? How do you defend against trolls? And how do you distinguish them from genuinely stupid people? [VIDEO] What do you do with all the teenagers (?) asking \"questions\" about sex?"},{"id":35502553,"author":"olidoc","month":"2023-04","story":"Am I Pregante?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"inspired by a legendary YouTube video","text":"Hello, Hacker News community! I recently created a small but enjoyable web game called 'Am I...?', inspired by a legendary YouTube video [VIDEO] Features: Randomly generated misspellings for endless fun A score counter that tracks your progress I developed this game using Flask, HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. It's been a great learning experience, and I hope you find it entertaining as well."},{"id":34223543,"author":"agentwiggles","month":"2023-01","story":"English as She Is Spoke","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"this old classic still makes me laugh","text":"I find that grammar perversions like this have a direct line to my funny bone in a way that almost nothing else does. Must have something to do with the subversion of expectation with something so incredibly basic as language. Even after seeing it many times, this old classic still makes me laugh: [VIDEO]"}]},"L75ESD9PBOw":{"wy":"HN points to it as proof that manned multicopters and eVTOL craft existed well before the current hype.","q":{"x":"Manned multicopters have already happened.","i":12370782,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":30110554,"author":"FabHK","month":"2022-01","story":"We have jetpacks and we do not care","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"eHang was not the first, but they do seem to have good marketing. German company Volocopter flew a manned demonstration rig in 2011 (the famous pilates ball [1]), and had the first manned flight of the Volocopter multicopter (certified, 18 rotors) in February 2016, with the CEO himself flying [2]. I don't know when the eHang 184 flew first (a video on youtube says Feb 2018 [3]). Personally, I find the design (with 8 rotors in a quadratic arrangement at knee-height, perfect for decapitating pedestrians) atrocious. (The Volocopter blades are above the cabin, as in a helicopter.) And where do you get the 30 minutes endurance for the eHang from? When I divide the range by the cruise speed from Wikipedia, I get about 8 minutes endurance. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":12710609,"author":"trhway","month":"2016-10","story":"Hyperloop One Raises $50M, Hires Former Uber CFO","stance":"reference","why":"Volocopter prototype as example of viable multicopter.","quote":"","text":">50+ years and $100m in R&D tell this to fusion guys :) >no viable prototypes i find this for example as looking very viable back at the time [LINK] and actually if you add more props (possible with electric today not so much with gas engines back then) and much-much better control electronics you'll get the modern multi-copters : volocopter prototype [VIDEO] and pre-production [LINK] >Maybe the approach was doomed from the start, but that didn't stop Moller from promising that you would be commuting to work by air in 10 years for decade after decade. only the last one, m400 was doomed as i mentioned above (and it wasn't actually that clear from the start, m400 was actually pretty logical and reasonable move forward - only after first tests one could see it clear). Put yourself at his place at any point in the history of his flying car development and those 10 years would look like a reasonable estimate. I personally actually say today that in 5 years we'll have the Volocopter likes flying around."},{"id":12370782,"author":"jakemoshenko","month":"2016-08","story":"Plasma wings could change the way airplanes are designed and flown","stance":"reference","why":"Example that manned multicopters already exist.","quote":"Manned multicopters have already happened.","text":"Manned multicopters have already happened. [1] MIT has also demonstrated a variable pitch quadcopter. [2] If they can use plasma laminar detachment and reattachment to somehow simulate a variable pitch propeller, while still getting some weight savings over a design with multiple swashplates, then yes I think it should help the quadcopter design scale up to larger propellers. That's a big if though, since to simulate a negative angle of attack I'm guessing your propeller design would have to be pretty inefficient with all plasma turned off. I'm not entirely sure why you want this though, since the single large rotor is more efficient. There was a novel design a few years ago with one large rotor for lift and three smaller ones to counter torque and provide control. [3] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"}]},"N6K9dS9wl7U":{"wy":"HN cites it as a record of Obama's 2008 stance, usually to make a point about how mainstream views on gay marriage shifted.","q":{"x":"I'm not somebody who promotes same-sex marriage, but I do believe in civil unions.","i":7475829,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":19925482,"author":"m0zg","month":"2019-05","story":"Trump Moves to Ban Foreign Telecom Gear, Targeting Huawei and China","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Obama opposed gay marriage years later than Cheney's stance","quote":"Obama was against gay marriage well after that, as well","text":"It's the other way around, you got it all wrong. Bush wanted to introduce a constitutional amendment banning gay marriage. Cheney was against this amendment. I'll tell you more, Obama was against gay marriage well after that, as well: [VIDEO] Specifically, written of Cheney in 2004: \"Vice President Dick Cheney, whose daughter Mary is a lesbian, distanced himself from President Bush’s call for a constitutional amendment to ban same-sex marriage “Lynne and I have a gay daughter, so it’s an issue our family is very familiar with,” Cheney told an audience that included his daughter. “With the respect to the question of relationships, my general view is freedom means freedom for everyone. People ought to be free to enter into any kind of relationship they want to. “The question that comes up with the issue of marriage is what kind of official sanction or approval is going to be granted by government? Historically, that’s been a relationship that has been handled by the states. The states have made that fundamental decision of what constitutes a marriage,” he said.\" Source: [LINK] Which is basically a position identical to Obama's position in 2008. Funny between two people holding the same views one is called a \"homophobe\", and another is a paragon of virtue."},{"id":7533513,"author":"cynwoody","month":"2014-04","story":"Corrosive Conformity","stance":"reference","why":"Obama's 2008 stated position on marriage and civil unions","quote":"Barack Obama opined on this topic around the same time as Brendan Eich","text":"For those who haven't seen it, Barack Obama opined on this topic around the same time as Brendan Eich, and over a much bigger megaphone than $1000 will buy: [VIDEO] tl;dv: \"I believe that marriage is the union between a man and woman ... For me as a Christian, it's also a sacred union .. you know, God's in the mix. ... I’m not somebody who promotes same-sex marriage, but I do believe in civil unions.”"},{"id":7542367,"author":"crusso","month":"2014-04","story":"The Hypocrisy Of Sam Yagan and OkCupid ","stance":"reference","why":"Obama's earlier stance favoring civil unions over marriage","quote":"","text":">> and would be completely fine with another civil contract with the same rights but a different name. >Because \"separate but equal\" had such a good run last time around eh? Do you take the same hardline stance with others who were opposed to gay marriage a few years ago and advocating civil unions? [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7475829,"author":"cynwoody","month":"2014-03","story":"Gay Developers Boycott As Mozilla Appoints Allegedly Anti-Gay CEO","stance":"reference","why":"Obama's 2008 position on civil unions versus marriage.","quote":"I'm not somebody who promotes same-sex marriage, but I do believe in civil unions.","text":"In 2008, when Prop 8 was on the ballot, Obama was for civil unions but professed to believe that marriage is a sacred union between a man and a woman. \"I'm not somebody who promotes same-sex marriage, but I do believe in civil unions.\" [VIDEO]"}]},"RnIvhlKT7SY":{"wy":"HN uses it as a go-to example of impressive 5-axis machining and CAM-controlled toolpaths.","q":{"x":"Each year they do some insanely elaborate piece of art in metal as a CNC machining demo.","i":9356511,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":33274658,"author":"pen2l","month":"2022-10","story":"Charges and arrests involving export violation schemes to aid Russian military","stance":"reference","why":"Modern CNC precision example","quote":"","text":"[LINK] specifically mentions a high-resolution jig. I wouldn't be surprised if in some cases just the tooling bits are around $5000, leaving aside the whole machine. For high-resolution machining, e.g. for lens fabrication, there's a whole science to drill bits, where special processes allow for achieving up to sub-nanometer surface roughness in parts. If they're making holes in hard metals they might be using titanium, diamond, or carbide drill bits that have crazy physical properties and cost a pretty penny. In some sense CNC machining is also a WWII era technology, but CNC machines from that era couldn't touch what modern-day machines can do and the repeatable precision with which they can do them: [VIDEO] While on the subject, this really is a fascinating field and it rules the world more than people might think. Consider that TSMC's core machines are made by ASML. Among the most important and specialized-IP parts in ASML machines are the huge lens which are made by Zeiss. Zeiss makes the lens with some next level machining equipment from companies around Heidelberg and Karlsruhe area. High-resolution machining isn't super known to the general public but it is so fascinating once you get to see the things they can pull off."},{"id":28056550,"author":"wlesieutre","month":"2021-08","story":"Where are the robotic bricklayers?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of complex part made simple by automation","quote":"As an example, not something you'd want to make on a hand operated milling machine","text":"There's also machining a piece of metal in an incredibly complex way, but with the right automation can be turned into a comparatively simple process. If you showed someone 100 years ago what we could do with a block of metal and a 5-axis CNC machine it would blow their mind. That's the type of area where automation can do great things. Look for places where \"simple for a machine\" and \"simple for a human\" diverge. I could do the job of every logic gate in my computer, but I wouldn't want to try and boot a copy of Windows with a pad of paper and a pencil. As an example, not something you'd want to make on a hand operated milling machine: [VIDEO] But to look at something that's easy to do for people and think \"That's easy, a machine could do it!\" It's always going to depend on why it's easy. Handling situations that differ from one job to the next is a pretty strong point for humans, especially when those differences are handled by grabbing and moving things. But then, five years ago we would've put \"playing Go\" on there with grabbing and moving things, so you never know."},{"id":15515152,"author":"LeifCarrotson","month":"2017-10","story":"Printers Meant to Make Rockets","stance":"reference","why":"Example of crazy internal 5-axis milling","quote":"You can do some pretty crazy internal milling with a 5-axis machine","text":"It's definitely possible to use a CNC machine to mill the shape of a rocket engine. You can do some pretty crazy internal milling with a 5-axis machine: [VIDEO] But the problem isn't the shape of the bell at all. It's that the wall of the bell is not solid, but hollow, filled with plumbing. [LINK] The fuel actually flows through the pipes and voids in the red-hot engine, where it absorbs the heat that would otherwise melt the engine. It then flows back up to be burned. You'd need an endoscope, not a robot arm, to mill that out! And neither an endoscope (obviously) nor a robot arm would likely be rigid enough to mill in these materials. Robot arms are typically only used when milling wood, plastic, or soft modeling materials. A CNC mill in metals like the exotic Nobium alloys used in these engines needs incredible rigidity which robot arms just don't have. Consider the hardened tool-steel shafts of the end mills used in the first video - and those are easily broken, while just cutting aluminum!"},{"id":9356511,"author":"Animats","month":"2015-04","story":"The Stanford 3D Scanning Repository","stance":"reference","why":"Annual elaborate metal art as CNC demo","quote":"Each year they do some insanely elaborate piece of art in metal as a CNC machining demo.","text":"What \"original\" means in the art world is amusing. It's all about monetization.[1] Cranking out a copy of Michaelangelo's David as a bronze would be a nice project. Hypermill/Daishin should take that on. Each year they do some insanely elaborate piece of art in metal as a CNC machining demo.[2] [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":9405026,"author":"Animats","month":"2015-04","story":"What doomed MakerBot? The Osborne effect","stance":"reference","why":"Hypermill controlling a 5-axis machine","quote":"Look at the videos of it controlling a 5-axis machine","text":"Carbide3D is still at \"preorder\". PocketNC has a very nice 5 axis machine for about $3K, but they were demoing two years ago and still aren't shipping. Roland has some nice machines starting around $5000, and those are real, shipping products. I'd really like to see PocketNC deliver. Milling software remains a problem. For 3-axis machines and simple work, there's the Cut2D/Vcarve/Aspire line, which is easy to use. Most work at TechShop uses that program. At the high end, there's Hypermill - $22,000 a seat, but you get your money's worth.[1] Look at the videos of it controlling a 5-axis machine, with long thin tools working deep through narrow openings, without screwing up. In the middle, there's SprutCam, from Russia, which is startup-grade software - clever, but buggy. (I've used it.) Since in the CNC business, bad software results in rejected parts and tool and machine damage, buyers are unwilling to buy bad software. There's some open source software for CAM, but it's below Cut2D, which is considered entry level. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8977617,"author":"Animats","month":"2015-01","story":"Computational Geometry in Python","stance":"reference","why":"5-axis machining simulation done well","quote":"Now that's a triumph of CSG.","text":"It's hardest for CSG systems that can't say no. If you use Autodesk Inventor, there are some geometries where the fillet generator just gives up and says the geometry is too complex to fillet. Because it's a user-driven program, it has that option. But a machining simulator does not. Those are programs which simulate what happens as a tool cuts through a part, starting with the geometry of the blank and subtracting every cut. For 2.5D machining (tool always approaches material from straight down), the simulator can use a height field, and that works fine. But the general 3D case, where the tool can come in from any direction, seems to be beyond the state of the art in CSG to get perfectly. I've had both InventorCam and SprutCam blow up. If you spend $22,000 for a copy of HyperCam, it seems to work much better. [VIDEO] Now that's a triumph of CSG."}]},"S1nc_chrNQk":{"wy":"Used as a neutral outsider's first-hand account of SpaceX's fast engineering culture and Musk's hands-on role.","q":{"x":"Sandy Munro was invited to SpaceX design review session, so he has some first-hand comments about that","i":26519955,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27848757,"author":"tokipin","month":"2021-07","story":"SpaceX will soon fire up its Super Heavy booster for the first time","stance":"reference","why":"Outsider's view of a Starship design review","quote":"Here's a video from someone who observed a design review for Starship[1].","text":"Here's a video from someone who observed a design review for Starship[1]. From the sound of it, Elon is in the thick of it in the R&D engineering. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27387745,"author":"simondotau","month":"2021-06","story":"“It's a truck full of traffic lights”","stance":"reference","why":"Neutral observer evidence of Musk's engineering involvement","quote":"was \"blown away\" at Elon's depth of involvement.","text":"Elon spends nearly as much time at SpaceX as he does Tesla. There's no question that Elon regularly mucks in at the lowest levels of engineering at SpaceX. If you want evidence from a reasonably neutral observer, take Sandy Munro (himself an engineer who has worked on everything from cars to aeroplanes). He recently interviewed Elon, ostensibly about Tesla but the interview was in a meeting room at SpaceX. After the interview he was invited to a two hour design review meeting and was \"blown away\" at Elon's depth of involvement. [VIDEO] (6:10 to 8:45)"},{"id":27388109,"author":"Geee","month":"2021-06","story":"“It's a truck full of traffic lights”","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Musk's day-to-day engineering role","quote":"He is very much involved at actual day-to-day engineering at SpaceX.","text":"He is very much involved at actual day-to-day engineering at SpaceX. He is the CEO and the chief engineer there. Sources: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27095756,"author":"simondotau","month":"2021-05","story":"Elon Musk, master promoter","stance":"reference","why":"Neutral observer evidence of Musk's engineering involvement","quote":"was \"blown away\" at Elon's depth of involvement.","text":"Elon spends nearly as much time at SpaceX as he does Tesla. There's no question that Elon mucks in at the lowest levels of engineering at SpaceX. If you want evidence from a reasonably neutral observer, take Sandy Munro (himself an engineer who has worked on everything from cars to aeroplanes). He recently interviewed Elon, ostensibly about Tesla but the interview was in a meeting room at SpaceX. After the interview he was invited to a two hour design review meeting and was \"blown away\" at Elon's depth of involvement. [VIDEO] (6:10 to 8:45)"},{"id":26519955,"author":"Geee","month":"2021-03","story":"Tesla's “full self driving” beta is potentially dangerous","stance":"reference","why":"Munro's first-hand comments on SpaceX design review","quote":"Sandy Munro was invited to SpaceX design review session, so he has some first-hand comments about that","text":"Sandy Munro was invited to SpaceX design review session, so he has some first-hand comments about that on this video (@6:17): [VIDEO]"},{"id":26520579,"author":"nickik","month":"2021-03","story":"Tesla's “full self driving” beta is potentially dangerous","stance":"reference","why":"Munro describing a SpaceX design review","quote":"Sandy Munro (former Chief Engineer at Ford and worked on lots of Aerospace projects) was invited to SpaceX design review session","text":"Sandy Munro (former Chief Engineer at Ford and worked on lots of Aerospace projects) was invited to SpaceX design review session (@6:17): [VIDEO]"},{"id":26221490,"author":"sitkack","month":"2021-02","story":"How I started believing in Cycle Time over Estimation","stance":"reference","why":"Illustrates spacex's continuous flow versus cautious pessimism","quote":"The picture they paint of spacex is one of being in continuous flow vs cautious pessimism.","text":"This aligned perfectly with the thread, [VIDEO] I went on a tour of Blue Origin, I learned next to nothing. It looked like a the rich kids house that had a coleco and a neo geo. The picture they paint of spacex is one of being in continuous flow vs cautious pessimism."}]},"_EUPwsD64GI":{"wy":"Used as an example of going the extra mile on small details in animation and design.","q":{"x":"This was such a small detail that it would have been forgivable if the animators had left it out entirely","i":5002262,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":32659317,"author":"pdpi","month":"2022-08","story":"Stable Diffusion animation","stance":"reference","why":"Roger Rabbit lamp scene as example of animation effort.","quote":"they casually bumped a pendant lamp and let it flail about.","text":"> It's pure insanity that people ever drew, by hand, mountains of individual drawings each slightly changed and assembled them into compelling illusions to tell stories. Traditional animation by itself is nothing short of insanity, convincingly blending live action and traditional animation takes it a step further, and then there's the \"Bumping the Lamp\"[0] scene in Who Framed Roger Rabbit. The film as a whole refuses to keep the camera static to make things easy for the animators, which was unusual enough by itself, but then they went above and beyond — they casually bumped a pendant lamp and let it flail about. Every time it slows down, it gets bumped again. And they shaded and cast shadows for the damned rabbit for every single frame of that sequence. Madness. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28763063,"author":"satvikpendem","month":"2021-10","story":"Stripe Press","stance":"reference","why":"Roger Rabbit lamp scene illustrating attention to detail.","quote":"This was such a small detail that it would have been forgivable if the animators had left it out entirely","text":"I am often pulled towards this comment for an article that asks, \"Are Designers Crazy?\" [0] \"\"\" No, designers aren't crazy. You just don't understand a very fundamental concept of design. It even applies to engineering. It's okay—many people have the same frustrations as you do. But those who care about the details achieve truly high quality results overall. It extends to all areas of the design, not just to the parts you can't see. In the movie \"Who Framed Roger Rabbit,\" there's a scene in a dark room where Roger Rabbit (an animated character) flies across the room, knocks a hanging lamp around, and the lighting becomes so dynamic that all the shadows move around including the animated character's shadow. Here's the scene in question: [VIDEO] This was such a small detail that it would have been forgivable if the animators had left it out entirely: if they had not moved the lamp, kept the shadow steady, no one would have really noticed the difference. It would have been 100 times easier to animate and the effect wouldn't really have been that different. But they did it anyway. The term was later coined, and \"bump the lamp\" is used throughout Disney (and probably other organizations) to mean something akin to \"go the extra mile\"—but I see it as having a special significance to design. You're right, most people won't notice. By that logic, you could cut corners a lot of other places too. You could be lax about button colors matching exactly, or per-pixel sharpness on the map an"},{"id":24535132,"author":"cercatrova","month":"2020-09","story":"Ask HN: Your Favourite HN Comment?","stance":"reference","why":"Roger Rabbit lamp scene illustrating attention to detail.","quote":"This was such a small detail that it would have been forgivable if the animators had left it out entirely","text":"This one about whether designers are crazy because the OP didn't understand why designers would care about minute details in their work. It is because attention to detail creates superlative work. It is similar to why premature optimization isn't necessarily bad for engineers. [LINK] \"\"\" No, designers aren't crazy. You just don't understand a very fundamental concept of design. It even applies to engineering. It's okay—many people have the same frustrations as you do. But those who care about the details achieve truly high quality results overall. It extends to all areas of the design, not just to the parts you can't see. In the movie \"Who Framed Roger Rabbit,\" there's a scene in a dark room where Roger Rabbit (an animated character) flies across the room, knocks a hanging lamp around, and the lighting becomes so dynamic that all the shadows move around including the animated character's shadow. Here's the scene in question: [VIDEO] This was such a small detail that it would have been forgivable if the animators had left it out entirely: if they had not moved the lamp, kept the shadow steady, no one would have really noticed the difference. It would have been 100 times easier to animate and the effect wouldn't really have been that different. But they did it anyway. The term was later coined, and \"bump the lamp\" is used throughout Disney (and probably other organizations) to mean something akin to \"go the extra mile\"—but I see it as having a special significance to design. Yo"},{"id":5002262,"author":"calinet6","month":"2013-01","story":"Are Designers Crazy?","stance":"reference","why":"Roger Rabbit lamp scene as example of going the extra mile.","quote":"This was such a small detail that it would have been forgivable if the animators had left it out entirely","text":"No, designers aren't crazy. You just don't understand a very fundamental concept of design. It even applies to engineering. It's okay—many people have the same frustrations as you do. But those who care about the details achieve truly high quality results overall. It extends to all areas of the design, not just to the parts you can't see. In the movie \"Who Framed Roger Rabbit,\" there's a scene in a dark room where Roger Rabbit (an animated character) flies across the room, knocks a hanging lamp around, and the lighting becomes so dynamic that all the shadows move around including the animated character's shadow. Here's the scene in question: [VIDEO] This was such a small detail that it would have been forgivable if the animators had left it out entirely: if they had not moved the lamp, kept the shadow steady, no one would have really noticed the difference. It would have been 100 times easier to animate and the effect wouldn't really have been that different. But they did it anyway. The term was later coined, and \"bump the lamp\" is used throughout Disney (and probably other organizations) to mean something akin to \"go the extra mile\"—but I see it as having a special significance to design. You're right, most people won't notice. By that logic, you could cut corners a lot of other places too. You could be lax about button colors matching exactly, or per-pixel sharpness on the map and buttons. No one would probably notice. But if you go for every detail like it was the most imp"}]},"_m97_kL4ox0":{"wy":"Referenced as an interesting early example of developing AI through evolution and artificial life simulation.","q":{"x":"Polyworld seemed like an interesting idea (developing AI through evolution)","i":7614219,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":20997998,"author":"brianpgordon","month":"2019-09","story":"Emergent Tool Use from Multi-Agent Interaction","stance":"reference","why":"Earlier artificial life simulator the commenter remembers being amazed by.","quote":"I remember being amazed a decade ago by the primitive graphics in artificial life simulators like Polyworld","text":"This is incredible. The various emergent behaviors are fascinating. I remember being amazed a decade ago by the primitive graphics in artificial life simulators like Polyworld: [LINK] [VIDEO] It seems that OpenAI has a great little game simulated for their agents to play in. The next step to make this even cooler would be to use physical, robotic agents learning to overcome challenges in real meatspace!"},{"id":7614219,"author":"tsenkov","month":"2014-04","story":"OpenWorm: A Digital Organism In Your Browser","stance":"reference","why":"Polyworld as an interesting idea of developing AI through evolution.","quote":"Polyworld seemed like an interesting idea (developing AI through evolution)","text":"As much as I understood, this project isn't so much about AI, as it is about a better understanding of biology. I don't know if the project still lives, or are there other many such works, but Polyworld seemed like an interesting idea (developing AI through evolution): [VIDEO]"},{"id":886909,"author":"sp332","month":"2009-10","story":"Evolved virtual creatures (evolutionary programming, 1994)","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk on evolving artificial intelligence.","quote":"Something like this Google Tech Talk","text":"Something like this Google Tech Talk, \"Polyworld: Using Evolution to Design Artificial Intelligence\"? [VIDEO]"}]},"azEvfD4C6ow":{"wy":"HN mostly drops it as a joke whenever giant machines or huge ships and excavators come up.","m":[{"id":45588380,"author":"fragmede","month":"2025-10","story":"New England's last coal plant has stopped operating, according to its owners","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as a sight of a huge excavator; the commenter adds facts.","quote":"You should see this one!","text":"You should see this one! [VIDEO] It's a bit dated (completed in 1978) and cost approximately $100 million. At one point it was the heaviest land vehicle, clocking in at 13,500 tons. It's since been succeded, but this bucket-wheel excavator needed only five people to operate. Those five people could mine 240,000 tons of coal[8] or 240,000 cubic metres of overburden (rock/soil on top of the coal) per day . That's 2400 coal wagons! After totally tapping out all of the coal at the Tagebau Hambach mine, in 2001, it took a crew of 70 together to move it 14 miles to the next mine. This move cost 15 million German Marks. (Just watch the linked documentary.)"},{"id":29293468,"author":"dan-robertson","month":"2021-11","story":"On the Impossibility of Supersized Machines (2017)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I’ve heard ideas[1] about supersized machines and they terrify me.","text":"I’ve heard ideas[1] about supersized machines and they terrify me. Thankfully it is probably NP-hard in size to make a thing so we’re probably fine. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26586271,"author":"Arrath","month":"2021-03","story":"Is that ship still stuck?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Obligatory","text":"Just bring in a Bagger 288[1] and use it to excavate a new diversion canal in front of the ship long enough to get it out of the main canal, bada-bing bada-boom. Simple! [1]Obligatory: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25609132,"author":"Defenestresque","month":"2021-01","story":"EU Signs €145B Declaration to Develop Next Gen Processors and 2nm Technology","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The leaders of the free world sat around a secret table...","text":"The leaders of the free world sat around a secret table... [VIDEO] (I know this comment is not exactly in the mood of HN, but this is the one thing I never fail to think of when someone mentions the Bagger family of murderous machines!)"}]},"iaSUOFleNRU":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference in threads about cheating, fraud and exam proctoring.","m":[{"id":48128453,"author":"dylan604","month":"2026-05","story":"Princeton mandates proctoring in-person exams, upending 133 years of precedent","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Now students have ChatGPT to write their papers and they've been practicing how to use cell phones without the teacher noticing for 10 years before getting to college. Colleges will need to remodel the rooms where these tests are given to become large SCIF type rooms so that wireless communication is not possible. Let the students go back to writing on their arms, wearing eye patches, or shoving notes up their casts. Yeah, I've probably seen Spies Like Us a couple of times: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32671683,"author":"assttoasstmgr","month":"2022-09","story":"Ask HN: Anyone having issues with job applicant fraud?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's nearly 40 years old and it still makes me laugh every time.","text":"Or the over-the-top cheating scene with Chevy Chase from \"Spies Like Us\" [VIDEO] It's nearly 40 years old and it still makes me laugh every time."},{"id":29432262,"author":"LinuxBender","month":"2021-12","story":"Man tries to dodge Covid vaccine wearing fake arm","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"paid hundreds of euros for the silicone prosthetic And probably a lot more in fines. Surely finding a less than ethical nurse or technician to bribe would be easier and possibly less risk since they can just decline before you give your name. How inebriated would a nurse have to be to think a prosthetic arm is real? I suppose if one were to try this, at least give it the old college try and put that arm in a sling and cast so you can't be expected to make a fist or move your arm. [1] \"Inject my broken arm since I can't use it anyway\". Remember to warm it up. [1] - [VIDEO] [video]"}]},"lOTyUfOHgas":{"wy":"Invoked as a joke reference when someone seems to give up or ask for help without trying anything.","m":[{"id":31054119,"author":"ncr100","month":"2022-04","story":"Tesla must pay ex-worker $15M for “disturbing” racist abuse, judge rules","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"It seems like an illogical position to me. Doing the correct thing, to solve a problem, involves getting buy in from the stakeholders. E.g. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25585853,"author":"ryandrake","month":"2020-12","story":"The Cruise Ship Suicides","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's like the Simpsons quote","text":"> The lockdowns simply do not work. We don't really know this because almost nowhere is actually locking anything down. We have these feeble and unenforced \"stay at home\" orders which are full of loopholes and largely ignored. If you go out for a drive in your town today (against stay-at-home guidance), you'll see all manner of stores open, people out and about horsing around, even restaurants. We're hardly taking any precautions at all. We don't know if lockdowns work because we haven't actually tried it. It's like the Simpsons quote, \"We've tried nothing, and we're all out of ideas!\" [1] A few places on earth like NZ and Australia actually did stay-at-home with far fewer loopholes, mandatory quarantining, contact tracing, and they're doing much better. 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20848708,"author":"midev","month":"2019-08","story":"YouTube Said to Be Fined Up to $200M for Children’s Privacy Violations","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"One should come before the other. You should parent before calling for regulations. > The comment about moms blocking YouTube is a bit ridiculous. Seems hard to believe likelihood of finding one could be higher than the likelihood of finding a kid who can work around it. It is not hard to block domains at the router, and restrict the devices your child has access to. Of course they might use a friends, or find a way around it. But the OP is literally talking about overhearing the video on the couch and not doing anything about it. That's why I asked what they tried... [VIDEO]"}]},"n0CT8zrw6lw":{"wy":"Cited as a shorthand for a spectacularly misguided publicity stunt, or as a classic embarrassing Cleveland episode.","m":[{"id":29720462,"author":"vgeek","month":"2021-12","story":"Midwest Developer","stance":"reference","why":"Balloonfest as a classic embarrassing Cleveland episode","quote":"The two best things denigrating Cleveland: Balloonfest","text":"The two best things denigrating Cleveland: Balloonfest ( [VIDEO] ) & your already mentioned Mike Polk Jr's tourism videos ( [VIDEO] & [LINK] ). If you can get past the dreary gray skies for 8 months of the year, Cleveland is cheap (even the nicer suburbs like Solon or Aurora) and has easy access to scenic western PA & upstate NY."},{"id":28188477,"author":"Borrible","month":"2021-08","story":"As cargo shipments boom, ship strikes imperil whales worldwide","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I guess the airspace will look somewhat like the Cleveland Balloonfest '86...","text":"You will need a lot of pricey Helium-filled airships to replace an average container ship. I guess the airspace will look somewhat like the Cleveland Balloonfest '86... [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":18783159,"author":"unixhero","month":"2018-12","story":"How to dump 3000 pounds of confetti on Times Square","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Pretty much as dumb as the Cleveland Balloon event[0].","text":"Welp. Pretty much as dumb as the Cleveland Balloon event[0]. I think it is funny as hell, but incredibly dumb, yes. \"Cleveland, WE DID IT! YEEEAH\". [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"o8NPllzkFhE":{"wy":"HN cites it for Linus's remarks on code taste, his self-description as an engineer rather than a visionary, and the growth of Linux.","q":{"x":"I think he puts it pretty well","i":21839356,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":46500562,"author":"chistev","month":"2026-01","story":"CSS sucks because we don't bother learning it (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Source of Linus quote on being unable to do UI.","quote":"","text":"\"There are people who care about the UI. I can’t do UI to save my life. I mean, if I was stranded on an island and the only way to get off that island was to make a pretty UI, I’d die there.\" - Linus Torvalds. Source of the quote above - [VIDEO] If you don't want to watch the whole video, here's the exact part about the UI - [LINK] Transcript - [LINK]"},{"id":46825872,"author":"Imustaskforhelp","month":"2026-01","story":"Code is cheap. Show me the talk","stance":"reference","why":"Linus on Linux's growth and why he open sourced it.","quote":"","text":"I don't think Linus is a people person. This is something which he talks about himself in the famous ted-ed video. I just re-watched the video (currently halfway) & I feel like the point of Linux is something which you are forgetting but it was never intended to grow so much and Linux himself in the video when asked says that he never had a moment where he went like oh this went big. In fact he talks about when the project was little. On how he had gratitude when the project had 10 people maybe 100 people working on it and then things only grow over a very large time frame (more than 25-30years? maybe now 35 just searched 34) He talks about how he got other people's idea which he couldn't have thought of things themselves and when he first created the project he just wanted to show off to the world to look at what I did (and he did it mainly for both the end result of the project and programming itself too) and then he got introduced to open source (free software) by his friend and he just decided to have it open source. My point is it was neither the code nor the talk. Linus is the best person to maintain Linux, why? Because he has been passionate over it for 25 years. I feel like Linux would be just as interested in talking about the code and any improvements now with maybe the same vigour as 34 years ago. He loves his creation & we love Linux too :) Another small point I wish to add is that if talk was the only thing, then you are missing the point because Linux was create"},{"id":21839356,"author":"delusional","month":"2019-12","story":"Modular C: Reusable and Maintainable Code Using the C Language (1994) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Linus explains why double-indirect iteration is better.","quote":"I think he puts it pretty well","text":"It's sort of funny that linus torvalds actually talked about why that sort of iteration is better: [VIDEO] I think he puts it pretty well (although he them also goes on to say dumb little snippets like that really don't matter in the big picture). Essentially, by making a doubly indirect pointer you get to treat the first element exactly the same way you are going to treat every other element."},{"id":18995436,"author":"clusmore","month":"2019-01","story":"Special Cases Are a Code Smell","stance":"reference","why":"Linus on code taste with indirect pointer list removal.","quote":"","text":"I'm reminded of Linus Torvald's interview at Ted [1] where he talks about programming \"taste\", and compares the following two code snippets: remove_list_entry(entry) { prev = NULL; walk = head; while (walk != entry) { prev = walk; walk = walk->next; } if (!prev) head = entry->next; else prev->next = entry->next; } remove_list_entry(entry) { indirect = &head; while ((*indirect) != entry) indirect = &(*indirect)->next; *indirect = entry->next; } [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11648234,"author":"j1vms","month":"2016-05","story":"SpaceX lands rocket at sea second time after satellite launch","stance":"reference","why":"Linus describes himself as more of an Edison.","quote":"","text":"Tesla is really great, but there exists a \"take\" on Edison that makes him also not so bad. Here, listen to Linus talk about him a bit: [VIDEO] Linus: \"I'm more of an Edison...\" (TED2016, Feb. 2016)"}]},"rthHSISkM7A":{"wy":"Not recommended; commenters use it as a joke reference in threads about military lasers and similar technology.","m":[{"id":37665669,"author":"karmakaze","month":"2023-09","story":"Britain to build world’s most powerful laser","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"popping popcorn[0]","text":"Good to see it's for a nuclear fusion energy source, rather than say a 'star wars' weapon or popping popcorn[0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23123602,"author":"hnhg","month":"2020-05","story":"X-37b space plane’s microwave power beam experiment","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"that was the ending of Real Genius","text":"I don't know if you're referencing it but that was the ending of Real Genius: [VIDEO] [edit: possibly a spoiler?]"},{"id":19849247,"author":"mr_overalls","month":"2019-05","story":"U.S. Air Force has shot down multiple air-launched missiles in a test","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"are you made of popcorn?","text":"This scenario has been carefully explored since the mid-1980s. For clarification, are you made of popcorn? [VIDEO]"},{"id":9613440,"author":"baldfat","month":"2015-05","story":"Military technology: Laser weapons get real","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"A 1980s movie about technology, military and academia.","text":"Here the link I am referring to before I get 4 down votes. This \"joke\" actually is referring to something special. A 1980s movie about technology, military and academia. [VIDEO]"}]},"ryFB6mvy4uE":{"wy":"HN mostly cites it for specific anecdotes, such as Thiel's early Facebook investment and how he thinks about the future, not as a general recommendation.","m":[{"id":16079521,"author":"Roybot","month":"2018-01","story":"Why things might have taken so long","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel interview on thinking about the future","quote":"It reminded me a little bit about an interview with peter thiel where he responded to a question about the future.","text":"> People always assume that the future will simply be like present only more so. Thanks for your thought. It reminded me a little bit about an interview with peter thiel where he responded to a question about the future. I guess popular culture in tech moves past ideas rather quickly and generally seems to jump from trend to trend until realizing the value of a trend. [VIDEO]"},{"id":11448012,"author":"w1ntermute","month":"2016-04","story":"Losing Money","stance":"reference","why":"Thiel on his early Facebook investment","quote":"Peter Thiel said recently in an interview[0] that when he made his initial $500K investment in Facebook","text":"Peter Thiel said recently in an interview[0] that when he made his initial $500K investment in Facebook at a $5M valuation in September 2004, he thought it would be big on college campuses, but didn't anticipate how successful it would actually become. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10313534,"author":"transpose","month":"2015-10","story":"Transpose: A structured alternative to Evernote","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Hi everyone, I'm Hussein, the CEO of Transpose. Thanks for the feedback, positive and negative. The idea behind Transpose is the lack of options between something as simple and ubiquitous as note taking apps like Evernote and fully fledged database solutions like FileMaker and Access. What we found out from our research is the a lot of use cases and critical applications should have been built on a database if it was feasible, affordable, cloud based and anyone can build without the need of hiring a software house or even dedicating an enterprise IT department to build something from scratch. People have been tracking orders, leads, assets and even critical project planning on various apps including even Evernote which is not designed for those use cases. With Transpose we are also tackling the growing epidemic of having a dozen of different apps all stitched together somehow duplicating the data across and demanding the team to learn and use each one of them just to visualize information differently; like Trello on boards, spreadsheets in table form, notes just for ease and cross platform functionality. We shooting high aiming to disrupt and fix that as well. If the data is structured and we extract all this extra metadata on top of that automatically so why not utilize your data and get as much value out of it? Here is a quick intro video on Transpose [LINK] We have all sort of amazing technology working seamlessly behind the scene. Text Analytics, Entity Extraction, Voice "}]},"sgspkxfkS4k":{"wy":"Shared as a joke skit that HN says captures the absurdity of real-world defence and geopolitical situations.","m":[{"id":48579272,"author":"florkbork","month":"2026-06","story":"US holds off blacklisting China's DeepSeek, +100 firms deemed security risks","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"basically explains the situation.","text":"Re Australia vs China [VIDEO] basically explains the situation. While having data/code stolen isn't ideal, there is a certain point where you need to assume it's already out there. There's actually more probability of harm from shady US companies imo, because people are less suspicious about data sovereignty"},{"id":46474335,"author":"schappim","month":"2026-01","story":"Explosions reported in Venezuelan capital Caracas","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Comedy is becoming reality","text":"Australia is spending close to $30 billion a year to protect our trade routes to China from China. Comedy is becoming reality: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45875350,"author":"rswail","month":"2025-11","story":"How the UK lost its shipbuilding industry","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"On the other hand, the primary strategic threat is apparently also China.","text":"Australia's naval strategy is to defend our sea lanes that we rely on for trade, primarily our trade with our largest customer, which is China. Conveniently, they need what we have, and we want what they make. On the other hand, the primary strategic threat is apparently also China. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36609972,"author":"bobzibub","month":"2023-07","story":"Video: Iranian Navy Warship Fires on Oil Tanker in the Strait of Hormuz","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Enjoy a comedy skit on that subject","text":"What the article didn't mention was that the US has pirated (ahem) \"commandeered\" Iranian oil tankers as well. This is an ongoing dispute between these two countries. Iran violates US sanctions, but technically they are merely US sanctions which have the legal weight of say Togo's sanctions on the high seas. (Togo doesn't enforce their domestic law on the high seas, nor can they.) The simplest way to get Iran to stop compandeering tankers is for the US to do the same. They don't want our \"peace\", it is true. \"Peace with Piracy\" is just not compelling to the Iranians. Can you blame them? Likewise, China benefits from trade near her borders but the US and allies want the ability to turn off the spigot when they wish to force China into concessions, as has been noted in US geopolitical articles for decades now. Note the Australian nuclear submarines deal: They don't really want to protect their trade with China...from China, they want the ability to control trade between China and her allies. These subs would do part of the job, with the bulk of it done by the USN. Enjoy a comedy skit on that subject: [VIDEO]"}]},"twhvtzd6gXA":{"wy":"Mostly cited as a recognizable example of antidepressant advertising in debates about pharmaceutical marketing and the serotonin theory.","m":[{"id":20076372,"author":"doitLP","month":"2019-06","story":"Evidence of a nocebo response following a nationwide antidepressant drug switch","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"probably the most the most recognizable anti-depressant ad ever","text":"It’s pervasive, especially in the early days of marketing antidepressants to the general public. Example, probably the most the most recognizable anti-depressant ad ever (at least in the US): [VIDEO] @ 0:20"},{"id":43209967,"author":"42772827","month":"2025-02","story":"The serotonin theory of depression: a systematic review of the evidence (2022)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"> People who having passing familiarity with neuroscience often assume that psychiatric medications work by correcting deficiencies, but this isn't true. They think this because that’s literally what the commercial for Zoloft said. [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32307907,"author":"coding123","month":"2022-08","story":"TikTok videos leave teens thinking they have rare mental disorders (2021)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"The drug industry does the same thing. Has anyone else watched a anti-depression medicine commercial and suddenly felt they are depressed? [VIDEO] I mean watch this and tell me you don't want what they're selling."},{"id":22020903,"author":"banads","month":"2020-01","story":"Medications that change who we are","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":">Citation required [VIDEO] >\"Depression may be related to an imbalance of natural chemicals between nerve cells in the brain. Prescription Zoloft works to correct this imbalance.\""}]},"uClWlVCwHsI":{"wy":"Used mostly as evidence of FSD Beta's limitations, for example pulling out in front of fast traffic.","m":[{"id":26519726,"author":"GhostVII","month":"2021-03","story":"Tesla's “full self driving” beta is potentially dangerous","stance":"reference","why":"Visualization showing cars colored yellow at 4:00.","quote":"at 4:00 the cars are all yellow when they shouldn't be","text":"In the video I actually do see the yellow outline if I really turn up the resolution, it's super faint though, hard to tell if it is there the whole time. Easier to see in [VIDEO] , at 4:00 the cars are all yellow when they shouldn't be."},{"id":26556061,"author":"Shivetya","month":"2021-03","story":"DARPA now has AI-controlled F-16s working as a team in virtual dogfights","stance":"reference","why":"Beta software making left turns.","quote":"This is not my video","text":"FWIW, the upcoming software which has about two thousand beta testers is making left turns. [VIDEO] This is not my video"},{"id":30049025,"author":"isx726552","month":"2022-01","story":"IBM’s Watson Health is sold off in parts","stance":"critical","why":"Example of FSD's shortcomings against safety claims.","quote":"Tesla’s safety claims are wildly overstated","text":"What videos would those be, like this one? [VIDEO] Tesla’s safety claims are wildly overstated, as documented here: [LINK] In aggregate, Tesla’s FSD is demonstrably not up to the task. “Limited driver assist” is a much more fair assessment of what their software is actually capable of than the “full self driving” branding."},{"id":27307479,"author":"throwaway77112","month":"2021-05","story":"Tesla loses U.S. designation for some advanced safety features","stance":"critical","why":"Listed among Tesla fail videos.","quote":"Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021","text":"Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021: [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] Other things: [1] [LINK] [2] a video which was taken down from youtube [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] . Bonus material: [1] [LINK] Edit: For saner info regarding self-driving, A.I etc: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] book to read, suitable even for laypeople >> 'Artificial Intelligence: A Guide for Thinking Humans by Melanie Mitchell IMO everyone should read that book Edit 2: Children of the Magenta Line [LINK]"},{"id":27326624,"author":"throwaway24006","month":"2021-05","story":"Camera-Only Autopilot Requires Automatic High Beams Enabled","stance":"critical","why":"Listed among Tesla fail videos.","quote":"Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021","text":"Copy-pasting a glorious Tesla comment from yesterday: Videos of Tesla fails, mostly from 2021: [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] [5] [LINK] Other things: [1] [LINK] [2] a video which was taken down from youtube [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK] . Bonus material: [1] [LINK] For saner info regarding self-driving, A.I etc: [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] book to read, suitable even for laypeople >> 'Artificial Intelligence: A Guide for Thinking Humans by Melanie Mitchell Perils of autopilot: Children of the Magenta Line [LINK]"},{"id":26691204,"author":"mlyle","month":"2021-04","story":"Waymo ordered “up to” 82,000 vehicles in 2018. Today, it has “well over 600.”","stance":"critical","why":"Contrasts Tesla's unprotected left turn with Waymo.","quote":"Tesla would kill you half the time making this unprotected left turn.","text":"Watch this entire video: [VIDEO] Tesla would kill you half the time making this unprotected left turn. Waymo can make unprotected left turns and have never killed or injured anyone or even bent metal in this situation. :P It's not a particularly challenging left turn. And the goal is to kill you less than .0001% of the time making left turns. Yes, Waymo tries to avoid them in route planning, but it still takes them if necessary."},{"id":26691421,"author":"mlyle","month":"2021-04","story":"Waymo ordered “up to” 82,000 vehicles in 2018. Today, it has “well over 600.”","stance":"critical","why":"Makes limits of Tesla self-driving clear.","quote":"makes the present limits rather clear","text":"It's a win. But-- if it's just driving freeway miles and lanekeeping and doing basic collision avoidance-- there's a lot of cars doing that and Tesla is in first place but not strongly differentiated from GM, etc. Other cars can provide you this \"less fatiguing road trip\" experience. If you want it to actually drive and be able to remove your attention, well, something like this [VIDEO] makes the present limits rather clear."},{"id":26519431,"author":"clouddrover","month":"2021-03","story":"Tesla's “full self driving” beta is potentially dangerous","stance":"critical","why":"FSD shown as dangerous and not road-ready.","quote":"Full Self Driving\" looks dangerous and probably shouldn't be on the road","text":"Well, here's a different video. \"Full Self Driving\" looks dangerous and probably shouldn't be on the road: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26519495,"author":"GhostVII","month":"2021-03","story":"Tesla's “full self driving” beta is potentially dangerous","stance":"critical","why":"FSD repeatedly pulls out in front of fast cars.","quote":"It repeatedly tries to pull out in front of cars going 45+ miles an hour","text":"This [0] is much worse than the video in the article, IMO. It repeatedly tries to pull out in front of cars going 45+ miles an hour, in some cases giving the driver very little time to stop it. For level 5 (what Elon keeps claiming they will have very soon), they need to be able to go tens of thousands of miles without a crash-avoiding human intervention, not 10 minutes. [0] [VIDEO]"}]},"uo9nGzIzSPw":{"wy":"Shared as a curiosity showing that with enough power almost anything can demodulate AM radio.","q":{"x":"With enough power, literally anything can become an AM radio.","i":30859681,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":37710177,"author":"jjoonathan","month":"2023-09","story":"A scientific curiosity that happened to me","stance":"reference","why":"Nonlinear conversion demodulates AM.","quote":"once it's AM just about any energy conversion will do.","text":"Slope conversion is the hard part, once it's AM just about any energy conversion will do. abs(x) and |x|^2 are plenty nonlinear. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37024821,"author":"blincoln","month":"2023-08","story":"Radio frequency hearing in humans and its effects (2003)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of hearing AM radio from structures.","quote":"Was the sound you heard like either of these?","text":"I grew up in a house that was next door to a radio rebroadcasting facility, and we could definitely hear the radio station coming from the walls of our house sometimes, e.g. even their station identification so there was no ambiguity. I assumed it was the house wiring (mostly knob and tube) acting as an antenna. I'm reasonably sure that was AM, though. Was the sound you heard like either of these? [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30875643,"author":"ngcc_hk","month":"2022-04","story":"How is portable AM radio possible?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] [LINK] Do not understand the explanation. Just put a space will get the hn act. And copy is just right click or touch-hold. Am I missing something ?"},{"id":30859681,"author":"1970-01-01","month":"2022-03","story":"How is portable AM radio possible?","stance":"reference","why":"With enough power anything demodulates AM.","quote":"With enough power, literally anything can become an AM radio.","text":"Fun link time: With enough power, literally anything can become an AM radio. No tuning or filtering is required. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":23384462,"author":"PoachedSausage","month":"2020-06","story":"40 uses for Germanium Diodes (1950) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates arcing near a high-power AM tower.","quote":"It could actually have been arcing","text":"I'm not entirely sure what process is converting to sound, but the metal fence makes a great antenna and we are talking about 10's of feet from the antenna with 500 kW AM at 200 kHz going into it. It could actually have been arcing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23459156,"author":"neurostimulant","month":"2020-06","story":"Toto’s “Africa” Played on Tesla Coils","stance":"reference","why":"AM audio heard by shorting the tower.","quote":"Also you can hear AM radio transmission if you short the tower","text":"Also you can hear AM radio transmission if you short the tower: [VIDEO] Electricity is both awesome and terrifying"},{"id":22292448,"author":"cranium","month":"2020-02","story":"Ask HN: What’s the most important modern simple invention?","stance":"reference","why":"AM radio demodulated by an arc at a transmitter tower.","quote":"The AM (Amplitude Modulation) transmission is even quite simple to understand and build.","text":"I'm surprised nobody mentioned radio communication. The AM (Amplitude Modulation) transmission is even quite simple to understand and build. You can even create an electric arc between the ground and the transmitting tower as a cheap (and dangerous) receiver: [VIDEO]"}]}});
